      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WHAT EVERY WOMAN WANTS.</head>
        <p>WHAT EVERY WOMAN WANTS.</p>
        <p>HELP IN THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The arduous duties of the household, combined with the anxious care of the little ones, render the task of the wife of the worker a peculiarly trying one. She is often on the verge of a breakdown. She needs help The best help a woman can have is a cup of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa. It is really invaluable as a "pick- me-up." Most women take tea when they require a refresher. Vi-Cocca is far and away superior to tea. Tea is a stimulant and a stimulant only. Vi-Cocoa is a nutrient—a nourisher as well as a refresher. It is lastingly beneficial in its effects. Moreover, it is tastier than tea--more pleasing to the palate. Some people doubt this at first, but when they once try it they always buy it. Of all beverages in use Vi-Cocoa is the best by test. Give Vi-Cocoa a trial. Never mind what you've been drinking at breakfast or supper in the past, start thinking Vi-Cocoa to-day--you'll feel the benefit of it at once.</p>
        <p>„..„. Do not ask your grocer for” Cocoa "—ask for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Weoexa</head>
        <p>Weoexa</p>
        <p>—lt makes all the difference. Every grocer sells Vi-Cccoa in 6d. packets and 9d. and is. 6d. tins. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDING.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Percival—Hughes.</p>
        <p>A wedding of considerable interest to Warring- tonians was solemnized at St. Mary's Church, Denbigh, on Thursday of last week, when Mr. Arthur Percival, of London (eldest son of the Rev. B. and Mrs. Percival, of Warrington) was married to Miss Gwendoline Hughes (eldest daughter of Mr. J. Bulkeley and Mrs. Hughes, Vale-street, Denbigh.) The Rev. B. Percival officiated and was assisted by the Rev. T. Redfern, rector of Denbigh and rural dean. The service was choral. The bride, who was given away by her father, was dressed in her travelling costume of lavender cloth, a large black hat with ostrich feathers, and long scarf of old family Limerick lace. She was attended by her sister, Miss Alen. Hughes, who wore a blue velvet dress trimmed with lace, ard bat to match ; both carried bouquets of floweis with white heather, given by the bridegroom. Mr. Barwise Percival undertook the duties of best man to his brother. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>-- ----- After the ceremony the breakfast was served at the Bull Hotel. Later on the happy couple left for the South, amidst the cDngratulations and good wishes of their relatives. The following is a list of the principal presents : Mr J Bulkeley Hughes, cheque, suite of furniture, sideboard and oak bureau Mrs Bulkeley Hughes, tea service and cutlery Mr Howard Hughes, cheque Mr Cyril Hughes, steel engraving in oak frame Miss A Hughes, silver butter knife, sugir tongs and jam spoon Rev B Percival, cheque Mrs Percival, household lin:n Mr B Percival, silver cruet J H Percival, cheque Misses M and L Percival, dinner service Miss D Percival, two silver-mounted silt cellars Mr Bernard Percival, brass kettle and stand Miss Hughes (Bangor), cheque Miss Ursula Hughes, cheque Mrs Rost (Oxford), case of flih knives and forks Mr A Rost, brass candlesticks and artistic figures Rev C H Hughes, case of silver-mounted carvers Mr U H Hughes, silver-mounted jam jar Mr Brown (London), silver inkstand Miss Smith (Edinburgh), sideboard cloth Mrs Bamforth (Warrington), silver 113wer vases Miss Jones (London), oak letter rack Mr Pike (Penmaemnawr), silver sugar basin and cream jug Mrs Blake (Dumfries), eiderdown quilt Mrs Hutton (Dumfries), tea service Mrs Dixon (Annan), silver cake basket Mr and Mrs R Davies (Denbigh), handsome carriage clock Miss Carratben (Denbigh), band-painted work-bag Miss Parry (Denbigh), gilded photo frame Mr and Mrs Hughes, antique brush and crumb tray Miss Bowdage (Denbigh), flower vase Mrs Buller (Denbigh), silver sugar tongs Mrs McCubbing. silver toast rack Messrs H and W Smith (London), complete set of Rodgers's cutlery Mrs Graves (London), bed quilt Mr and Mrs Young (London), two cut-glass fruit dishes Mrs Lifetree (London), oilpainting Miss Lewis (London), two flower pots Miss Pierce (London), pair of ornaments Miss George (London), dining-room clock Mrs Morrison, brass crumb tray and bruih Mrs Morgan, cheque Mr Lloyd Jones. tapestry cushion Rev Wand Alrsilracecamp,tvvo water-colour paintings Miss E Adams (Denbigh), afternoon teacloth Mr Hughes (London), silver-mounted oak clothes brush Mr and Mrs Steel (Warrington), silver butter dish Mr B Madeley (Warrington), silver sugar spray Mr Dinin (London) clock The maid, the Vicarage (Warrington), damask table- cloth Miss Horton. drawn threedwork afternoon teacloth Mrs Lewis (Danbigh), marmalade jar Miss G Buller (Denbigh), oil painting in frame Miss Helsby (Denbigh), picture Mr F Dunn (Dublin), silver servietts rings Mr H Cle.2g (London), silver biscuit barrel Mrs Cattermole, sold snake bracelet set with emeralds Miss Roberts, china rose bowl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CAUGHT GOLD AT THE THEATRE.</head>
        <p>CAUGHT GOLD AT THE THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Bronchitis Followed and Only Peps Could Stop it,</p>
        <p>Many a cold is caught while standing in the queue waiting for the theatre doors to open, and thus while seeking pleasure, the foundation of serious chest trouble can be easily laid. It was so in the case of Mrs. R. Ryan, of SO, Stowe-road, Shepherd's Bush, London, W., who told the following graphic story to a London reporter : " I caught cold three years last Christmas," said Mrs. R. Ryan, "through standing for an hour and a half in a biting east wind waiting for the theatre to open. " My chest got very sore and painful, and a cough developed that fairly shook me. There was phlegm vt the bark of my throat, and a tightness across my chest which no amount cf coughing could relieve. At night I couldn't sleep, for the moment I put my head on the pillow the cough began. Often my bead felt as though it would burst, yet I couldn't stop coughing and gasping for breath. The pressure round my heart was stifling and quite frightened me. " After giving a doctor's medicines a long trial without any good result, I went to the West London Hospital. I attended there tegularly for months, but got no better. Then I was laid up for three months, trying all kinds of things. My husband reckons that I used over a hundredweight of bran in poultices, but its only effect was to make my skin very sore. As an experiment I started taking Peps a short time ago, after I'd heard them well spoken of. To my great joy, Peps gave me relief the first night I started with them.</p>
        <p>"The Peps fumes seemed to go right down into my chest and lungs, easing the tightness and soothing my rad. throat. The change from ceaseless toture to comfort sad restful sleep which Peps brought was really astonishing. By taking these wonderful Peps tegularly, the phlegm was broken and came away easily. The cough disappeared too, and with the return of comfortable and regular breathing, I con- sidered myself quite cured." The moral of Mrs. Ryan's experience is " always keep Peps bandy " to keep coughs and colds at bay.</p>
        <p>NO BARKING ALLOWED. NEW BLACKPOOL BY-LAW WHICH EVERY</p>
        <p>DOG MUST KNOW.</p>
        <p>"Let no dog bark" is the substance of an order issued on Tuesday by the Blackpool Town Council, when a new tby-law prohibiting dogs barking on the foreshore was passed:</p>
        <p>Alderman Tdm Bickerstaffe said no such absurd thing bad been considered by any other resort. Councillor Caills ridiculed the proposal. If, he asked, a few romping children made a dog lively, would that be considered inciting to bark? (Laughter.) If a lady's poodle barked at another, would that be punish- able. (Laughter). Answering Mr. Callis, the Town Clerk said the by-law did not relate to dogs on the promenade. (Laughter). The order is the result of complaints by visitors.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND is a good Cough Mixture. Effective and Economical. 01 Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
