      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PREVENTS McDOUGALLI SELF, IN DIGESTION</head>
        <p>PREVENTS McDOUGALLI SELF, IN DIGESTION</p>
        <p>AMERICAN LABOUR TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>THE LOS ANGELES TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>[By Mr. H. Whithaml</p>
        <p>It is safe to say that not since the time when the fate of the nation as a unity has the United States been in such:an excited state as it has experienced since the morning of October Ist, 1910. At that time the plant of the " Los Angeles 'limes" was, by explosion and fire, completely wrecked, and the lives of twenty-one persons crushed cut, and otherwise destroyed. A year previous to this horror the Llewellyn Iron Works was destroyed by dynamite, but no lives were sacri- ficed. Those who are unacquainted with the Labour and Capital question as it exists to-day in the United States may well put the question, " What is it all about? " A part of the answer may be, "We are in a vicious circle," or We are in a puzzle maze, and no one can EA the way cut." Whatever answer may ' be given to the question, it is an undoubted truth that in eveiy manufacturing city to-day in the United States there are thousands of unemployed in desperate straits ; and in ne large city I know well, life is of ' little value, and to carry nothing in your pecket means too often a good "slugging," for putting the man whoi holds you up to too much trouble for nothing. There is another side, and it is the men who make up the other side or who partly make up the oiler side who have brought about the Reign of Terror, Ithat now exists in the United States. And yet again, there is still another factor to be considered. We have the employer who, in the mad race for wealth, " blindly " insists in running his own works as he thinks fit and employing who he thinks fit, and more than all this, declaring a man is only fit for the scrap heap when he has reached the age of 35. I think the above fairly states the situation, re the employer and the employee. The owner of the building that was blown up prides himself upon being a fighting man. For years he has been unfriendly with the Labour organizations. For years the atmosphere of Los Angeles has been filled with that peculiar electric fluid, that emanates from dis- satisfied labour meetings, when such meetings are guided and controlled by men who carry and are able to wield a big club, and are at the same unprincipled and selfish enough to suggest to the men the idea of making a demand that must be acceded to, or a strike must follow. Here is the vicious circle. Orgacised labour, one trade after another, unskilled unions' one after another, all engaged, indirectly I admit, in fixing the cost of living that has to be faced by the home, the wage earner of such home not being nor able to be in a union. A Japanese has this day done for my wife four hours washing ; she had to pay him ss. If this maze is not a puzzle, and an economic one, I should like to know what is ? This is the situation, and it has called out of its secret depths the two brothers McNamara. They are natives of Cincinnati, one a brainy man, was a member of the International Association of Bridge and Structural Iron Workers, the other a printer. Since the explosion at Los Angeles numerous deft ctives have been hard at work seeking the criminals and gathering up evidence from all parts of the con- tinent. A few weeks ago the two brothers were arrested and the usual preliminaries to a criminal trial commenced. In a sense the effort to form a Jury of twelve good men and true failed. Week after week went by and attoineys on both sides could not find the needed good men and true, when at last the great sensation came ; indeed the nation had two sensations sprung upon it. The two brothers made confession of their guilt, and the State gained complete evidence that bribery, and in consequence perjury, was con- trolling the Jury box. The game was up, the defence was forced by circumstances to acknowledge that it had known for weeks that the arrested brothers McNamara were guilty, and that there were a many more guilty men who must be arrested, and to get , hold of them the best course to pursue was to hang no one. The Court accepted the suggestion and gave a life term to one of the brothers, and fifteen years for the other. This by no means concludes the terrible business. I give the following quotation from remarks made by the chief detective in the case,— "We are going after the men back of the McNarnaras," said Burns, "and the investigation will be pushed energetically In we bring to Justice the men who are really responsible for the dynamiting outrages over the country. "I have lately come from Indianapolis, where I conferred with the United States District Attorney. Enough evidence will be brought before the Grand Jury at the meeting there December 11th to cause a number of indict- ments. "When Samuel Gompers tells the people be was deceived in the McNamaras he is uttering a lot of drivel and buncombe. It is Gompers who is fooling organized labour, for he knew the MeNamaras were guilty, and alter we had arrested them he came to Indianapolis and sat in conference with them." The Samuel Gompers referred to above is president of the American Federation of Labour. This state- ment made by Burns as to the character of Gompers Ilmeans a very great deal to the workmen of the United I States. lie has an immense number of supporters, and when this statement has a greater' breadth given to it by United States District Attorney Miller we may hear something in the air, not at all pleasant. Says the Attorney," Gompers is an unsafe leader, and it is time for labour leaders who stand on honest ground to take charge of labour organizations." I The situation at present is a demand for peace in theindustrial world. While this may be a consumma- tion devoutly to he wished for, I am decidedly of opinion that it will not be gained if the conversation as reported be true. The employers insist upon the open shop, which really means no member of a labour organization shall be employed. The union labour man refuses to work with the non-unionist. He refuses to handle in any form any kind of material that has previously been handled by a non-union man, or that does not have en it the Union Labcur stamp. The Unionist declares against compulsory arbitration. He claims the right to strike, boycott or picket to gain what he under- stands to be his rights. For myself I see no peace without the closed shop. I see no peace without the utter and most complete routing out of the scab system that has grown into an organized system of trampling out all human rights. I speak from experience. We shall have no peace until secret labour meetings become a thing of the past, and employers quit all their secret methods of dealing with labour. WHITHAM. San Francisco.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance on Sunday at the Wycliffe P.S.A. meeting which was presided over by Mr. B. Farrar, wbo spoke upon God's great mercies, His over- ruling providence, watchfulness and preserving care over us, and pointed out our duty to Him in return. Mr.; Farrar wished all present "A very happy and prosperous New Year." The Rev. W. Cooper gave an interesting address upon "George Fox, the Quaker." Fox was, he said, early drawn towards the truths of the Bible, and wanted help from the cleric. He failed to obtai a it, but he became a student and while he worked he thought out the plan of salvation. He was taught of God, and became an evangelizer of the world. He was driven from town to towr, but be preached Jesus, even behind the prison bars, and although imprisoned in a damp, filthy dungeon his faith in God was strong ; and God used him in bringing many to Christ. Mr. Payton played an organ solo "Bethlehem," in masterly style. Mr. McEachren also took part in the meeting, which concluded with the singing of the hymn "A few more years shall roll."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MUSICAL FESTIVALS, NO LONGER "ORGIES."</head>
        <p>MUSICAL FESTIVALS, NO LONGER "ORGIES."</p>
        <p>At the Conference of the Incorporated Society of Musicians at Llandudno on Wednesday there was a dis- cussion Cu the question of musical festivals. Mr. John Barrett, of Bristol, said that a change was coming about in regard to the musical festivals of this country. They were DO longer what Sir Arthur Sullivan described as "an orgy." In former days the festival was the only opportunity every three years of hearing good music, but now music was increasing in point of interest and in the number of performances, and he thought it would be well for the society to recognise the changes and give greater attention to local talent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>THIRTY NE IV BOOKS A DAY.</head>
        <p>THIRTY NE IV BOOKS A DAY.</p>
        <p>According to the "Publishers' Circular," the number of books published during the year is 10,914, an increase of 110 on the total for 1910 and of 4,870 on that of 1001. This is the highest total ever recorded for a year's publishing in the United Kingdom. Owing to the events of the year, the rate of production has not been so steady as in average years, and the monthly totals vary from 673 in June (the Coronation month) to 1,527 in October. The increase appears to be dis- tributed evenly over all classes of literatare.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>RAI 31NO FLOUR</head>
        <p>RAI 31NO FLOUR</p>
        <p>NEW LAWS.</p>
        <p>LEGISLATIVE ENACTMENTS-1912. BY A WELL-KNOWN BARRISTER., ~) The Parliamentary session of 1911 will be remembered as one of the most prolonged and laborious of recent times. When Parliament was adjourned on August 22nd the most important work was left over for the autumn sitting. The legislative fruits of the year cer- tainly more than make up for the meagre output for the year 1910.</p>
        <p>Besides the Parliament Act and 'the National Insurance Act, two measures fraught with enormous results to the nation, time Wat found for the passing of many other imr- portant and useful bills into law.</p>
        <p>Altogether, without accounting for numerous private bills, there were sixty-five public Acts passed during the Kesion. Of these fifty-five were Government bills and ten were private Members' bills. We proceed below to give a brief summary of their effect. Wlliere no date is given as to the commencement of the Act it should be understood that it came into opera- tion on the date when the Royal Assent was given. The provisions of the Annual Acts, which are necessary to be passed in order to keep going the machinery of government, need not be noticed here. They are the Appropriation Act, 1911, the Army (Annual) Act, the Con- solidated Fund Acts.(No. 1 and No. 2), and the Expiring Laws Continuance Act. The last-mentioned Act has the effect of continu- ing in operation certain Acts of Parliament which otherwise would come to an end on December 31st, 1911. Among these may be named the Agricultural Rates Act, 1896, the Unemployed Workmen 'Act, 1905, and abouk, thirty-nine or forty others. The public is familiar with the main pro- visions of the Parliament Act, therefore it need not be dealt with at length here. The object a tils. AM' ;1, niralof +he. rols.t.ions</p>
        <p>superannuation, or retihving allowances the legal personal representatives of deceased officers or servants of the Indian Govern- ment. The latter Act increases the number of Judge's of the High Court of Judicature in India to twenty, and also enables additional High Courts to be established in any portion of his Majesty's dominions in India.</p>
        <p>The Rag Flock Act was passed In prevent the manufacture of bedding and other articles from unclean rag-flock. The Act applies to Scotland and Ireland, and will come into operation on July Ist, 1912. SHOP HOURS.</p>
        <p>The Shops Act amends and extends the Shops Regulation Acts 1892 and 1904. The measure when introduced was very wide and comprehensive, but in order to save the bill it had to be shorn of its most controversial clauses. It secures for every shop assistant a weekly half-holiday, that is, after half-past one o'clock in the afternoon. The provision is not to apply to the week preceding a bank or public holiday, or any day of public rejoicing or mourning if the shop-assistant is not em- ployed on that clay, and if he gets a half. holiday in addition during that week. There is an exception during four months of the year in favour of shopkeepers who grant two weeks' holiday with full pay to their assistants each year. Intervals for meals must be allowed in accordance with the First</p>
        <p>Schedule of the Act. The Act also provides for the closing-of all shops not later than one o'clock in the afternoon on one week-day in every week. Power is given to the local authority to fix a day for closing; but, unless such a clay is fixed by order, the occupier shall close his short on the dem pauseified by</p>
        <p>him in a notice atnxed to the snop, but ne cannot change the day oftener than once in a period of three months. Certain trades and businesses enumerated in the Second Schedule of the Act are exempted from the Provisions of the Act as to weekly half-holiday. The Act is to apply to Scotland and Ireland, and is to come into operation on May Ist, 1912. The Copyright Act deals with Imperial copyright and International copyright. Sec- tion 1 enacts that copyright shall f3tibsist throughout the parts of his Majesty's dominions to which this Act extends if (a)in the ease of a published work, the work W first published within such parts of his Majesty's dominions; and (h) in the case of an unpublished work, the author was st the date of the making of the work a British sub- ject or resident within such parts of his Majesty's dominions. Copyright is defined in section 1, and its infringement is defined in section 2. The term of copyright is extended from forty-two years from the date of pub- lication to fifty years from the death of the author. Copyright is also extended to a much wider class of literary and artistic pro- ductions than hitherto. The Act 16 to come into operation, so far as the United King- dom is concerned, on the first day of July, 1912, or such earlier date as may be fixed by Order in Council. The Coal Mines Act is an Act to consolidate and amend the law relating to coal mines and certain other mines, viz., mines of stratified ironstone, mines of shale, and mines of fire. clay. In this article it is impossible to give other than a very brief outline of the objects of the Act. Its provisions are designed to safeguard the lives and health of colliers. It raises the minimum age of boy and girl workers underground to thirteen years, and gives fuller protection to horses worked underground. The Act provides that every mine shall he under the daily supervision of a duly appointed manager or under-manager, limits the number of mines for which a manager may act, and defines the qualifica- tions of managers. It also provides for the appointment of inspector; of mines, with cer- tain power to enter, inspect, and examine mines. The Act applies to Scotland and Ire- land subject to nee2ssary modifications, and comes into operation on July Ist, 1912.</p>
        <p>HOUSE FURNISHING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>OFFICIAL SECRETS.</head>
        <p>OFFICIAL SECRETS.</p>
        <p>tween the two Houses of Piti:ll4[nel:it, to define the powers of the House of Lords over general legislation, and over Money Bills, and to limit the duration of Parliament to five years. The absolute veto of the Lords is abolished, and, instead, it has now only what may be called a suspensory veto of not less than two years and of three sessions. The Lords have no power either to amend or re- ject a. Money Bill, certified as such by the Speaker of the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>OLD-AGE PENSIONS. The Old-Age Pensions Act, 1911, amends certain provisions of the principal Act. The Act defines when a person arrives at the ao when he is entitled to a pension, by providing that a person is deemed to have attained the age of seventy on the commencement of the (lay previous to the seventieth anniversary of his birth. The remaining sections deal with the various anomalies met with in the admini- stration of the Act of 1908, particularly in re- gard to the calculation of the mes.ns of mar- ried couples living together, and as to the nationality of a woman who was a British sub- ject before marrying an alien, and also as to the disqualification for a pension owing to the receipt of 'poor relief, and owing to a convic- tion with imprisonment without the option of a fine.</p>
        <p>The Isle of Man (Customs) Act, 1911, illus- trates the control which the Imperial Parlia- ment exercises over that island, notwith- standing that it has its own legislature. The Act sanctions the continuance of the duties imposed by the Manx Customs Acts of 1900 and 1906 on tea, tobacco, spirits, and beer im- ported into the Isle of Man.</p>
        <p>The Revenue. Act (1 George V. c. 2) is rather important, as it amends the principal Act relating to the taxation of Land Values. By section 1 any contract made between the transferor and transferee by which the bur- den of the increment duty is placed on the latter is void. The other sections of Part I. of the Act are mainly explanatory of the sec- tions in the principal Act dealing with the new tax, as on land values and minerals. Part 11. deals with Excise duties. The annual value of any premise.s for the purpose of Excise licences is defined in section 8. The Act provides that in Ireland the annual value shall be determined by the Commis- sioners of Customs and Excise. Section 10 deals with the keeping, buying, and selling, or having in possession live game for the pur- pose of breeding. Part 111. relates to Income- Tax, and provides, among other things, that where the Commissioners are unable to ob- tain from a husband a satisfactory return of his wife's income, they may require the wife to make such return. Part IV. relates to stamp duties. Part V. alters the provision in the principal Act relating to payments of land value and local taxation duties to local authorities. By section 91 of that Act, half the proceeds of the land taxes was to be paid over to the local authorities. The operation of this section is how suspended until March 31st, 1914, and instead a provision is made for the payment of a sum out of the Consol,i- dated Fund to make up the deficiency. The Geneva Convention -Act, 1911, makes certain regulations . and resfrictions as to the use of the heraldic emblem of 'a red cross on a white ground. It prohibits, under • a penalty of £lO, the use of the emblem for the purpose of track) or business. Proceedings under the Act can only be instituted in Eng- land and. Ireland with the consent of the Attorney-General. The Acts 'affecting India which were passed during the session, are the Govern- ment of India Amendment Act, and the Indian High Courts Act, 1911. The former amends the Government of India Act, 1858, by giving in ePrinill ca.F.O4. comnensation.</p>
        <p>7</p>
        <p>The Official Secrets Act, in view of the recent cases of espionage, is of considerable importance. The Official Secrets Act of 1829 is re-enacted with amendments. The present Act contains very stringent provisions to pre- vent the publication of Government secrets, which may be directly or indirectly useful to an enemy. In certain cases the offence is de- clared a felony, punishable with penal servi- tude for not less than three or more than seven years. In other cases it is declared a 'misdemeanour punishable with imprisonment with or without hard labour for not more than two years, or a fine' or both fine and im- prisonment. Knowingly harbouring a spy, or refusing to divulge any information which it is in one's power to give in regard to such per- sons, is a misdemeanour punishable with im- prisonment for one year, or a fine or im- prisonment, or both. The Pensions (Governors of Dominions, &amp;c.) Act authorises the Secretary of State to grant a pension to any Governor who has completed not less than ten years' service in that capacity on retirement at the age of sixty, or on breakdown, or on abolition of the office. For the purpose of the Act, Gover- norships are divided into four classes. In addition to a pension under this Act, a Civil Service pension may also be granted to a Governor who is entitled to one, but the total &amp;Mount in any case is .not to exceed £1,300 per annum. The Perjury Act consolidates the law re- lating to perjury, false statements on oath made otherwise than in judicial proceedings, and similar offences. The Act does not apply to Scotland or Ireland.</p>
        <p>The Aerial Navigation Act illustrates the progress of aviation, and nd doubt many more Ae.faels,42.l in a with thia Tnatt,c,, ix ;11 fanow.</p>
        <p>The present 'Act provitles for th 4 protection of the public against the clangers incidental to flying. Power is given to the Secretary of State to prohibit aviation over prescribed areas. The Factory and Workshops • (Cotton Cloth Factories) Act is a useful and beneficial addi- tion to our factory legislation. It empowers the Secretary of State to make further regu- lations for the protection of workers in cotton factories. The Act is the result of the report of the Committee appointed in November, 1907, to inquire into the subject. The Merchant Shipping (Seamen's Allot- Mein/ Act, 11111, interprets the Acts of .03134 to 1906 in regard to the payment of seamen's allotment notes. The Municipal Elections (Corrupt and Illegal Practices) Act amends the Act of 1884. It provides that any person who, before or during any municipal election, for the purpose of affecting the return of any candidate, makes or publishes any false statement of fact in relation to the personal character or conduct of such candidate, shall be guilty of an illegal practice, and, there- fore, subject to the penalties and conse- quences provided in the 1884 Act, unless fit proves that he had reasonable grounds for believing, and did believe, the statement made to be true. A candidate is not liable for an illegal practice committed by his agent unless it can be shown that the false statement was authorised, or consented to, by the candidate, or unless on the hearing of a petition the court is satisfied that his elec- tion was obtained or materially assisted by the circulation of the false statement. CRUELTY TO ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>- - The Protection of Animals Act is an amending and consolidating statute dealing with animals and knackers. Nine previous acts are repealed either wholly or in part. Cruelty is defined, and includes causing or permitting any unnecessary suffering to an animal, or, as owner, 'permitting the con- veyance or carriage of any animal so as to cause it unnecessary suffering, or causing, procuring, or assisting at the fighting or bait- ing of any animal. An owner is to be deemed to have permitted cruelty if he has failed to exercise reasonable care and supervision in protecting the animal therefrom. Any per- son found guilty of any of the offences under the Act shall be liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding £25, or alternatively, or in addition, imprisonment not exceeding six months with or without hard labour. Power is given to the Court to order the de- struction of an animal if satisfied that it would be cruel to keep it alive. Restrictions and regulations are imposed on the trade or business of a knacker, and constables are authorised to enter on the premises to see if the regulations are being complied with. Animals impounded are to be provided with suitable food and water. The Act does not apply to Scotland, but with certain modifica- tions it applies to Ireland. It comes into operation on January Ist, 1912. The Poultry Act contains provisions (1) for the protection of live poultry from unneces- sary suffering while being conveyed either by land or water, and in connection with their exposure for sale; and (2) for cleansing and diAinfecting receptacles or vehicles used for the conveyance of live poultry. The Act also gives certain powers . of inspection to duly appointed officers. The Telephone Transfer Act provides for the acquisition of the property of the National Telephone Company, Limited, by the Post- master-General, and for the further improve- ment of telephonic communication. Part I. relates to financial provisions, and Part 11. makes provisions for the transfer of the officers from the service of the Telephone Company to that of the Pnatmaeter-G‘merr...:.</p>
        <p>Chapter 7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NATIONAL INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>--- The fact that the National Insurance Act has been passed is familiar to everybody. It is impossible to do more than merely touch its fringe in the scope of this article. The Act establishes a scheme of insuranoo against sickness and unemployment. As re- gards the former, it is compulsory for all per- sons above sixteen and under sixty-five years of age who are employed under a contract of service, except those otherwise provided for, such as men in the Army and Navy, persons in other public services, elementary school trnanharis who ar liqihl for sune.ranmiation</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Some Folks Use</head>
        <p>Some Folks Use</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha simply because it makes clothes whiter and a better colour than any other soap. Others use Fels-Naptha because it saves so much work and bother. Others use it because it makes the clothes last so much longer. Fes-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
        <p>benefits, and employees of local 'anthorities for whom provision is being made at least as good as that in the Act. With the exception of those persons who receive not exceeding fifteen shillings a week wages, the contribu- tion of the insured person will be 4d. per week (men), and 3d. per week (women). The employer will contribute 3d. per week, and the State contributes two-ninths of the bene- fits, which is equivalent to about 2d. per week. In return, the benefits will be:</p>
        <p>(1) Sanatorium benefit for the insured per- son, his wife and children; (2) Medical attendance, medicine, and surgical appliances for the insured person; (3) Sickness benefit of 10s. per week for twenty-six weeks, commencing from the fourth day of sickness, provided notice is given; (4) Disablement benefit of ss. per week lig long as the insured person is unable to work owing to illness; (5) Maternity benefit of 30s. for the wife of the insured person, and if the wife is herself an insured person' she will get an additional 30s.</p>
        <p>No contributions are payable during sick- ness, and tnere is a period allowed in each year for unemployment during which pay- ments need not be made. The scheme will be administered mainly through the societies which become approved within the meaning of the Act, but the medical benefit will be ad- ministered through the local Health Com- mittee. Separate Commissioners are estab- lished to carry out the scheme in England, Scotland, Ireland, and Wales. Besides the compulsory scheme, a person who earns under £l6O per annum may join voluntarily under prescribed conditions. The scheme of Insurance against unem- ployment applies to two large groups of tmdes, viz., the building and engineering. Every workman employed in these trades must join by contributing 2d. per week, to Which the employer adds 24d. per week. A modified rate of contribution is provided in the case of workmen below the age of. eighteen. In return for these contributions, the workman is entitled to unemployment benefit at the rate of 7s. for each week fol- lowing the first week of unemployment. No workman shall receive unemployment benefit for more than fifteen or such other number- of weeks as may be prescribed, either gene- rally or for any particular trade or branch_ thereof, within any period of twelve months, or in respect of any period less than one day. The Act comes into operation on July 15th, 1912, but power is given, should necessity arise, to postpone its commencement not later- than January Ist, 1913, in the case of health insurance, and not later than October Ist,. 1912, in the case of unemployment insurance. Among the other numerous Acts of lees im- portance may be mentioned the following: The Naval Discipline (Dominion Naval Forces) Act is intended to give effect to the agreement arrived at by the Imperial Con- ference with respect to the government and discipline of the naval forces of the self- governing dominions, and their relations to the Royal Navy and to one another. The Maritime Conventions Act amends the law relating to merchant shipping to enable' the Brussels Conventions ta be carried into effect.</p>
        <p>By the University of Wales Act that univer- sity is empowered to hold qualifying examina- tions in medicine, surgery, and midwifery for. the purpose of granting a diploma or diplomas conferring the right of registration under the Medical Acts.</p>
        <p>The Military Manoeuvres Act makes certain small amendments in the Military Manoeuvres Act, 1897, with the object of removing certain- difficulties which are found to occur in the working of that Act. The amendments have been recommended by various Military Man- oeuvres Commissions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ECZEMA ON ARM, COULD NOT SLEEP.</head>
        <p>ECZEMA ON ARM, COULD NOT SLEEP.</p>
        <p>" The Cuticura Remedies I can strongly recommend to any person suffering from eczema. I can tell you I got relief the first time I used the Cuticura Soap and Ointment. I had eczema on the wrist of my right arm ; I had it for fully six months and "tried lots of remedies that people told me about, but did no good. A chemist told me it was eczema and gave me a small box of ointment, but it had no effect. I often Ektir the Cuticura Remedies advertised so I resolved to try them and see. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>" I am very pleased to say that the first time I used both the Cuticura Soap and Cuticura Ointment they gave me relief. I suffered over six months and some- times I could not get to sleep for the itch in my arm Then the pimples broke out and the moisture spread on my arm and a city, crusty scale came over it. My arm was all swollen and inflamed up to my shoulder. I could hardly lift it up. When I got the crusty scales washed off, it was raw red flesh underneath, but in alput a week's time you saw it disappearing and a nice new skin coming over it. "It is all nice and strong and I can work in as much water as I need, it does not hurt it. I just cm-€d it with Cuticura Remedies." (Signed) Miss M. Lillie Hatchednize, Coldstream, Berwickshire, N. 8., April 13, 1911. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free from nearest depot : Newbery, 27, Chsrterhonse Sq., London ; R. Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N•S•W• ; Lennon Ltd., Cape Town ; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay ; " Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Shaving Stick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD. The First Eleven put up a good performance last week in making a 2-2 draw with such a strong team as Timperley at Timperley, the latter having the assistance of A. Menzies and Chappell. From the start the visitors showed good hockey although ;Timperley led by 2--1 at half time, Travis scoring for Latchford . There was some sound hockey played by both sides in the second half end each goal had to eral narrow escapes, Travis at length finishing a strong attack by scoring an equalizing gee!. Chappell in goal for '1 imperley played very finely and Dixon for the visitor also kept out raspy hot shots by good stick work. Th Timperley full backs and forwards were very good bu Latchford had the pull at half-back, Isherwood Wilkinson and Knight all doing well against some fast and clever forwards. Dale and Fletcher on the visitors, right wing and Travis in the centre were prominent among the forwards. The Second Elevens met at Latchford and a hard game ended in favour of Timperley by 2-1. Latchford were a goal up early on from a shot from outsido left tnt the visitors inside left put his side on even terms before half time with a Oran fast shot. Ding dong play took place after changing ends and then, following a penalty corner, an amusing scramble took place in the- home goal-mouth, from which the one of the rimperley forwards popped the ball into the net. Bunt at centre for the visitors led his line well and himself played a, nice polished gsme. Behind the backs and goalkeeper were also good. The best man on the Latchford side was Wright in goal who played a sterling game and certainly saved his aide from a more decisive defeat. Walsh at full back and Bradshaw at centre half also had much to do with keeping the Timperley forwards in check.</p>
        <p>Th) First Eleven at Bebington to-day is :—T Dixon, goal ; P Allanscn, K Walsh, backs ; A E Bradshaw, J B Wilkinson, A Knight, halves; R. A Dale, J Pearson,. D Travis, H Parker, E Banks, forwards. Referee, Mr Cobb. Train from Central Station 1-8 p.m. The Second Eleven v Sale " B" at Latchford is : H Wright, goal ; A Albinson, R Parkinson, backs ; H Eccles, F %V Isherwood, T Beaks, halves ; H It Colton, H Fletcher, W Fletcher, W H Minards, A Starkey, forwards. Referee, Mr Warburton. Bully-off 2.15 p.m. prompt.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CURATE'S RESIGNATION. The Rev. Dr. Devincey, an author of well-known theological works,' has resigned the curacy at St. John's,. Earlestown, and with the closing of the year ceased his duties. Be will take up private residence in Liverpool for some thee. His successor is the Rev. Oscar Hill, of' Ravenlead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NOTES BY " MUST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " MUST."</p>
        <p>Poor old Warrington. They beat both the previous Colonial teams who appeared at Wilderspool, but our visitors on Saturday avenged their countrymen in no uncertain fashion. To the vast surprise of Warring- tonians who had not seen them before,the Australasians waltzcd round the Wirepullers to the tune of 34 points to 6. I have contended ever since I first saw the present combination from down under, that they were the finest team which has ever landed in England ; possibly the doubting ones at borne will be prepared to admit the fact now that they have seen the ease with which Warrington were crumpled up last Saturday.</p>
        <p>It may be argued that Warrington are not the team they were, which is quite true. But the best team Warrington ever put in the field would not have checked our visitors t f last Saturday in my °pillion. They play faster, cleverer, and more resourceful foot- ball than hog ever been seen previously in this country, and they have left the home teams with a lot to learn. We have neither the men nor the methods of the Colonials. The fact may be hard to swallow, but it is a fact, nevertheless, and the sooner it is fully absorbedand the better.lf any further proof were needed the third test match on Monday affrods it in full. The Colonials beat the chosen of the Union as easily as they beat Warrington, by 33 points to B—and that after being eight points behind.</p>
        <p>There was a general hope—based on reliable experience —that Warrington would rise to the occasion last Saturday. Instead they gave a most inept exhibition of football, and were so much the inferior side that the wildest partisan could find nothing to enthuse about. Not a man distinguished himself, for the simple reason that there were better men opposed to him. Bradshaw, Dickenson and Arnold did try to stem the tide of disaster but their efforts were ill-supported.</p>
        <p>The home half backs were the worst section of the team, and instead of centre being the chief weakness, the halves are now the least satisfactory, part of them. Brooks is out of condition and making none of the running that distinguished him miler in the season, while Creevey has faded away to nothing. On 3 wonders if the St. Helens man is really trying. He certainly does not look for work even on the attack— and the lees said about his defence and the better. Taylor was highly tried on Saturday at full-back, and though safe, he played a very weak game, his punting comparing very unfavourably with that of Hallett. Mclntyre did little on the wing and would have been much more serviceable in his proper Position.</p>
        <p>The problem in frost of Warrington has become Increasingly serious, as a result of the falling-off in half-back play, and the committee are not to be envied in their efforts to build up a workmanlike team. With the cup-ties almost in view, lam sure no effort will be spared t 3 remedy the more glaring defects. Present prospEcts are gloomy in the extreme.</p>
        <p>The visit of Huddersfield to-day does not offer much hope, seeing that the Fartown men won here last time by 30 points to 8, and are probably a better aide now than then. A week later, Warrington go to Oldham for the annual drubbing. Then follow a few home matches which may revive the falling barmieter, and raise the requisite hopes for the first round of the Cup.</p>
        <p>Just another word about the Colonials. I cannot refer to many of the men individually, but I think it will be admitted that McKivat is the finest scrum half who !As yet been seen at Warrington, while better combination on a heavy ground there could not have been. Frawley pleased his Warrington admirers with a clever disrlay anl a bag of three tries. On the other. wing Berecry took everyone's eye with his fine phyrnque and some beautiful running and doubling. Ete well earned the preference to Boxer Russell for ..the concludidg test match on Monday, and is the most improved player of the tour. Nothing further about the forwards. They stand in a class by themselves.</p>
        <p>The League table up to Saturday last is as follows: For. Against. Per. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'nege Wigan l9 17 2 0... 56 72 328... 18 18 90 89.47 Huddersfl'ld2l 17 2 1... 72124 516... 26 30 142 87.50 Hull K. 11 19 14 5 0... 61 GI 311... 36 33 151 73.68 St. Helens _l9 13 6 0... 41 63 271... 33 32 162 68.42 Oldham ^0 13 6 1... 55 76 338... 35 44 202 67.50 Hunslet 20 13 6 1... 34 71 281... 35 33 169 67.50 Halifax l9 11 6 2... 37 57 245 20 34 142 6315 Wakefield T.lB 10 7 1... 38 48 220... 38 49 223 5833 B Vey lB 10 R 0... 34 32 164... 20 25 115 5555 Widnes lB 9 7 2... 29 29 145... 18 29 123 5555 Rochdale 11.18 10 8 0... 34 40 188... 28 49 203 55'55 Dewsbury 19 10 8 1... 21 55 253... 34 44 200 55.26 ...... 2211 9 2. 24 63 267... 27 45 189 51.51 R'eighley _l6 8 7 1... 1789 181... 24 52 204 5312 Warrington.2o 10 9 1... 21 44 178... 32 38 178 5250 Broughton —l9 9 9 1... 19 33 151... 27 30 141 50'00 Leigh "0 9 10 1... 19 43 203... 32 37 175 4750 Leeds ^0 9 11 0... 18 57 237... 31 54 230 9500 Swinton 19 7 9 3... 17 32 154... 31 38 176 44.73 Salford 19 6 10 0... 15 35 155... 35 37 181 39.47 Barrow lB 6 11 1... 13 32 134... 46 56 269 36.11 York 17 G 11 047-12 29 137... 46 48 236 35.29 Runcorn lB 512 1... 11 25 113... 35 41 193 3055 Coventry l9 412 2... 10 25 127... 49 81 350 2611 Ebbw Vale 17 1 13 3... 5 16 78... 48 71 309 14.70 Braciley l7 015 2... 213 65... 66100 432 5.88 Bradford 2l 119 0... 222 102... 53 85 361 5.00</p>
        <p>St. Helens have at last reached the first four, and it looks like being a close fight between them and Oldham for a place. Warrington have dropped below the middle, and points for and against just balance.</p>
        <p>Warrington's defeat at Runcorn on New Year's Day was not unexpected, if disappointing. With the weak side which took the field there could not be much hope of success even against the Linnets. The latter are rare triers, and make up for lack of cleverness by enthusiasm for the game. Warrington would have won if the halves had- shown any ability to make Openings, hut neither Jordan nor Daintith made any attempt to beat a man before passing, and the combination was thus of the poorest quality. Bradshaw redeemed the side from utter failure by scot ing two clever tries in the last quarter when Warrington csme near to pulling the game out of the fire.</p>
        <p>The committee of the Lancashire and YoikEhire County Unions met on Wednesday in Manchester and L-ecls respectively and selected the teams for the Lancashire v. Yorkshire match in the County Championship at Halifax on January 11th as follows • _ .</p>
        <p>LAN-C 0311111 E.— Clarkson (Hull), Reid (Widnes), Johnson (Leigh), Harris (Broughton), Flanagan (St. Helens) ; Gleave (Wigan), Ganley (Leigh) ; Lee (Leigh), Johns)n (Widnes), Bailey (Swinton), Briars (St. Helens), Logan (Barrow), Beetham (Broughton).</p>
        <p>YORKSIIIRE.—MetcaIfe (Halifax) ; Moorhonse and -Wagstaff (Huddersfield, Batten and Jenkinson alanslet) ; Smith (Hunslet), Milner (Dewsbury) ; 'Harrison (Leeds), Sherwood (Huddersfield), Haley (Bradford Northern), Mann (Hull Kingston Hovers), Smales (Hunslet), Robinson (Halifax.) These are all Yorkshire-born players.</p>
        <p>I am glad to see that local players had been picked -f.rorn this match, thungh the thirteen for Lancashire 'is not very inspiring particularly at threequarter.</p>
        <p>For to-day's match with Huddersfield, Warrington base made some surprising selections, Tilley has been removed from the transfer list to his old place •at full back and Johnston has been transferred from the captaincy to the transfer 1181 What next, I Wonder? Mclntyre is either injured or dropped, I 11 ,4t not sure whi i cl, but be should be n the team if it to play. There is a vacancy left at wing three- anarter in the hope that the Colonials will release tFrawlEy for this important match. There are only wo more matches of the official tour, and the Warrington man could well be spared by the Australasians.</p>
        <p>Warrington's team for to-day is : Tilley, full back; Bradshaw, Renwick, Dickinson, and A. N. Other, threequarters ; Brooks and Creevey, half backs; Ihcrnas, Shugare, Chesters, Arnold, Tranter and 'earl:day, forwards.</p>
        <p>The "A. " team to visit Pem beiton is : Brown full back ; Waywell, Taylor, Vernon and Povey, threequarters ; Jordan aid Daintith, half backs, II McCabe, Critchley, P Boardman, Bradshaw, Skelhorne, and Cooper, forwards.</p>
        <p>00T6FILL' Atta fif / --f</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>THE WEEK-END MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Saturday. COLONIALS' TOUR.</p>
        <p>Australasians</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>STA inton Oldham 35 St. Helens Rochdale II: lO St. Hord 5 Wigan Hunslet 22 Widnes Barrow 9 Bromley 4 Keighley Huddersfield Hull Kingston R Wakefield T. Hull Leigh 5 Coventry</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>AUSTRALASIANS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALASIANS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A Lesson In Combination.</p>
        <p>There was a fine crowd at Wilderspool on Saturday, interest in the visit of the Co'cilia's being remarkably keen. Though the lowest charge for admission was one shilling, there was a gathering of about 7,500 spectators, (including members) and the receipts totalled .£260. Luckily the day kept fine, but the ground was very soft and holding as the result of heavy rain during the week. ' The visitors fielded almost their finest team, including Flawley, who is soon to return to the Warrington colours. On the home side there were last minute changes, Woodburn being unable to play. Mclntyre went on the wing, and Ike Taylor was entrusted with , the full back position. Prior to the game, M. Creevey ' the Warrington half back, gave a jumping exhibition, which was interesting, but nearly resulted in an injury to the player. The teams were : Warrington—Taylor, back ; Mclntyre, Dickenson, Renwick and Bradshaw, threequarter backs ; Brookes and Creevey, half-backa ; Shugars, Thomas, Johnston, Arnold, Fearnley, and Tranter, forwards. Australasians—Hallett, back ; Frawley, Gilbert, V. Farnsworth and Berecry, threequarter backs ; W Farnsworth and McKivat, half-hacks ; Williams, Noble, Cann. McCue, Murray and Sullivan. forwards. Referee, Mr W H Wood, Huddersfield ; touch judges, Messrs It Moore, Runc3ru, and \V T Chapman, Swinton. . . . .</p>
        <p>1 After- 'Warrington hed given the Colonials a hearty cheer and heard the Maori war-cry in response, the , serious business of the afternoon was commenced. The locals began in bustling style, and Renwick was early prominent with a floe tackle of Gilbert, the crack Australian centre. Treater and Arnold pat in seine good footwork and for ten minutes there was nothing to choose between the teams. Then the Colonials began to find their feet and Frawley broke away on the left, only to be downed by Bradshaw. Brooks beat Mcgivat and got away, but Creevey did nothing with , the pass and a fine punt by V. Farnsworth sent Warrington to the rear again. The same player snapped up the ball again at centre, and after a tricky run gave to Frawley on the touch line. The wing man cut inside beautifully and after a corkscrew run across the field he raced over the goal-line near the posts and opened the score for the vieitors. Cann tried for goal but did nothing with the greasy ball. The visitors were now getting possession, and some brilliant handling completely non- plussed Warrington. Mcgivat and Farnsworth opened the way for Berecry, who made a great run down touch and then cut in for the posts. Bradshaw raced across and tackled the lengthy winger just outside. Berecry threw the ball away instead of playing it, and Williams got a soft try, which the referee allowed despite the protests of the home team, The kick at goal was again miserably poor. Warrington came up scratch again and 13radshew put in a capital dribble, but was forced into touch by Frawley. Mcgivat soon got his men going again, and a clever move by the cnptain sent Cann over under the posts for a simple try. Gilbert kicked a goal. At this stage the locals were com- pletely outplayed by the clever, speedy Colonial, and it was not long before Gilbert dashed througk from centre, but failed with the kick at goal. Warrington rushed the ball in loose formation to the other end, and Mclntyre had a penalty shot at goal which did cot realise anything. Back came Frawley at full speel, and a neat transfer to Mcgivat enabled the "skipper " t•) romp over again. Midfield play followed and Dickenson did some good tackling. Then Farnsworth whipped the ball across to Berecry, who strode away at a great pace and made Taylor and Creevey look fooling in their attempt to stop him. The try was easily made, and the interval came with the Colonials leading by 1 goal, 6 tries, 2 points, to nil. On resuming, the Warrington men put their head, down and by desperate play penned the colonials to the line, Brooks made great efforts to break through, but the tackling of Ballet and Farnsworth was sound. The borne forwards were very active at this stage and after a rushing dribble by 'Thomas, Johnston forced the ball over and scored, Mcintyre making a fair attempt at goal. Again Warrington pressed, and the visitors were lucky in holding up Renwick when he appeared likely to go over. By the aid of clever passing the Colonials made matters warm for the " Wirepullers," and the home side were fortunate in having Berecry pulled up. Mr. ‘Vood ruling a pass taken at half-way to be forward Brookes made an opening for the rear line and had Dickenson been anything like a sprinter be would have troubled Ilallett. Cann and Williams dribbled out of danger and following on another hot raid on the pavilion goal, came a sequence of quick transfers which eventually lodged the ball in the possession of Frawley, who simply tricked Bradshaw with a clever swerve to score the visitors' seventh try. The Warringtonians fell back into their old groove, and the Australasinna maintain- ing a brilliant turn of speed, Berecry effected a fine run along the reserve touch and disaster was averted when the Sydney man was forced out of bounds. Mcliivat was very active in keeping his men on the move, and after a clever passing movement, in which Gilbert, the Brothers Farnworth and Williams were the shining lights. Cann was sent over fora try near the posts, but Williams was no more successful than his predecessors. Wartington could not cope with the opposition at this period, and after William Farnsworth had beaten the opposition, Gilbert took up the running and the way was made clear for Frawley to score his third try of the day, Hallett missing the shot at goal. The hall was handled with rare facility by the Colonials and another round of passing let in Williams, and this time cheers greeted Frawley when he suceesssully landpd the goal. In the closing stages of the game Warrington made a desperate rally and Brookes and Bradshaw dribbling past Berecry, the tall was eventually forced over. Mr. , Wood consulted the linesman and a try was awarded Bradshaw. Renwick missed the shot at goal. The Australesians attacked what few remaining moments there were, but nothing further accrued. Result : Australasia/is, two goals ten tries (31 points) ; War- rington, two tries (six points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Monday. TITLED TEST MATCH.</head>
        <p>Monday. TITLED TEST MATCH.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. Australasians</p>
        <p>At Birmingham. NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Rochdale Hornets 2 3 13 Bradford N 2 2 10 Leigh 3 4 18 Hull li R 1 1 5 St Helena 5 7 31 litinget 2 2 10 Leeds 4 4 20 York Hull Huddersfield 0 3 9 Broughton R ... 0 1 3 Oldham 5 7 31 Coventry Swinton 4 2 14 Salford 0 0 0 Runcorn 1 3 11 Warrington Wigan 5 937 Barrow 2 0 4 ST. HELENS v. HIINSLET.</p>
        <p>After being beaten by 35 points at Oldham, on Satur- day the saints took it out of Hunslet in the match on New Year's Day. This holiday League fixture attracted 10,000 spectators to the St. Helens ground. Playing with a glaring sun at their backs, Hundet were at once prominent, Whittaker making a grand opening for Mitchell, who ran for the line, but later banded to Farrar, who finished the movement by crossing at the corner. Farrar goaled from the touch line. Immediately upon resuming St. Helens took up an attacking position, and Trenwith dashed away and gave to Prescott, who scored an unimproved try. Hunslet again forced matters, and Place, nicely situated, dropped a goal. End-to-end play ensued, and at the Hunslet end Waddell picked up in the loose and crossed, Turtill augmenting. St. Helens were forcing matters at this stage and from a passing movement Belshaw scored,Turtill failing Resuming Flanagan raced through his opponents and crossed, but the try was disallowed, and the St. Helens forwards dribbled away, and Mavitty rounded Place for Turtill to do the needful. Play was waged in the Hunslet quarters, and Greenwood, nicely served, swerved over near the posts. Turtill again goaled. Butterfield forged past Guerin and I'lace and added another try. Turtill improving. Half-time :—St. Helens 26 points, Hunslet 7 points. Upon restarting St Helens passed on the Hunslet line, and Greenwood ran over, Turtill gosling. Hunslet livened up, and after Guerits had lost the ball Mitchell handed to Farrar,who crossed. Farrar failed. Result :—St Helens 5 goals 7 tries (31 points), Hunslet 2 goals 2 tries (10 points).</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday January 6, 1612.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Warrington at Runcorn.---Another Defeat.</head>
        <p>Warrington at Runcorn.---Another Defeat.</p>
        <p>On New Year's Day Warrington visited Runcorn, but were only poorly represented, Brooks, Creevey, Wood- burn and Mclntyre all being absentees. The Linnets were as selected, and after a herd game they secured a victory which was very welcam• at Irwell-lane. A capital crowd attended the match, several hundred spectators journeying from Warrington and Widnes. Teams : Runcorn—Shaw, back ; Deakin, Butterworth, Steven- son and J NV Bosworth, three-quarter backs ; Watton and Jos. Bosworth, half backs ; Padbury, Richardson, Peacock, Massey, Inman and Donoghue, forwards. Warrington—Taylor, back ; Bradshaw, Renwick, Dickenson and Unsworth, three-quarter backs ; Dainteth and Jordan, half backs ; Thomas, Shagars, Cheaters, tranter, Fearnley and Arnold, forwards. Referee, Mr. C. Platt, Swinton ; touch-judges, Messrs E J Bannaford and T Foulkes. The first half was keenly contested, and Warrington were unfortunate not to got a lead, as Dickenson got over after charging Padbury's kick, but the referee was agalcst hint. Renwick several times put Warrington on the attack, but the combination of the three-quarters was poor, and the wings got few chances. Daintith, Renwick, Dickenson, and Unswortla got going nicely on one occasion, but the wing man was unable to beat Shand, the home full back. Half-time, no score. Soon after resumption Bradshaw showed fine pace on the wing, but Shaw's sterling defence proved ea insuper- able barrier. Runcorn took up the running very strongly and Padbury dashed for the bottom corner in characteristic style, but Taylor barred the way. Richardson failed to gather the ball when well placed. Finally the home forwards dribbled over the line and Bosworth fell on the ball, Richardson's bid for goal falling short. After this reverse Warrington attacked with vigour and Shaw touched down. A knock-on by Watton again gave Warrington a footing in the home half. Padbury cleared them out by following up a kick and downing Taylor heavily. Runcorn got on the attack after this and Watton opened out to Stevenson, who gave 'to Butterworth. Bos- worth accepted the final transfer neatly and ran in, Padbury just failing with the goal shot. Runcorn had largely changed their tactics and were playing the passing game very well. Butterworth scored another try after a fine run by Bosworth, for Richardson to ad i the goal. Warrington struggled desperately and Butterwotth was called upon to get through some sound tickling. At the moment when Warrington looked beaten most a change came over the complexion of matters. The backs got going from the scrimmage and Renwick gave to Bradshaw, who accepted a poor pass splendidly. Be travelled along the top wing at a great rate aud forced his way past Shaw. Thomas making a good but ineffectual attempt with the kick. A few minutes later Taylor ran with the ball instead of kicking, and gave to Bradshaw, who ran over in exactly similar fashion again. Thomas kicked a goal. With only a few minutes to go Warrington played strenuous football. Stevenson was penalized for not playing the ball and Thomas tried a drop at goal, but failed. Result : Runcorn, one goal three tries (IL points) ; War rington, one goal two tries (eight points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CUP. THIRD QUALIFYING ROUND. RYLANDS' RECREATION V. WIGAN HIGHFIELD.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP. THIRD QUALIFYING ROUND. RYLANDS' RECREATION V. WIGAN HIGHFIELD.</p>
        <p>Two thousand spectators attended this match at Warrington on Saturday. The home side were with- out Braruhall, Leah and Stretford, Rylands' playing a grand game in the first half, were unfortunate to be three points behind at interval, as several times they broke away and were unable to bring about a score. Half-time : Highfleld, three points ; Itylands', Resuming, the home team forced tio visitors to defend, as the result of keen forward play. Hennessey left the field hurt and never returned, and Gallop had to retire for repairs. The visitors now had all the game and the home team had to acknowledge defeat,four times being scored in the last quarters and three goals kicked. Final : Wigan Hightleld, 21 points ; ltylands' nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR CUP. SEMI-FINAL.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR CUP. SEMI-FINAL.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECREATION V. CADISHEAD (HOLDERS).</p>
        <p>Great interest was shown in this match at Cadishead on New Year's Day. !Wands had a special train and took several hundred spectators. The Rees started the game bofore 2 000 spectators. Play settled in the home half, but eventually the home team broke away and Roscoe scored, Foster failing. The visitors now played a fast game and the home team R ere kept on the defen • FliVe• L?ah secured and passed to Hennessy, who kicked over the full back and had the line at his mercy, when he was obstructed, the referee giving a try. Half-time: Three points each. Resuming, the home team played hard and were rewarded with a try, no goal resulting. The Recs forced the game to the other end, and after loose play Horrocks scored, Leah failing. 'rho game was now ' exciting and smart following up led to Itylands scoring, no goal resulting. In the last few minutes a penalty goal was scored by Huffman. The whistle sounded for time, with Cadishead a beaten time by three tries (nine points), to one goal two trios (eight points.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT DEADED. RUNCORN WRITE STAR V. CASTNER KELLNER. Played on the Park on Saturday. In the first half Thornton scored a try and B.Nolley placed four goals. In the second half Ilenigan, P. Moore? and Wilson scored tries for the Star, and Jolley added another four coals, making his total for the match eight. Result: White Star, eight goals four tries (28 points) ; Cast ner-Kell ner, pil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM DISTRICT' LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goal.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. TL F. A. PT. Mobberley l4 9 . 3 9 31 ... 11 ...20 Lymm ll . 9 1 1 ... 13 ... 15 ...19 Latchford l5 9 . 5 1 36 ... 21 ...19 Loatock Gralam ... 8 ... 2 ... 2 ... 52 ... 14 ...18 Broadheath 12 6 .. 4 2 ... 33 ... 21 _ll Oughtrington Am'ol.4 ... 5... G ... 3 ... 26 ... 22 ...I3 Bowdon Vale 10 6 . 3 1 ... 19 ... 21 .. 13 Altrincbam Lads 9 4 ... 5 ... 0 ... 18 ... 23 ... 8 St. Vincent' e ll 2 .. 6 3 9 ... 23 7 Oughtringtou Park 8 3 ... 5 ... 0 ... 11 ... 13 ... 6 Sale Albion fl 9 . 8 2 14 ... 29 ... 6 Penketh Kuutsford St Crosslo ... 0 ... 8 ... 2 ... 26 ... 51 ... 2</p>
        <p>BROADHEATII v. ST. VINCENT'S. This local " Derby " took place at Navigation-road, Altrincharn. Both defences held out to the close and although the Heathens tried desperately hard to get even for previous defeats, they had to rest content with a goalless draw. LYIIM v. BOWDON VALE. At Lymm, the first meeting of these two clubs resulted, considering the conditions of the ground, in a capital game. Interest was well maintained right through the game. After one of the best games seen in Lymm for some time the home team gained the verdict with the score—Lymm two goals, Bowdon Vale I none. -</p>
        <p>MOBBERLEY V. ALTRINCHA 1.4 LADS. At Mobberley. Earlier in the season these two teams met three times in the Cheshire Amateur Cup and eventually the Lads won. On Saturday last the Dockers had their revenge and bad by far the best of the game, eventually running out easy winners with the score :—Mobberley, three goals; Altrincham Lads, nil. _</p>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON AMATEURS V. LATCHFORD. This return engagement took place at Heatley on Saturday last. The Amateurs started the same with ten men. The Amateurs were at first the moat dangerous, Holt making a capital effort, but found Green and his backs a great stumbling block. Latch- ford retaliated and a capital effort by Taylor was well cleared by Kay. Both sets of forwards missed many chances, and the all-important goal came in the last minute, Denny heading through for Latchford from a splendidly placed corner by Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The holiday season has been notable for the packed P houses " at each performance at the Palace and Hippodrome, where a well-varied programme has found plenty of favcur. Renee Ray presents a novel and striking spectacular turn. By the aid of duplicate lanterns on the stage the artiste is made the centre of some beautiful tableaux, one picture dissolving and another taking its place while the artiste remains motionless. Same of the colour schemes were wonder- fully clever and the turn was one of the most popular of the evening. Edna Zora, described as the musical comedy girl, was responsible for a bright scena, entitled " The Garden of Music." The lady's singing and choice of ballads were of the sort that usually appeals to the crowd, and she was capably assisted by a droll pair of youngsters in Irish " paddy " garb. Her accompanist was Master Carl Rosina, a juvenile pianist of great promise. There is plenty of smart dancing in the bill this week, and Marguerite Ismay and her troup or girl r tread the light fantastic toe to some dazzling measures, and earn an enthu- siastic reception. Charles Mildare excels as "the premier whistling comedian," his imitations of the nightingale, skylark and thrush, bringing down the house. His singing was, however, not on a par with his whistling. " Interruptions" is the title of a comic drawing-room act between Marguerite Harold and John It. Bennett, the latter distinguishing himself , with a display of genainc low comedy , which provoked roars of laughter and was irresistibly droll. Learto and his " Pom-Pom " supply a clever novelty musical turn, and the Lawrence Bros co-operate smartly in Scotch airs and step-dancing. The Bros. Erard do weird things in the way of tumbling and simultaneous violin-playing. We do not suppose anyone could play the violin better or worse than they do in their way. The pictures are art ever-attractiue feature. SACRED CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Ou Sunday evening Mr. Macnaghten kindly placed the Palace at the disposal of the Old Folk's Treat Committee for a sacred concert in aid of the funds of that deserving institution. The circle was well filled but the attendance in other parts of the building was somewhat disappointing. A number of local artistes 'willingly gave their services sad submitted a pro- gratr me of more than average interest. The orchestral selections, under the skilful conductorship of Mr. E. A. Widdop, were patticularly enjoyable. Instru- mentalists from the Royal Court Theatre lent their co-operation in augmenting the usual Palace orchestra and Mr. Tom BoothrJyd was an excellent leader. The playing throughout was characterised by adm rable crispness, verve, and attention to detail, and finished performancos were given of the overtures " Morning, noon and night" (Suppe)' and "Pique Dame " (Suppe), the novelty "The Butterfly" (Bendix) and the march " Le Prophet. " (Meyerbeer)). Miss N. Mullins, whose offerts in public have hitherto been chiefly associated with light opera, showed considerable talent as an interpreter of more serious music, " 0 Divine Redeemer " (Gounod) being sung with deep feeling and expression. Miss A. Sutton made artistic use of a rich, well-trained contralto voice in " The Enchantress" (L. Hatton) and " The Lost Chord" (Sullivan), the effect of which was enchanced by the orchestral accompaniments. Miss M. Broadhurst gave a pleasing rendering of " Bid me to love " (D'Aver Barnard), her singing being bright and unforced. Mr. J. 11. Draper brought out to the full the contrasting pathetic and triumphant phases of " The Sailor's Grave " (Sullivan) and Mr. P. Bate's robust bass voice was heard to distinct advantage in " There is a green hill " (Gounod). Mr. J. Babbitt achieved a remarkable success with his stirring and soulful vocalisation of " The Trumpeter" (A Dix), and responded to a vociferous encore with" Two Eyes of Grey." The committee desire to express their thanks to Mr. McNacnaghten, to Mr. Turpin and his staff, to the artistes who gave their services, and to Messrs. W. Rogers, Herbert Hayes, George Rogers, F. Craik, B. Allen, H. Maddock and W. H. Malings for their valuable assistance in carrying out the arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>REMARKABLE RIDE. A New World's Long Distance Cyc ing Record.</head>
        <p>A REMARKABLE RIDE. A New World's Long Distance Cyc ing Record.</p>
        <p>This is an age of records, yet we ventnra to predict that amongst the outstanding accomplishments in the realms of sport achieved during 1911 the breaking of the world's long distance cycling record by M. Planes will eclipse all other exceptional feats of endurance that have recently been recorded upon the athletics' scroll of fame. Doggedly and without ostentation for 12 months past M. Planes day by day has been scoring century runs, sometimes riding 200 miles and more before the hands of the clock have completed a single circuit. And all for sheer love of the pastime. This is proved by the fact that the only rewtird Planes could hope to obtain, or in fact has actually received, is the gold medal awarded by "Cycling," the proprietors of which offered the prize to whoever completed the largest number of 100 mile runs within the past year. On December 31st, Planes,who by the way is an unpaced amateur, vanquished 600 competitors by cycling 33,200 miles in 365 days, thereby lowering Ilale's world's professional record by 1,900 miles and breaking Fl rrj Longs world's amateur score by 7,821 miles. In accomplishing his triumphant ride Piaues made 332 separate runs of 100 miles each and for ten out of the twelve months was always several thousands of miles in front of the next best man ; moreover, Planes rode 1,166 miles during 1911 which was not credited to his Century Record, making his total mileage for the year 34,366. Planes is a young man 21 years of age, slim and lithe of physique, whose fitness and staying powers are overwhelmingly evidenced by his ability to reel off runs of 100 miles day after day as unconcernedly and easily as most men undertake a short spin to business and back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>WHAT VOTES COST.</head>
        <p>WHAT VOTES COST.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING RETURNS.</p>
        <p>A Parliamentary return,. published on Wednesday night, gives in detail the expenses incurred by the various candidates at the general election of December, 1910.</p>
        <p>Summarised, the return shows that the returning officers' charges throughout the United Kingdom totalled £187,752 in respect of an election at which 7,691,741 persons were entitled to vote in the 13,609 polling stations provided in 8,601 districts, For the 670 seats there were 1,192 candidates.whose aggregate payments were £978,312, giving an average cost of 3s 8d for each of the 5,235,323 votes polled, the expenses of uncontested elections not being reckoned in the ascertainment of this average. The heaviest average recorded in the case of a successful candidate was the lle. 4d. per vote for Mr. Harry Hope's 1,569 votes which gained the Buteshire seat. Several unsuccessful candidates exceeded this figure, Mr. Victor Grayson being of the list with its, lld, per vote for his defeat in Colne Valley. The cheapest successes were those of Mr. James Parker, junior member for Halifax, and Mr. Ramsay Macdonald, who each averaged 6d. per vote. Other low figures, were the 7d. and Si. respectively of Mr. Edgar Jones, and Mr. Keir Hardy the members for Merthyr, and the 8d of Mr. Philip Snowden in Black- burn.</p>
      </div>
