      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>CHILDREN'S BALL AT THE</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S BALL AT THE</p>
        <p>PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>PICTURESQUE SCENES.</p>
        <p>Over three hundred radiantly happy children thronged the Parr Hall on Tuesdsy evening. They had assembled for the annual juvenile ball in aid of the " Children of Warrington Cot " in the Infirmary, and when they joined with all the zest of youth in the merry round of dances the scene was as picturesque as it was bright and cheeting. It was in truth, a children's night. Many townspeople had worked hard with the dual object of giving the little ones s. thoroughly happy time and doing something for the benefit of our premier chat ity. In both respects they were eminently successful. From a social point of view the function could not have been better and there should also be a substantial contribution to hand over to the cot fund, for which S'4so hss already been been invested.</p>
        <p>On entering the hall, the youthful guests were received by the AI iyor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) and Mrs. Harold Joseph, in place of the Mayoress who was suffering from a severe cold. A pleasing little ceremony was the presentation to the Mayor of a button-hole by Miss Ruth Mathias, daughter of Mr. F. V. L. Mathias, the borough electrical engineer, and to Mrs. Harold Joseph of a bouquet by Master Ernest Bowden (son of Dr. Bowden) Then the children— among whom the fair sex was in a decided majority- -formed into processional order and matched roetEd the hall, the pleasing spectacle evoking admiring comment from the spectators who crowded the galleries. As a charge of sixpence each was made for 1 the general public to view the proceedings, it was indeed gratifying to see such a splendid attendance. Messrs. Crosfield's band played an excellent pro- gramme of dance music and the children never seemed to tire of tripping "the light fantastic toe" Every- thing went with a delightful swing and all seemed sorry when the concluding dance—the old-fashioned country measure "Sir Roger de Coverley "—was reached. The ball was agreeably varied with several special items, the youngsters especially being loud in their appreciation of a clever ventriloquial sketch " Music and Mimicry' by Mr. Alfred Grounds, the well-known local enteitainer. Later ha gave further pleasure with the song "The dream man," the effect -being enhanced by the singing of the chorus from the centre gallery by the Misses Grounds. Miss Marjorie Kelly, from the London Coliseum, displayed wonder- ful ability as a fancy dancer in two very charming dances " Mignon " and " Sylvia." She tripped about as lightly as a fairy and the gracefulness and neatness cf her movements earned rapturous applause. Several pupils of Mise Ethel Spinks afforded evidence of excellent training in three novel dances :-1, Folly dance ; 2, Shadowland ; 3,Hunt dance—each of which was beautifully executed and delighted the audience. The Misses Grounds acquitted themselves admirably in the duet " The dancing dolls." The refreshment room was under the management of the Winmarleigh Cafe Co. whose catering gave the utmost satisfaction.</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the ball were on the most complete and efficient lines, a special word of praise being due to the hard working hon. secretary Mr. 1 James Henry Smethurst and to his capable enthusiastic assistant Mr. James Houghton, jun. The ladies committee consisted of the following:— The Mayoress (Miss Joseph), Mrs and the Misses Forshaw, Mrs and the Misses Smethurst, Mrs E J For, Mrs Bucktnn, Mrs Morley Stevenson, Mrs Brace- camp. Mrs E A White, Mrs Harold Joseph, Mrs J H Smethurst, Mrs Bowden, Mrs Murray (Penketh), Mrs J M Murray, Mrs Joseph Bennett, Mrs E S A Robson, Mrs Mathias, Mrs Ferguson, Mrs Harold Jolley, Mrs James Houghton, jun., Mrs F Shoi t, Mrs T Cooper, Mrs John Evans, Mrs and Miss Sankey (Church-street), Mrs F Parsonage, Mrs Lightburn, the Misses Spinks, Miss Monks, Miss Burton, Miss D Reid, Miss Parting- ton, Miss F E Clarke, Miss Plinston and Miss Lomas. Mr J H Smethurst acted as M.O and the duties of stewards were discharged by : The Mayor (Coun- cillor Dr Joseph), Mr W Peter itylands, Mr J Allison, Mr J Brooks, Mr F Bracecamp, Mr G W A Brown, Dr Burt, Dr Bowden, Mr B Browne, Mr. H Collin, Mr T R Critchley, Mr A. H. Fawcett, Mr L Henshall. Mr G W Houghton, MrJ B Hugbes,Mr James Houghton, jun., Mr J H J Hampson, Mr G Hatton,Mr A Hodgkin- son, Mr R Isherwood, Mr F Jolley, Mr F Kirtland, Mr J Lightburn, Dr Murray, Mr J H Murray, Mr F V L Mathias, Mr S L Partridge, Mr Milling, MrlW A Proud, Mr II Rogers, Mr H Rushworth, Mr J Richardson, jun., Mr W Richardson, jun., MX F Short, Mr I Sankey, Alderman Smethurst, Mr R Sutton, Mr F 0 Stansfield, Mr D Travis, Mr W S Tinsley, Mr J E 0 Thompson, Mr E A White, and Mr J W Welsby. Among others who attended L.) the comfort and enjoyment of the guests were :—Alderman Burton (chairman of the Workingmen's Infirmary Committee), Mr G S Frith, the Misses Broadbent, Mrs Allison, Miss Hulda Hought-m, Mrs W A. Proud, Miss War- burton, Miss Bainbridge, Miss Roberts, Mrs Weir, Miss Spinks, Miss Ellison, Mrs H Davies, and Mrs W Tomlinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Late Mr. Thomas Green.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Thomas Green.</p>
        <p>In the presence of many relative; ;neiTriends, the funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Sunday, of the late Mr. Thomas Green, fruiterer and commission agent, Knutaford-road. The cortege left the deceased's late residence shortly after half past two. Preceding the hearse was a carriage containing the Mayor and Councillor Dr McKee (deceased's - medical attendants) and Alderman Forahaw. The ceremony in the church and at the graveside was con- ducted by the Rev II Ef Corsbie,curate of St. James's. The chief mourners were Mr William Green and Master Thomas Green (sons), Mr William Green (father), Messrs Harry Green (brother), A Tasker (father-in-law), Peter Green, Harry Green (uncles), C Tasker, H Tasker, W Tasker, F Foulkes,lloaborne, (brothers-in-law), Peter Green, James Green, H Green E Green, Harry Green (cousins). Connor, .1 Waters, H Mather, J Howard, R Proctor, Upton Monks, E Lake, A Howard, R Rothwell, H Williams, J Jennings; W IThrigh, H Rimmer, Councillor G- Archer, W Dilworth, II Fogg, T Bailey, Miller. Whittaker, Shepherd, Kitchen, Don:wale, `White, .1 Cross, Mark Upton, Po well, Strange, Lindley, Bates Harrison, Jordan, Lunt, Place, Waterworth, Brierley, Collins, B Ford, Marshall, Connor, jun, Sangster. Gordon (Widnes) and Winfield (Widnes). Wreaths were sent by the following: Mrs Green and family, Mr William Green (brother), Alderman and Mrs Forshaw, Mr J Jennings, Mr Locker, Mr W Gordon Widnes), Mr and Mrs Harrison, Mrs Dilworth, Mr William Whittaker and family, Mr and Mrs Doraville, Mr and Mrs Rothwell, Mr and Mrs II Williams, Messrs James and Joseph Howard, Mr Samuel Wakefield, Mr and Mrs James Monks, Mr S Strange, Mr and Mrs Tasker, Councillor Dr and Mrs McKee, Mr and Mrs Upton, Mt and Mrs Shepherd. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr II F Greenough.</p>
