      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>INDUSTRIAL OUTLOOK.</head>
        <p>THE INDUSTRIAL OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>The opening of a new year is not infre- quently the occasion for a taking of stock— nationally no less than individually—and it is therefore relevant to the moment to con- sider the Irish industrial year which has just closed. And if we are to be really frank we must say straightaway that jubilations over the signs of increasing prosperity of Ireland must be restrained by two considera- tions. One is that the staple industry of the country is, with regard to its fundamental purpose, retrogressing. The other is that the outflow of our people to foreign lands, especi- ally the United States, continues practically without abatement. The emigration returns ' for 1911 are not yet available, but the monthly returns up to and including November shew a decrease, though slight, for the eleven months as compared with the corresponding period of 1910. The balance of figures will not be upsst by the emigration last month. The figures show a result not to be explained as a "natural" emigration,nor yet by the American letter containing the passage money for relatives and friends in Ireland. It isreasonable to couple the decrease in tillage that ;most disturbing fact in the modern economies of Ireland—with the persistence of this exodus of many thousands of the young people of Ireland. There is good work to be done on the land—the work of getting the most out of the soil. Mr. William Field, M.P., at a meeting of the Irish Cattle Traders Association the other day, drew attention to the amazing fact that the number of acres tilled in Ireland in 1911 fell short by many thousands of the number tilled in 1910. The same depressing circumstances are reported from Cork. Notwithstanding yeoman efforts by such men as Mr. Field and other notable public men in Ireland, we cannot help feeling, on the impartial evidence of the agricultural statistics, that some of the landowners in Ireland are losing rather. than gaining faith in mixed farming or more tillage. We cannot but feel though having no special knowledge of the difficulties which face the farmer in respect of increasing his acres under tillage, that one of the first fruits of the additional 20,000 labourers' cottages with plots attached to them, together with the increase in economic holdings, will be to stem this decrease in a most serious portion of Irelanfi's national trade. For in this reform the prosperity of the butter and other industries is bound up since so long as the Irish trader cannot guarantee to his British customers an unfailing supply all the year round, the Danes will hold the field. Although the population has been decreas- ing, Irish imports have increased by over ten millions sterling during the last six years. It is opportune to direct attention of any who are interested in Irish economics to the number of articles unneces- sarily imported. Soap, flour, biscuits, oat- meal, candles, matches, starch—all of which can be had of Irish manufacture—ire included in a list of commodities, in a list of importswe have just examined,valued at more than six and a quarter million sterling. It is clear that when the Home Rule Bill passes into law there will be an abundance of problems demanding the attention of the Dublin legislature to ensure that Irish capital, Irish labour and Irish enterprise may be more closely devoted to the task of catering for the needs of the Irish community at home. -</p>
        <p>--- Another reflection naturally suggested by the foregoing is the development of Irish ports on a practical and business-like basis. Lord Kedlanin, we were glad to notice,in the January issue of the "Empire Review," urges with much reason and sanity the claims of Galway as a Trans-Atlantic port. At present nearly all our exports and imports with the United States and with Newfound- land—the oldest and row one of the most rapidly developing colonies of the Empire— and Canada have to pass through English channels with consequent great increase of mileage in transport and to the hurt of Ireland's commercial reputation and goodwill. Sir Edward Morris, the Premier of New- foundland, has promised a subsidy from the funds of that State to the proposed service; Canada has made the same promise more than once and on all hands there is evidence to show that the scheme would be practical and widely advantageous. It would be possible for the voyager to take his seat in the train at Euston and step from it at the steamer's side in the port of Galway. The route thence even the poorest student of geography can see, would be much quicker than the present route, because steamers leaving Galway would be out in the Atlantic ocean at once, and able to move at full speed instead of slowly working their way through narrow seas and river mouths thronged with craft of every sort and subject to the frequently recurring fogs. Compared with the rest of the United Kingdom, Ireland's benefit from Imperial outlay on works of this kind is small indeed. And in face of the admitted huge growth year by year of Transatlantic traffic and the urgent need for some new channel, why should the community of both islands not rise to the use of a great waterway clearly indicated by the simple geography of the Globe.?</p>
        <p>NEWARK'S MOTTO-" TRUST GOD AND SALLY.'</p>
        <p>'.Newark's Town Council has decided that the motto to go with the borough arms shall be the historic remark made by Major Smith in 1616," Trnst God and Sally "—a remark made when the fortunes of King Charles I. were at their lowest. . _ . _</p>
        <p>The motto will be used in its Latin form, as the Mayor, Alderman Priestly, suggested that the word "sally "was almost obsolete, and would lend itself to misinterpretation by ignorant people.</p>
      </div>
