        <p>"It is nothing short of</p>
        <p>" CRABBING " cruelty," writ es Mr. THE INSURANCE Chiozza Money, "to ACT, attempt to rob aging men of the great chance which the Insurance Act gives them." Yet this cruelty is being perpetrated on the large scale. There is a fresh appeal to ignorance of the Act every day, and the misrepresenta- tions made for the purpose of getting up agitations against the most beneficent measure over passed by the Parliament of any country are absolutely without con- science. The offence of the Insurance Act is that it was passed by a Liberal Govern- ment The attacks made upon it are part of a plan for getting the Conservative Party back into power. If they were successful what would a Conservative Government do with the Insurance Act? Would it repeal it, remodel it, or what? We have no hesita— tion whatever in saying that it would leave the Act alone except as to those necessary amendments which will be dictated by experience, and which will, in due course, be carried out by the authors of the measure.No Government will ever interfere with the Insurance Act in any other sense than this. There is one possible improvement on it. It might be made non-contributory, as the Labour Party demanded ; but if a non-con- tributory scheme had been proposed there can be no doubt at all that the Opposition and all that it represents would have been against it to a man.</p>
        <p>What is the scale of benefits under the Act? Mr. LLOYD GEORGE answered this question at Whitfield's Tabernacle in one comprehensive phrase. He said that the Act gives ninepence for fourpence. This has on many occasions been denied by sneer and insinuation. The statement is nO tv* declared, by a widely-disseminated newspaper which is campaigning against the Act day by day</p>
        <p>MISMM</p>
        <p>BOYDELL Brothers,</p>
        <p>RESPECTFULLY REMIND THEIR CUSTOMERS OF THE- RECURRANCE OF THEIR</p>
        <p>Annual Reduction of Prices</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>in the absence of other exciting topics, to be actuarially unsound. The actuaries ought to know, and the actuary whose business it has been, before anything else, to make himself fully acquainted with the purposes and the probable effects of the Insurance Act con- firms the declaration of the CHANCELLOR OF THE EXCHEQUER. Mr. WATSON, of the Man- chester Unity of Oddfellows, says :</p>
        <p>"The benefits under the bill are worfh nine, pence per week for the first fifteen and a-half years, or for such similar period over which the redemption of the initial reserve may extend, and afterwards sevenpence per week; but by Clause 8 of the bill as amended an extension of benefits is provided for when the redemption has been accomplished, and it must be assumed in fairness that the benefits will be kept up to the value of ninepence for all time. "</p>
        <p>What is the reply to this? There is no reply. It is a simple statement of fact. Those ; who would deny it would deny anything if they believed that they were therebY injuring the party to which the country owes the Insurance Act and OS Age Pensions. What is the immediate object of the Anti- Insurance Act campaign? What can the enemies of the measure hope to achieve? It is affirmed that friendly societies are giving quite as good benefits as are given under the Act. Lord HUGH CECIL, it will be remem- bered, made this assertion concerning a friendly society in his own constituency. It is now being repeated in other quarters. And it is quite untrue. We again quote Mr. CHIOZZA MONEY, who says: "Such benefits, such generous treatment as to arrears in unemployment, such pains- taking endeavour to insure men and keep them insured, are not given by any friendly society. No contributions in sickness; thirteen weeks' arrears without loss of cash benefit; twentysix weeks' arrears without loss of free doctoring or free sanatorium ; maternity benefit; sanatorium benefit; insurance, whatever one's age, as though a boy or girl of sixteen—there is no friendly society in the world which gives all these benefits at any price. "</p>
        <p>This is indisputable. Whatever may be the motive of those who assert the contrary, it is quite certain that they have not in their mind any desire to do a good turn to those to whom the Insurance Act will be beneficial. If there were any real uprising against the new legislation it would be a national calamity. and those who caused it would be the enemies of the people.</p>
      </div>
