      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>If there was any sub- FLESH-AND-BLOOD stance in the opposition BONDS. to Irish Home Rule it would be concentrated on something else than on merely religious prejudice. Do we, as a nation, or do we not, recognise the principle of self-govern- ment? If we do, the religious beliefs of the majority of the people who wish for self- government in domestic affairs are a topic which has no bearing on the main issue. If we do not, then the grant of self-government to Canada and to South Africa were most -startling violations of a principle. At any rate, wherever the gift of self-government has been made, the result has been to create bonds of royalty within the Empire where nothing but discontent existed before. Flesh- and-blood bonds, after all, are stronger than the iron bonds of legality, which outrage every sentiment of nationality.</p>
        <p>7</p>
        <p>Let us suppose that in TOPSEY—TURVEY Ireland two-thirds of LAND. the people were bigoted members of the Church of England, and that the other third were Nonconformists. Would the Tory Party throw itself, with a desperate energy of vituperation, and with such whole-hearted enthusiasm as to threaten even some revolt against the British Parliament, whose desires are the tangible embodiment of the majesty of the British Empire, into the defence of the Nonconformist majority? If not, why not? If Lord Hugh Cecil can work himself into hysterics for the sake of Ulster Presbyterians Why did he welcome a Catholic vote to Inflict upon Nonconformist consciences in England a grievance which has sent hundreds, if not thousands, to prison?</p>
        <p>Liberalism does not FUNDAMENTAL find itself under any LIBERALISM, necessity of perform- ing those hair-turning gymnastics which the Opposition requires of Lord Hugh Cecil, the English " Anglo-Catholic," Mr. Austen Chamberlain, the English Unitarian, and Mr. Bonar Law, the Scottish Presbyterian—all of whom are, it would seem, united to deny justice to English Nonconformists and Irish Catholics. Liberalism believes in nationality as an integral factor of the British Empire, and it is just as much opposed to the imposi- tion by law of an alien Church in Wales, as 'to a denial of the right to the Irish people to have a voice in their own domestic affairs because the majority of them are Roman 'Catholics.</p>
        <p>A considerable propor- MEDICAL TRADE tion of the medical UNIONISM, profession still main- tain their agitation against what they believe to be the terms offered by the Insurance Act. The successful working of the Act depends upon the loyal, cheerful, and efficient co-operation of the 'doctors. The idea that the Government, or JAL.. Lloyd George hoped to work the Act by cadging" for " black-leg " members of the Profession is so absurd that it need only be stated in plain terms to obtain its own refuta- tion. But the doctors, in fighting for better terms than they think they have got, can 'only appeal to one final tribunal—the public. Like any other trade union, the organized body of medical men will be sure of Public support if they restrict them- selves to the demand for what is a living wage under reasonable conditions. But the doctors, on appealing to the public 'must show that they have taken the trouble to understand the legislation against which 'they profess a grievance, and that they are contending, not for some phantom of political Prepossession, but for simple justice and equity. Now, Sir James Barr, President- 'elect of the British Medical Association, has addressed to the " Times " a communication in which he challenges the whole principle of the Act, and derides its humanitarian pur- Poses. If the Opposition is not prepared to -eat its words with unexampled alacrity, the President-elect of the British Medical Association is as completely out of sympathy With Tory as with Liberal views on National Insurance. And the protests of responsible practitioners, which seem to have even taken in" the" Times," show a complete misapprehension both of the question of the supply of drugs to the insured, and of the elasticity which will enable country districts 10 have conditions different from those of a crowded urban district.</p>
        <p>Bishop Welldon has THE CHURCH been inspired, by the AND THE great conference at DEMOCRACY. Cardiff, to emphasise the responsibility of the Churches and the wealthy ;classes in social matters. Speaking to a fashionable audience at Eastbourne, the Bishop construed the demand of democracy as meaning that wealth must be honestly earned and generously sPent rather than that it should be more equally distributed. With the economies of such a theory we are not for the moment, concerned, but has the Bishop interpreted the demand of the working classes correctly or adequately? It is a comfortable doctrine to urge a generous expenditure, and the definition of wealth honestly earned iS capable of wonderous adjustment. We are inclined to think that the hard facts of the democratic demand are more in accord with that proposal which the Bishop denies than With that which he asserts. The grave</p>
        <p>_ unrest in the industrial world, with its accom- Panying strikes and lock-outs, its bitterness and its suffering, can scarcely be interpreted in any other spirit than that of a determined stand for a more adequate share of the wealth Produced in mine and mill, in factory and in workshop. No useful purpose can be served bY minimising the demand, whatever opinions may be held concerning the justice or the practicability of acceding to it. Bishop Welldon went on to speak of the responsi- bility of Christian citizens in the work of social regeneration. To awaken this respon- sibility will be to strengthen the forces of reform and of progress in a manner that must give tremendous impetus to the work of social amelioration. And this is precisely why it is of cardinal importance that the Problems to be faced, and the demands to b.n considered should be fearlessly stated. The good Bishop--even at the risk of offending 'What he termed the polite ears of a fashion- able audience—would have been better advised to have spoken less of the generosity of the wealthy and more of the cry for increased wages, better houses, and more -time to live in them. The forces of the Church are too valuable, and too potent for good, to be allowed to take up the social Mission under the conditions of blind man's buff,</p>
        <p>Jack: "Yes; Grace trusts me. I am sure .c.f it, because she admitted her age to me." OrMe: "Wasn't that clever of her? Jack: lf now do you mean?" Orme: clever to make you believe it!"</p>
        <p>"I Want to see you just a minute," says the lady at the bottom of the steps, "but "lie hobble-skirt is so tight Ic'cln't climb the stairs. You'll come out, won't you?" "1 'would if I could, but this new hat of mine is too wide to go through the dowway." 6ighl l'he one on the inside.</p>
        <p>Page 2 of this issue contains an informa- tive article specially written by a well- known barrister, on "New Laws :Legislative Enactments, 1912." * * _</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Vivian, Candidate at the recent bye-election in South Somerset, was chosen as prospective Liberal candidate for the division at a meeting of the South Somerset Liberal Association on Saturday. * *</p>
        <p>* - The Right Rev. Francis John Jayne, D.D., Bishop of Chester, celebrated his sixty- seventh birthday on Monday. He and the Bishop of St. Asaph (Dr. Edwards) are now the senior acting diocesans, both having been consecrated to their present sees in 1889.</p>
        <p>* Capies of the Liberal Calendar for 1912 have been issued by the Liberal Publication Department, embellished by a portrait of Mr. A. H. Crosfield. The only other portraits on the sheet are those of the Premier, Mr. Asquith, Mr. Lloyd George, Sir Edward Grey, Viscount Morley, and the Marquess of Crewe, so that Warrington's ex-Member is in very distinguished company.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Crosfie-ld have been enter- taining Mr. Lloyd George and Mr. Rufus Isaacs at Cannes from Wednesday to yester- day. The two Ministers had a slight adventure en route as the train by which they had intended to travel was detained owing to the wreck of a goods train near Toulon,and they accordingly elected to motor by the coast road. They have been spending the time in golfing over the Cannes links, Mr. Crosfield being the much-esteemed presi- dent of the Cannes Golf Club. * *</p>
        <p>Mr. T. B. Curran,- ex-M.P. (1892-1900), recalls a slip of the tongue once perpetrated by a member of the House of Commons. This was Mr. Mathias Bodkin, now a county court judge in Ireland, who sat in the House from 1892 to 1895, and on the occasion in question greatly amused his follow-workers by addressing the Speaker as " Your Rever- ence." It is, of course, a very common thing in Ireland for the parish priest to preside at political meetings. Hence, doubtless, Mr. Bodkin's blunder. *</p>
        <p>The accident to Mr. John Redmond, M.P., as the result of which he will not be able to attend to any political business for the next couple of weeks, occurred near his country residence in Aughavanagh Mountain. The Nationalist leader resides in the famous Aughavanagh shooting-lodge in county Wicklow, which formerly belonged to Mr. Parnell. It was originally a military barracks, and was built in 1798, so as to cope with the rebellion which broke out in that year in the south-eastern districts of Ireland. * * _ _</p>
        <p>To obtain a new lease of life one should wear a loose collar. Such, apparently, is the opinion of Dr. Walter G-. Walford, who writing in the " British Medical Journal," answers a correspondent's inquiry as to the cause of his hair changing colour. "The probable cause," he states, "is that the inquirer has not very long since enlarged his neckwear,and thus improved the communica- tion between is heart and brain and the lymphatic circulation. This is what I happened to do," the doctor continues, "when I had just reached seventy, when, in addition to greatly improved health, my hair not only became darker, but grew so profusely where I had previously been bald, as to become quite noticeable." Now would-be veterans know what to do. it-</p>
        <p>Mr. Alfred Tennyson Dickens whose death at New York is recorded this week, was the eldest surviving son of the novelist, and Mr. Henry Fielding Dickens, K.C., is now his only brother still living. He was named, of course, after the poet, in conformity with his father's fancy for giving his sons names that he had learned to love in literature. Thus, while the first be named after himself, others were called Walter Landor, Francis Jeffrey, Sydney Smith, Edward Bulwer Lytton, and Henry Fielding. In the case of the latter, by the way, it has been said that Dickens con- templated calling him after Oliver Goldsmith at first, but afterwards changed the name to Henry Fielding, " in a kind of homage to the style of work he was now so bent on beginning."</p>
        <p>* * Mr. A. T. Dickens was the first of the family to emigrate to Australia, where he was joined later by the youngest son, Mr. Edward Bulwer Lytton Dickens. The novelist's pathetic farewell letter to the latter is printed in John Forster's Life. Both brothers were in partnership for some years as stock and station agents, with offices in Sydney, Melbourne, and Wilcannia. Mr. E. B. L. Dickens was returned by Wilcannia, the centre of an extensive pastoral district, to the Parliament of New South Wales, and held the seat for six years, when he was defeated by a Labour candidate. He died in Sydney at the age of fifty-one. Mr. A. T. Dickens lectured at Warrington as recently as in February last, on his father's life and works, his visit to the town having been arranged by the Philomathic Society.</p>
        <p>The past year has been a remarkable one in the annals of our railways not only for strikes but for new developments in engines. Nearly all our big railway companies,accord- ing to the supplement of the "Manchester Guardian," have been building new types of engines and perfecting the old types. The chief feature, says our contemporary, is that considerable strides have been made in the adoption of superheating, which in the case of several of the great lines now appears to have passed the experimental stage. Among the most powerful engines that are now being built are the 4-4-2 express passenger loco motives of the Worth-Eastern Railway, the Great Central's 2-8-0 locomotives for mineral traffic, and the London and North- Western 4-4-0 express passenger engines, the first of which was called "Coronation," * *</p>
        <p>Although no building of first-rate interest has been removed from London during the year that has just closed (says the London correspondent of,the "Manchester Guardian") several very notable buildings have been added. The main interest centres in the Hampstead Garden Suburb, which by extraordinary good fortune has attracted some of our most gifted architects, so that in buildings as well as in planning it is by itself as one of the few considered efforts of our time expressed on a good scale. Undoubtedly it will stand before posterity for us and our ideals when our grandiose commercial buildings in London have gone down before new business exigencies, just as the work of Nash and his contemporaries is going to-day. And it is appropriate that the Garden Suburb should represent the architecture of our time, for since the Renaissance the main achievement of English architecture has lain in its domestic work.</p>
