      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>Mr. Crosfield has wired us from Cannes the following New Year's greeting to friends in Warrington : Best greeting to all friends in Warrington. May New Year bring comfort to thcs who sorrow, and added blessings to those who are happy.— CROSFIELD. * * * We wonder whether the members of the Warrington Rural Council are seeking great- ness. At their meeting last Wednesday a resolution was passed petitioning the Lord Lieutenant of the County to appoint more magistrates for the Lancashire district around Warrington, as it was averred there was a dearth of magistrates. * * * Judging from the numbers who avail themselves of it, the Warrington Old Folk's Treat becomes a more indispensable feature of the local New Year festivities with every succeeding year. On Thursday over 1,100 old people were entertained and right well did they enjoy the bounteous feast provided. The Mayor saw in the large array of guests an argument for the healthiness o smoke,but unkind critics would no doubt be inclined to say that the old people possessed such uncommonly good constitutions they had survived to such a ripe age in spite of the smoke evil.</p>
        <p>An Act of Parliament long wanted came into force on New Year's Day—the Protec- tion of Animals Act to wit. It is now an offence cruelly to beat, kick, ill-treat, over- ride, over-drive, over-load, torture, infuriate, or terrify, or being the owner to cause or permit any of these things to be done in regard to any domestic or captive animal of any kind whatever. The maximum penalty is increased from £5 to £25, and any alter- native or additional imprisonment may be six months' hard labour. The act is due to the energy and initiative of Mr. George Greenwood, M.P., to whom the warm thanks of all lovers of animals are due.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS. ..</p>
        <p>Town Council. The mthly meetino o! the Counail wi I be held in the Council Cnamber, Town Hall, oa Tuesday next h t 6 p m.</p>
        <p>ANTII RAC ITE STOVES, Ecomonical, cleao, cheerful Price from 37/6.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to .:.5, Bridge st. Warrington.—Adct.</p>
        <p>Scenic Railway in the Market Place. Collins' Scenic Railway will occupy the market place in Queen-street for a short seamn, commencing on Friday next. This unique attraction comes direct from the Festival of Empire, London. Patrons enjoy a most exhilarating ride in luxuriously-appointed cars through mountain scenery. The great Niagara Falls are passed on the journey. Pantomime at the Palace. • -</p>
        <p>, , Pantomime will hold sway at the Palace and Hippodrome next week when Messrs. M. Montgomery, and E. C. Jazon will present Jazods grand comic pantomime '' Cinderella." Tlere will be a strong company, and an augnymted orchestra aid chorus, under the direction of A. T. Maclnnes. The perform- ance will be given twice nightly and there will will be a special matinee on Thursday, at 2 o'clock. The Pleturedrome. An excellent series of pictures of the Delhi Durbar has been shown this week and large audiences havebeen the rule. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday next, " The Queen's Necklace," a romantic historical subj-ct of great interest will be presented, and on Thursday, Friday and Saturday, the chief picture will 1)3 " The Mate of the ' John M.' " a photo play of the sea.</p>
        <p>CUTLERY, Rodgers celebrated knives and forks, &amp;3 —Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge st. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Co-operative Concert. • Another highly successful concert promoted by the Education Committee of the Wars ingtqa Cii-operative Socie'y was held on Thurs:lay evening, when a large audience enjoyed a splendi I programme, provided chiefly by the Warrington Apoili, 'Male Vein° Choir. Under the conductorship of Mr. S. Hassall, finished interpretations were given of " The fishermen," " Spartan herces," " Crossing the plains," "To Celia" and "The long day closes." S los were very pleasingly sung by Miss E. Parr (St. Helens), Mr. Arthur Greenough and Mr. Ernest Walker. The humorist was Mr. W. Milne (St. Helens), who was eminently hopey in his efforts to amuse. Messrs. J. Foster and H. J. Iddon were the accom- panists. Mr. C. H. Hibbert presided. Treat to Poor Children.</p>
        <p>At 430 on Saturday about 520 very paor children were entertained to a Christmas tree and treat in St. Clement's Mission, Bank-street. A large proportion of the youngsters were fairly well dressed, but their cases being known, no one could say that they were not poor and deserving The tea served ti them cons)sted of sindwiches, bread and butter, cakes and jam, all of them of the very best quality. It would have surprised a stranger to see how much bread and but or they could demolish. During the evening each child was given apples, oranges, sweets and Christmas cards. A musical programme was gone through, the children contributing to it very largely. It was a treat for the workers to see their happy faces at the tables, and with total delight they sang their well known songs. Before going home every child was given a suitable toy, and in a good number of cases clothing was distributed to the children in need of garments —Mr. Firth wishes to thank all who con- tributed in any way to the fund for providing the little ones wita such a good time. They will not soon forget it. OSRAM "Awn TANTALUM "LAMPS.—Agent. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. J.S Latchford Tradesman's Bankruptcy. At ihe Warrington .Bwnkruptcy Court, on Friday, before Mr. Registrar Ridgway, William Boles, 45, Wash-lane, Latchford, appeared for hi s examination ,in bankruptcy.—Mr. W. J. Forshaw appeared for the , debtor, who stated that be commenced business as a general dealer in the Warrington Market Hall about eighteen years ago with .47.25 in cash capital. In August, 1009, he was induced by the Rev. J. Howard, a retired Wesleyan minister,. to start business as a furniture remover at Birmingham. He represented that it would be a good thing for him, and money was advanced for the purchase of the things required for the business. Debtor agreed- to pay Mr. Howard half of the profits. The business was net successful, and in November, 1909, be gave it up Subsequently Mr. Howard sued him for .t.t lIS at the Manchester Assizes. He consented to judgment for 3.2100, which he thought. included costs, but with the costs it amounted to £ll5 153. sd. On the 13th November Mr. Howard levied an execution for the hal Inca owing, and he thereupon filed his petition. His unsecured liabilities amounted to £257 15s. 5d., and the assets ..-t8 Sc., leaving a deficiency of .-C219 10s. sl.—The examina- tion was closed.</p>
        <p>FIRE GRAM. It you want a new grcite our larg and varied stook will interest you.—J. S. GARLAND Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Helsby Produce Merchant's Affairs. The hearing of the bankruptcy proceedings re..pect- leg Mr. F.Pennington, a produce merchant, of Helsby, was resumed at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court yesterday, before Mr. Registrar Ridgway. The Official Receiver, Mr. Grant Gibson, intimated that it was not his intention to complete the examination that day. He was going to ask the court to make an order for the debtor to file a statement in relation to all goods purchased and sold since July let, 1911 to the day of the receiving order, a period of about three months. The list to disclose the names of the pur- chasers, a description of the goods, and grosi and net prices of the goods sold. The deficiency was a large one, and amounted to .C.:2,312. A number of creditors were of the opinion that the debt ir had not fully disclosed the disposition of the goods. Mr. H. J. Longton, solicitor, objected—to the application and said that the particulars asked for had already been furnished. A great many of the cash trans- actions with farmers were not rrc n.ded in the books. He thought it would be unreasonable for the bankrupt to be put to this tremendous task. The order was granted and the examination was adjourned until March let.</p>
        <p>./Y./•,..•••/N.!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>Claim for Rent. The hearing was commenced at the Warrington County Court on Thursday before Judge Stanger, K.C. of the claim of Miss Annie Felicia Sutton, professional vocalist of Warrington against John Morton, of Friars Green House for £3 4s. 2d. the portion of rent and rates alleged to be owing by him for rooms let at Trinity Chambers. Mr. A. Browne appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. 11. J. Longtcn kr the defendant. The case for the plaintiff as stated by Mr. Browne was that a suit of rooms were taken by the plaintiff at the Market Gate Chambers, from Mr. J.T. Lee, draper at a yearly rent, subject to a month's notice fr m any time. Miss Sutton was asked by the defendant in April 1909 if she would let him have one room. She agreed to do so at £lO per year and each to ply their portion of the rates and taxev. Defendant was told by Miss Sutton that she was subject to a month's notice at any time in view of any objecti,.n being raised by the other tenants on account of the singing. Permission was given by Mr. Lee to sub-let the rooms. The greatest possible difficulty had been experienced in getting any money from defendant. Mr. Lee required the rooms, and substquently notice was given to clear out by December Ist. Defendant did not go until six days later. Mr. Lee inf,nned him he could remain until he found suitable rooms. There was no cessation of business. Miss Sutton corroborated his statement, but in reply to Mr. Longten admitted that she told Mr. Morton that the next would he £lO per year nayable quarterly. The hearing was adjuurued until January 19th.</p>
        <p>VACUUM CLEANERS free the home from dust. Price 2to 7 guineas. Try one on hire.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, to 55, Bridge et, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. J. F. KIERNAN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. J. F. KIERNAN.</p>
        <p>Many Warringtonians will deeply regret to hear of the death of Mr. John Frecierick Kiernan, who passed away at his residence, Woodhank, Sandiway, near Northwich, on Monday night. Tee deceased gentle- man, who was only 30 years of age, was the younger son of the late Mr. Kiernan, for many years owner and licensee of the Patten Arms Hotel, Warrington. He was a native of Warrington, and spent the greater part of his life in this town, taking a great interest in minket and other sports. He received his education in Germany, and afterwards began business as a cotton manufacturer and merchant in Manchester, his com- mercial activities in this and other directions being ear an erensive scale. He was a partner in the firm of Dowdali's, Commercial-road, Liverp aol, who trade as the " Butter Pat Company." In 1905 be married Miss Emile Sybil Thompson, eldest daughter of Mr. Alfred Thumps n, J.P., salt proprietor, cf Merlewood. cuddington. He was of a quiet and retiting dis Position, but did an enormous amount of good without show or estentation, and many poor people will be all the poorer for his death. Mr. Kiernan was a member of the Boman Catholic Chuich, and was a liberal supporter of St. Wilfi id's, .Northwich. &amp;rice taking up his residence at Sandiway, about two years ago, he evinced an interest in hunting, and was often to be seen in the saddle at the, meets of the Cheshire pack. Mr. Kiernan's death was pathetic. It was his birthday on the day before Christmas Day, and a family pirty asaembled at W,odbank, Mr. Kiernan himself being the life and soul of the gather- ing. Later in the evening he was taken ill and was attended by Dr. Gough. Appendicitis supervened, and an operation was performed by Dr. Tell .rd, of Manchester. From this he virtually recovered but further complications ensued, from which death resulted on New Year's D iy. The deceased gentleman leaves a widow, a little son, and a daughter and to them, and to the relatives generally,the deepest sympathy is extended in the great bereavement which has befallen them.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL. 1 The Indy, which was enclosed in an oak casket, was conveyed to St. Wilfrid's Church, Northwich, on Wednesday evening. The coffin reposed on a catafalque surrounded by a large number of beawiful vvreatos, practically all of which were composed of white tl ,wers On the casket was a magnificently constructed floral cross, almost the length of the coffin, made entirely of white carnations, arum lilies, and lilies of the valley, and to which was attached a card bearing the simple inscription, " From his loving and devoted wife." As is customary, all through the night lighted tapers illuminated the darkness. On the Wednesday evening the Psalms " De Profundis " and " Miserere," together with the other prayers for the dead, were recited by the Rev. Father Cregan, and the choir sang a dirge. On Thursday morning the Rev. Father Cregan cedebrated a Requiem Mass, the usual prayers for the repose of the soul beiog offered. The first pa, tof the funeral service was held in the aftern ion at St. Willi id's Church and frora thence the remains were conveyed to Witton Cemetery. The chief mourners were Mr Kiernan, brot'icr ; Mr Alfred J Thompson, J.P., rather- in-law ; Mr H Thompson and Mr S Thompsoo,brothers- in-law ; Mr Finuiga,,, cousin ; itlet.sre J w Thompson (Freshflelds , G Finley, Picklrs, T porter (Whit- church), George Horner, Buckley Taylor, and J I Frankenberg. In addition to the mourners thee were private carriages' and motor-cars sent Iy lkirJ I Frankenberg (Elefferston Grange), Mr Backley Taylor (Gorstrige), Mr George Horner, sfr J S why, Mr Noel Smith (Dunham Lawn), and Miss Horner. Amongst those who met at the church were Messrs Ingram Thompson Henry Ingram ionopson, Major E Clarke (Cud 'll dington), Messrs T Eels: wood, T Tomlinson, J J DIXOD, W Miles Brnt', J Armstrong (Liverpooll, and P Flannery. The Rev Father Cregan, accompanied by an acolyi,e, read the last rites at the grave side.</p>
        <p>_ Many beautiful Glral tributes were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>TRADE'S EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>TRADE'S EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>Interesting Display at th 3 Empire Rink.</p>
        <p>The en'erprise which is the keynote of modern business life is strikingly exemplified this week at the Empire Hall Skating Rink, Museum-street, where a trades and foods exhibition :is in progress. Numerous well-known firms have adopted this pleasing means of bringing before the notice of the public articles of daily use and practically demonstrating the advantages of using up-to-date food preparations, domestic and household articles and appliances. Each day the ' exhibition has been visited by large crowds and, as it remains open until Friday next, there is still ample opportunity for everyone to patronize its many, and varied attractions. During the afternoon admission is free, but in the even- ing a charge of one penny is made. The spacious, well-lighted rink lends itself most admir- ably to a display of this character and the various stalls are arranged to the best advantage. There are some very entertaining side shows, including the "ball of laughter" and Professor Jennion's Canadian electric marionettes. A palmist is in attendance to " read the hands " of vis.tors and a novel exhibit is that of the silk manufacturer at .work producing beautifully finished handkerchiefs, which may be purchased direct from the loom. In the centre of the hall is a fairy fountain which, when illuminated,' presents gorgeous colour effects, the spectacle calling forth general admiration. Mons. Rigbe's orchestra lays choice selections of light music from 2 to 5 and 6.30 to 10. Of special interest to housewives are the cookery demonstrations given daily by Miss Smallwood, a first class diploma holder of the N U.S.C. The latest " paper bag" methods are employed and any s• ecial dish will be prepared by request. A special lecture on sweet-meat making Ivill be given on Friday next at 2.45 p.m. A particularly striking display is seen at the stand of Price's Patent Candle Co., Limited, who show a splendid collection of their specialities in toilet soaps, &amp;c. Statuettes of the the King and Queen in stearine at once direct attention to the exhibit and there are artistically band-painted candles, hultable for decora- tion purposes. Our townsmen, Messrs. C. H. and .T. Dawson (late Wadsworth's), Sankey-street, have a fine exhibition of the leading makes of piano, which are cffered at special terms. The models on view include Broadwoods, Chappells, Collards, Hopkins In's, Iboch, and Samea. A baby " grand " by Waldbng, pro- ducing a particularly rich, deep tone, arouses much interest.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Pendlebury, house furnish ers (near Central Station), show their entermise by a very effectively- arranged display of high-class familia:l-a. A special feature is also made of anthracite stoves which burn all day witboutattention and are invaluable for private or public rooms. They are also most artistic in appf arancs. The " Mitletoe " drawing-room stove is particularly worthy of close inspntioa. • _ Death of Mr. William Benton.</p>
        <p>Much regi et has been cccasioned in the Bank Quay district by the death which took place last night week after a short illness, of Mr William Bent-m, of 218, Imvely-lane. Mr. Benton,whl was 55 years of age, was a mill roller at Dallam Forge. He took a great interest in bowls and was a member of the Bull's Head Bowling Club. His unexpected end haa caused great sorrow to a large circle of friends, many of whom aitended to pay a last tribute of respect at the funeral which took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
