      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PERFORMANCES.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>Photo, Tong(' MISS GLADYS ADAMS ("Mrs. Ericson ").</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Dramatic Society will make their annual bow to the public at the Royal Court Theatre on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday next,w hen ihey will present Mr. H. V. Esmond's highly successful no medy in three acts " When we were twenty-one." For many years the society has done an excellent work on bEhalf of local charities and the proceeds of next week's performlncos will be hdnded over to the Infirmary Million Penny Fund and the District Nursing Association. There is every promise that the enterprise will be fully in keeping with the high traditions of the society. The company, which is an exceptionally strong one, is headed by Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., under whose direction the comedy is being produced. The complete cast is as follows : Richard Carew° Ur. Harold Smith, M.P' Sir Horhce Plnmels, Bart , (commonly called Waddles) Mr. Sidney Wellhead Colonel Miles Grahame (the SJldier Man) Terrence McGrath (the D )ctor) M,r. H. M. Ashton Richard Terrence Miles Audeine (The</p>
        <p>tOtn, B;111C3.</p>
        <p>MISS DOROTHY BROWNE ('' Phyllis ").</p>
        <p>Inv) Mr. F. W. Richmond. Herbert Corrie Mr. Walter Young Mrs. Ericson ••• Miss Gladys Adams Phyllis (her daughter) Miss Dorothy Browne The c.medy will be preceded each evening by Mr Arthur Morrison's one-act play. 'That Brute Simmons," and the characters will be sustained by Mr. Geoffrey Dodd as Thomas Simmons, Mr, H. G. Mosley as Bob Ford, and by Miss Bessie Foishaw as Mrs. Simmons. Mr. Charles It Wood will act as stage 'manager. There will be special scenery and effects. A plan of the reserved seats may be seen at the theatre box office, where seats may be b. oked daily from 10 a.m. to 3 p.m., and each evening after 7.31 during the performance, while the tickets for the unreserved seats may be had Item the usual places in town.</p>
        <p>The President of the society, which was founded in the year 1562, is Mr. IV. Peter Itylands ; the hon. treasurer, Mr. Arthur Browne ; aid the hon. secretary Mr. J. W. Men.</p>
        <p>partridge fell tribute to the skill with which he handled his gua on the Cumberland Moors where he has shot over for the last thirty years. As a raconteur he had few equals; his stories were usually of incidents of his own life which he made wonderfully vivid with word and gesture, weaving into them a number of connected incidents which made his friends often wait in amusemeLt to see if he would ever be able to peck up his main thread again. His business naturally led him in the direction of art and he was a connoisseur of exceptional ability. He was intimate with George Sheffield, Warringt n Wood, and other ' local men of talent, and his kindly encouragement to rising artists is gratefully remembered. His interest in art naturally increased when his second son Alfred Payne Garnett, adopted it as a profassion. The unexpected death of his sin two years ago affected Mr. Garnett very deeply and the break up of his health seemed to begin with this sorrow. During these two years the shadow Ity heavy on him, and a slight paralytic seizure in the summer gave warning of the end which has come so rapidly. With his usual energy and strong %ill he was up and about again as soon as possible, and had regained much of his usual vigour, bat a second seizure six weeks ago rendered him Inhpless and he gradually became weaker, until Thursday evening, when Ls quietly passed away. He leaves a widow, two smis and three daughters to cherish his memory and emulate a character of sterling worth.</p>
        <p>The cortege will leave 2S, Westcliffe-roal, Birkdale at 1.45 to-day (Saturday) and the interment will take plo.oe at Birkdale Cemetery at 2 30.</p>
        <p>„ • 4' ' Z`Wiks ; itttal4,44it,' 1 4 ; I I * • tf • - • - +g, is3,B”</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DEATH OF M. DAVID GARNETT, J.P.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF M. DAVID GARNETT, J.P.</p>
        <p>The passing away of one of our townsmen, Mr. David Clarnett, whose death occurred at his residence, Birkdale, Southport, at six o'cl :ck on Thursday even- ing, will fill the heart 4 of many of our readers with a deep sense of loss. He was born and lived at Penketh the greater part of his life ad his removal to Southport some twenty years ago occasioned great regret in his native village. Mr. David Garnett was planer with his late brother, Robert, in the business of Robert Garnett and Sons, and although of late he had no direct financial interest in the business, he has con- tinued until quite recently to take a keen interest iu its affairs, and has been a familiar figure in and about the firm's premises and Sankey-street for at least forty years. He bad not many interests outside his business and never entered into municipal, and rarely into political life. Of late years he took a peE.simistic view of the development and activity of labour com- binations; this was much on his mind and frequently came up in cc nversatioi. His treatment of individual workmen was always kindly and sympathetic, but he fancied he saw in the combination of working men, grave social and economic dangers. Early in lire Mr. Garnett associated himself with the temperanca movement and it was to an explosion of gas used fore ternperance lanter lecture that be owed his partial deafress. He maintained his temperance principles to the end and as years advanced he seemed to be more and more impressed with the Let that strong drink often insidiously weakened the</p>
