      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>LATE MR. DAVID GARNETT.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. DAVID GARNETT.</p>
        <p>will and character, bad an injurious effect upon every organ of the body, and paralysed the brain. Of careful habits himself, he tiled to impress everybody with the dtsirability of saving money for rainy days, and he acted as treasurer and trus'ee for the Penketh. Penny Savings Bank for nearly fifty years. This B ink, which collected during its existetice about £50,000, all deposited in small sums, served a good purpose and has latdy been transferred to the branch of the Union Bank of Van- ' cheater which has been formed in the Penketh village. I With the rest of his family his loyal life association I was with " the people called Methodist.," first iu the little chapel of his native village and afterwards at Trinity Wesleyan Church, Southport. Although his outside interests ‘tere few lie entered with avidity into those which claimed him. Perhaps music was his chief delight. For many years while resident in Penketh he both played the organ and superintended the choir of the Wesleyan Chapel, aid the appreciation of good music which was characteristic of his native village was almost entirely due to him. Many rare musical trests were afforded to the village people through his intimacy with a-tistes of ability, and he helped with generosity such as had talent without means for its development. In the formation and sustenance of the Wariington Musical Society he took an active part when Doctor Henry Hiks was conductor. During his residence in South- port he hits contitin d his interest in the development of music both publicly and privately. He was a keen sportsman and many a grcuse and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>BURNING FATALITIES.</head>
        <p>BURNING FATALITIES.</p>
        <p>No Fireguard.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. B. ighouse, the coroner, conducted two it:lomat, at the Police Stati 3D, yesterday afternoon, relative to the deaths of two young children, v‘ho died in the Infirmary from severe burns. In tie case of Mary Bailey, the eighteen months o'd daughter of Rose and Richard Bailey, of Carat Cct ages, Canal Side, Grappenhall, it was stated by the mother that on Tuesday morning list she placed the deceased, along with an )ther child slimed Thomas aged two years and eight months, on the rug in front of the fire in the kitchen whilst she went about her work upstaias. She gave them some rags tied up as a doll to play with. Whilst, upstairs she detected a smell of something burning end en comirg downstairs she saw the deceased with her clothing on fire. She pit out the flames and the chill was conveyed to the Infirmary whore she died the 681119 Porzious of burnt rag were found on the hearth- rug. She was of the opinion that the boy placed the rag doll in the fire and then threw it en the rug,setting fire to the deceased's clothing. In teply to the Coroner, the mother admitted that al e hat no fireguard, but she had purchssel one since the accident.</p>
        <p>Mr. l'rigbouse : It is not in my province to order a Prosecution. I don't know what the police will do. Toe only thing I can say is that if it is not brought before the magistrates it ought to be. Sergeant Barlow (of the Cheshire County Force) said he attended the inquest to report the circumstances to his superintendent. A verdict of " Accidental desth " was returned, the jury associating themselvts with the remarks of the Coroner. The second inquiry was into the circumstances of the death of Harry Cotterill, aged 0 years. of 103, Pierpoint- - street, who died in the Infirrn,ry on Wednesday. It appears that on December 29th the mother put the deceased to bed along with two other children. She slep% in the front room downstairs. About half-past one in the morning she heard screams from upstairs and on going into the room saw the deceased with his shirt on lire. Her son Frank, aged seven years, told her that they got some wood and coal from downstairs and lit the fire. In order to make it burn quickly the deceased stood in front with his shirt before the grate. The draught drew his shirt iuto the fire and set it alight. The flames were extinguished by Frank with a blanket and pillow. In reply to tbe Coroner the mother said they must have come downstairs whilst she wAs asleep. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>TRADESMAN'S BANKRUPTCY.</head>
        <p>TRADESMAN'S BANKRUPTCY.</p>
        <p>I At the Warrington Bankruptcy on Friday Arthur Cl..ric, 30 Market-street, Earlestovrn carrying on business as a house furnisher and stationer appeared for his public examinstions The liabilities amounted to £799 13s. 9d. and the assets were estimated to produce £157 4s. 3d. leaving a deficiency of £640 9s. ad. Debtor attributed his faqure to heavy business expenses in p-oporaon to proft:s, interest on loans, law costs, lack of capital, and expenses of the illness of his late son. Mr. Gil son ths (Anis' raceiver eli&amp;ted the fact that the assets were iiFely to produze £7O more than the debtor had estimated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>16, 18 and 20, Sankey Street,</p>
        <p>Today and throughout January.</p>
