      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DOMESTIC GIRL'S SAD RECORD.</head>
        <p>DOMESTIC GIRL'S SAD RECORD.</p>
        <p>At the Cheshire Quarter Sessions on Monday, Minnie Birkenhead, alias Minnie Millington (19). a domestic servant, pleaded guilty to stealing a lady's coat and bicycle, the property of Harriet Millington, at Dutton, on October 14th. Superintendent Ennlon said the prisoner first carve before the court in 1902, at Runcorn, when she was bound over on a charge of housebreaking. Since then she had been several times charged with theft, and had been in four different homes. Miss Wright, Chester Rescue Home, said kindness was thrown away on prisoner. The Chairman said prisoner had had every possible advice, but took no heed of the advice or chances given. Her sentence was eighteen months' hard labour. TICKET COLLECTOR CHARGED WITH EMBEZZLEMENT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday,before Mr. Charles White (In the chair) and other justices, Uilliam Arthur Bradshaw, a .ticket collector at the Central Station. residing at 1, Godfrey-street, was summoned for embezzling three sums of money while in the employ of the Cheshire Lines Railway Committee. Mr. Pinfold, solicitor (Liverpool), prosecuted and Mr T S Steel defended atd pleaded not guilty. Mr. Pinfold said it was part of the defendant's duty to receive excess fares from passengers and to account for the same. The duty appeared to have been only partially done. From information which came to the knowledge of the Company they decided that tests should be made as to the defendant's honesty. On November 18th be received Is. for two excess fares from Widnes and a further sixpence on December Bth. An examination of the books showed a considerable discrepancy between the excess fares received by Bradshaw and the other collectors who worked with him. When charged with the offence defendant replied, " I have never in any shape or form kept them myself. All the excess fares I got I paid in." Mrs. Norah Webb, wife of Inspector Webb, of Liverpool, 62, Adelaide-road, Liverpool, said that on November 18th, she travelled from Widnes to Warring- ton in company with her sister. They had DO tickets and on arrival at Warrington she paid one shilling for the two excess fares. No receipt was given. In reply to Mr. Steel witness said she did not ask the collector for a receipt. She gave him time to make one cut. She was told not actually to ask for a eceipt. Witness further stated that on December 9th, she travelled from the same station. On that occasion no receipt was given. Miss Knight, a sister of the last witness, corroborated. John Lynch, 13, Morpeth-street, Liverpool, said on December Bth he travelled by the 2 43 train from Widnes. He paid the excess fare to the defendant who gave no receipt. Mr Joseph Davies, chief booking clerk at the, Central Station, produced the books of the company which showed that the defendant had not entered the excess fares on the date mentioned. By Mr. Steel : Don't you know there has been an egitation at the Central station for additional collectors ?—That does not come under my notice. Do you know it is is practice for collectors to forget in the hurry to make out a receipt for excess fares ? I don't know,a collector has time to enter up the excess fares after every train. Mr. Steel, for the defence, pointed out that the defendant had been a servant of the company for some- thing like twelve years. Be earned promotions at the St. James' Station, Liverpool. On the evid- ence he contended there was no felonious intent on the part of the defendant to defraud his employers. He would tell them that in order to avoid any confusion of money he had a habit of putting the monies in his right hand pocket. It was quite probable that the young man among the multifarious duties be had to perform had no time to enter up the fares. It was also probable that the money might have got into the wrong pocket and that be forgot to enter it up. There might be neglect of duty or insufficient care, but there was no intention to defraud the railway. The system at the booking office of entering up the excess fares was not in itself complete. It was admitted that an excess fare collected on the 17th of November was not entered until the day following which plainly showed that mistakes did occur. He pleaded for leniency in view of the defendant's previous good character,</p>
        <p>, The defendant in evidence said so far as his memory served him, be bad taken no money from the company f for his own nEe. tie had not stolen any mom- y inten- ff tiEnally. Sernetimcs it happened that be forgot to % Enter the excess fares, but when he found the money ‘Lissun COMPOUND for Coughs Colds, and Bronchial troubles. 9id., is. lid. Of Chemists.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
