      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr J W Hayward (presiding). Alderman 1 innion and Messrs A J Jolley, R T Fairclough and T Pemberton. D -MAC n AT THE POLICE STATION WINDOW.— William Moran, of no settled abode, cvss sent to goal for a month for doing damage to a window in the police buildings to the amount of 10s. Defendant deliberately threw a stone through the window of the inspector's office. CHARGE AGAINST A SERVANT.—Annie Turner, domestic servant, 150 Liverpool-road, was charged with stealing a silver watch, value £2, from 8 Suez-street the property of Beatrice Jane Lees, on December 23rd. Ihe Chief Constable, in asking for a remand, said the defendant was engaged as a domestic servant and recently the complainant had occasion to speak to the defendant and gave her notice. The defendant was then asked if she had seen anything of a brooch which was missing from the premises. Defendant appeared to have gone upstairs and upset everything and then left the place. The watch was afterwards missed and the defendant had shown it over a week ago to someone in town. When she was arrested in Widnes the watch was found in her possession. Defendant was remanded for a week. ALLEGED NEGLECT OF CHILDREN.—W Ml= Fleet- wood, 33, Ashton-street, was cha-ged with neglecting Ore children in a manner likely to cause them unneces- sary suffering and injury to health.—Defendant pleaded not guiltv.—Mr. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., said the defendant had gone through the form of marriage with a Mrs. Wilkinson who had not heard of her husband for six or seven years. On November oth defendant left home and had not been seep since and the childrea had been kept by the Guar- dians.—Defendant said he had been away from home to look for work which he had obtained at Stockton Heath —The case was adjourned for a month. MONDAY.—Before Mr S Wright (in the chair),Alderman Smethurst, Alderman Bolton, Messrs S Burgess, W Furness, G Pilling and W Roberts. FRUIT FRF.E OF CH ARC E.—Joseph Lee, an old man living at ICB, Winwick-road, was chaiged with having stolen a quantity of tomatoes, onions and apples, the property of Mr. Hugh McDermott, 48, Bewsey-street. The Chief Constable said that the prosecutor had a stall in the market, where the prisoner bad for some time been employed by him. His suspicions bad been aroused over a certain matter and he kept the prisoner under observation. Mr. McDermott saw Lee serve his daughter with the property which was the subject of the charge. Mr. AlcDermott questioned him about the money and Lee declared that he had placed Is. 3d. for the apples, &amp;c., in the till. When the money was checked this statement was proved to ba false. Lee then admitted that he had not taken payment for the property. Prisoner pleaded guilty. The Chief Con- stable said be had received a letter from a works manager giving the prisoner an excellent char- acter. Mr. McDermott said he did not wish to press the case. After being warned by the Bench the prisoner was discharged. OFFICER ATTACKED.—Brothers named John Roberts and Griffith Roberts, were charged with having assaulted , Detective Clough.—The prisoner's were taken into custody for begging and became very violent. A young man came to the detective's assistance and was also attacked, prisoners making their eccape. They were arrested at Poynton.—The Chief Constable said the men were two of the most desperate characters with whom they had to deal. They were very violent when arrested at Poynton. John had been 13 times previously con- victed and that was Griffith's twelfth appearance. They were menaces to society wherever they went, roaming about begging from and frightening people. Both men were sant to gaol for three months' hard labour.—The Chief Constable said that the police did not know the name of the young man who assisted the detective, but all the same they desired to tender him their thanks.— The Chairman said that whoever the young man was he was deserving of the warmest praise, not only from the police and the Bench, but from the public generally. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor, Councillor Dr Joseph (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Il Thornton, A J Jolley. G Pilling, W Furness and T Locker. HABITUAL DRUNKARD.—WaIter Brown, 124, Winwick- road, was summoned by his wife Ellen Brown for being an habitual drunkard, and she applied for a separation order.—Compleicant stated that during the Cnristroas holidays her husband was frequently drunk and threatened to assault her. On Friday night she was siting in the kitchen when he came home the worse for drink and caught hold of her by the shoulders and put her outside the house. He was drunk almost overy night.—Defendant banded the magistrates a reference from his employer which stated that he was a steady and attentive workman. Brown complained that his wife was addicted to taking whisky, snuff and ale. The Mayor said the charge of drunkenness had not teen Proved, and the application would be dismissed. It would be better if they could mutually agree to separate. The defendant promised to pay his wife ss. weekly if she would keep away from him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. PAROCHIAL TEA.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. PAROCHIAL TEA.</p>
        <p>The annual parochial tea in connection with the Parish Church was held on Thursday evening in the Fairfield Schools and proved one of the most enjoyable functions on record. About 150 people sat down to tea, served in one of the large classrooms which was tastefully decorated for the occasion. Admirable arrangements bad been made by the churchwardens (Messrs. J. G. Craik and B. Isherwood). assisted by the church officers, for the entertainment of the congreea- tion. A concert, whist drive and dance were in pro- gress simultaneously. The concert, under the direction of Mr. F. M. Darby (organist) was of a high-class character and afforded great pleasure. Songs were rendered by Mrs Atkin, and Mr T Humphreys, and a trio by Mrs Atkin, Miss Fairclough and Miss M White. An instrumental trio by Messrs. S. Crails, A. Shaw, W. Brandreth, was skilfully given and very any reziatively ireoeived. A dialogue bj t' e Padeate Amateur Dramatic S c e'y entitled " The .£5OO Prize " created much merriment.. Two sittings in connection with the whist drive were arranged, Mr. G.Brown acting RS M.C. The following wire the successful prize winners :—Ladies, Miss Wright, Mrs Noon, Miss Pierpoint, Miss Haintith, Miss Leigh ; Gentlemen, Mr Baldwin, Master Baldwin and Mr J T Whittle. The dancing was in charge of Mr It W Antwiss and the music was supplied by Mr. G. Underwood's band. The Rector during an interval wished those present " A Happy New Year" and congratulated them on having such a large party. Referring to the day schools he said it was a serious matter that there was no religious instruction given in the Secondary schools. Proceeding to give some illustrations of the ignorance of some of the scholars on religious matters, be said the conduct of boys and girls in the streets and parks had been remarked upon and showed the importance of real religious instruc- tion. The principle of religions instruction bad been acknowledged in the elementary schools and he failed to see why it should not be extended to the Secondary School. They must remember that the schools belonged to all ratepayers, whether Churchmen or Noncontormists ; the parents must, however, take the initiative to secure the adoption of the principle for which Churchmen contended. They should see to the election of really good men on the Council ; not simply popular men, but real good and earnest men who would do their utmost to secure religious instruc- tion. Another useful means to adopt was a class of pupils in religious instruction, out of school hours. Several clergy in the town were prepared to take the pupils. Only undenominational religious instruction was given in the Council elementary schools and parents had the right to see that the teaching of the Church should be given to Church children. They did not wish to impose it upon others. It was also the duty of parents to send their children to Sunday school unless ,they were prerared to instruct them themselves. Alluding to their own own parochial matters, he said the population had grown to over 19,000. They would have to consider before long the building of a third mission chapel in the neighbourhood of Orford B irracks. They had secured a site and they hoped it would be as flourishing as the other two missions of St. George's and St. Clement 's. They were also applying for a faculty and when it had been obtained they would begin at once to build an ew vestry at the Parish Church. The accom- modation at present was very poor. The new vestry would be double the size of the present one, and the cost was estimated at LAO. Of that sum £3OO had already been raised. Dealing with the wider outlook of the Church in Wales, the Rector said a Bill was shortly to be introduced in Parliament for the Disco- dowment of the Church in Wales. He would like to remind them that the Church in England and Wales was one. The advantages of Establishment were : (I) National recognition of religion; (2) the right of all to the services of the clergy ; (3) the voice of the Bishops in Parliament, which was always given fearlessly ; (4) the Coronation of the Sovereign. None of them would like to see anyone but the Archbishop of Cinterbury crowning the king. Dealing with endowment, he said, the Church money was money given by members of the Church and not by the State. The Church was always the Church of England there never was a Church of Rome in this country uutil 1570. The money was needed and was well used. There never was a time when the Church was more in earnest and the clergy more active than they were at the present time. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL LIBERALISM. SIR WILLIAM LEVER AS NEW LEADER. A development of great interest, and one which may have a far-reaching effect on Liverpool politics, took place on Tuesday afternoon, when, in response to an influential deputation who waited upon him at Thornton Manor, Sir William Lever consented to allow himself to be nominated as president of the Liverpool Federal Council and active leader of the party in the city. The occasion has arisen from the resignations—to take effect in March—of Sir Edward Evans as president and Sir Benjamin Johnson as chairman of the Federal Council. It is understood that Sir Ben Johnson's retirement is in consequence of the calls of business *lnd the claims upon his spare time of the work of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation, of which he is chairman.</p>
        <p>MUNE</p>
        <p>EMI</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
