      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ReLicioa.</head>
        <p>ReLicioa.</p>
        <p>" Untrodden Paths. " The Rev. j. R. Jones, writing to his parishioners in St. Mary's (Great Sankey) Magazine, says :—I heartily bid you one and all a very bright and a happy New Year. If good wishes on my part could avail anything, the New Year (1912) would indeed bring you prosperity No doubt 1912,1ike its predecessors, will gradually unfold to us new, strange, and hitherto untrodden paths. It will bring it own message and its own revelation of God's way of dealing with the 8008 of men. To some, no doubt, it will bring in its way some strange sorrow, to others some new joy, to all I hope, larger views—more faith that, come joy, come sorrow, we are all in the hand of a good Father, Who deals with us as with children. " lie knows best." New Curate for St. Barnabas' Church. The Rev. G. A. Coupe, curate, preached his farewel sermon at All Saints' Church, Great Nelson-street Liverpool on Sunday evening, prior to his departure for St. Barnabas' Church, Warrington. In the course of his remarks he said that during the few years of his connection with that church and parish, he had been encouraged by the unity and co-operation of the various organizations at work for the good of the people. Although their circumstances had been depressing they had kept their heads above the stream. It had been his privilege to come across those who had been brought up under the influence of the Church of England in that parish, and who had spoken of the lasting good they had received. Their work was pioneer and both himself and his wife were sorry to leave them, but they left them with pleasant memories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>' e-SOC/AL</head>
        <p>' e-SOC/AL</p>
        <p>Lancashire Congregationalists. A New Year's Pastoral. A pastoral address for the new year has been issued to the ministers and churches of the Lancashire Congregational Union by the chairman, the Rev. B. Nightingale, and will be read in many churches to-morrow. The churches of the denomination, it is said, in common with all others, have been passing through a serious and somewhat prolonged depression, which has not yet reached its end, although some clear- sighted observers think it is within sight. The main object of the address is to clrrect the idea that such periods in the experience of the Church are necessary and of Divine ordaining. History, writes Mr. Nightingale, shows a succession of religious vicissi- tudes of the nature of rise and fall, but history only records what is, and not what ought to be. l'eriods of depression are to be regarded as lapses and defections from the intended path, which both for individuals and churches is meant to be one of continued pro- gress and growth. The churches have suffered failure to make Use of their spiritual reserves. To Congrega- tionalists in especial these are vital, as they have little else to fall back upon in the way of machinery, organi- zation, or ritual. The message concludes with a refer- ence to the expected observance during the year of the 250th anniversary of the ejection of 1662, "an event which has bad a significance for Congregationalism, for the religious life of the country in general, and indeed for the life of the nation at large not yet appreciated." Watch-Night Services.</p>
