with hope to the future .• the failings of the past The Parish Church Parochial Magazine contains the should enable them to avoid similar failing in the f‘,ut_u_re:, Tile service closed with the singing of. the following :—We are living in critical times. Never in Come let us ." Mr. Bert Weaver presided our lifetime has, I think, a year opened fraught with '-'3Tu at Lhe organ. A collection was made en behalf of the such great issues to Church and State as that which , " National Children's Homes." now lies before ea. %lye have witnessed in the imme- diate past a momentous change in Parliamentary pro- GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHURCH. cedure. A weighty blow has been struck at the Home ! There was a good attendance at the watch-night of Lords, and now we are fa-Asi with three om nous service when the Rev II Ogden preached on the text measures, which must exercise deeply the minds eWe spend our years as a tale that is told " sc. 9. Life of all thoughtful men—Home Rule for Ireland, was brief, and they would soon be face to face with Disestablishment and Disendowment of the Church their God and Judge. Life was, indeed, as a tale that in Wales, and a Bill for the Settlement of was told, brief and absorbing. The answer to the ques- the Education Question in England. Whatever ! tion " Was life worth living ? " was in the negative opinions men may hold—and we are bound to unless there was confidence and faith in the eternal credit everyone with the intention of doing what is , word. At that solemn hour, before the death of the old right and best for the general good—we cannot help , year, let them remember that even though life was brief viewing these proposals with anxiety and fear, knowing God was with them, and that although life was" only a as to the first, the existence of two religious bodies in I tale" it might bee noble One, A member of the choir Ireland diametrically opposed to each other, and as to sang the hymn "Behold Me." the second, the utterly different views, both in England and Wale!' of Churchmen and Nonconformists, and as ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH. to the third, that Churchmen have definitely taken The service, which was largely attended, began with their stand upon the faith of the parent, whereas all the time honoured hymn " 0 God of Bethel by Whose others are either satisfied with undenominational I band," after which the minister led in prayer. Then religious instruction, or would banish religion entirely I followed the hymns "Rock of ages " and 'A few more from the day schools. This being the case, and know- years shall roll," between the singing of wLich Psalm ing the bias of the Government, who can help being xc. was read, and Mr. Russell end Mr. Dean led the anxious I people in prayer. Shortly before midnight the minister Brli Si —lthodist) i !°---a- few - !cords, gave the. motto for the New Year,</p>
        <p>ick A. Mission (Independent Me The annual congregational tea-party and concert in connection with the above Mission was held on Mon- day, when a large number sat town to an excellent tea A concert was afterwards held. The president (Mr. William Barton, sen.), presided, and gave a brief address which was greatly appreciated. The Church Secretary (Mr. S. Gallop) presented a report of the year's work together with the financial statement, which showed a satisfactory balance in hand. The Sunday school secretary (NI r. William Yates) gave the school report which was also very satisfactory. The musical part of the programme was gone through by the following artists :—Songs, Misses E Hewitt, A Harding and H Howell (gipsy dance and songs) ; Mr Peers (bass) ; Mr Booth (tenor) ; Misses Howell and Harding (Japanese dance) ; Mr S Aukland (humorist) ; Miss Eva Shaw was the accompanist: Mr A Palmer gave a violin solo. At the close a dialogue was gone through entitled "Fortune Hunting."</p>
        <p>, " Blessed is the nation whose God is the Lord; the people whom he bath chosen for His own inheritance." Election, he said, was the rock truth on which the Hebrew bad made shipwreck of his national life. " Chosen to serve" was a presentation or the truth well calculated to realize salvation. The singing of the hymn "At Thy feet, our God and Father," followed by the benediction oonoluded the service.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbiterlan Church. On Thursday evening the Rev. William and Mrs Reid gave an " At Home ' at St. John's to the members of the congregation. In spite of the most unfavour- able state of the weather there was a good gathering the guests being received by the minister and Mrs. Reid Mr. Reid in the course of some introductory remarks, raid he hoped that the gathering might be as informal as possible and that those present might mai e specially Each other's acquaintance. Refreshments were handed round by a company of ladies of the congregation. A number of musical items were contributed, commenc- ing with pianoforte sections by Mrs. R. H. Albery- followed by songs and a duet by Mr. and Mrs. R. H. Albery, who render yeoman services to St. John's in connection with the musical part of the church services. Mr. Reid sang " Big Ben," and in response to an enthusiastic encore gave the merry Irish song " Peggy " or " The low-backed car." The hearty applause which greeted Mr. Reid, showed that his singing is appre- ciated at St. John's as much as his preaching, and that is saving a great deal. Mr. A. 0. Leweey, choir leader, and Mrs. Albery, acted as accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>New Pastor of Cairo-street Chapel.</head>
        <p>The New Pastor of Cairo-street Chapel.</p>
        <p>The Bev. Dr. Stanley A. Mellor, the newly-appointed pastor, preached to large congregations at Cairo-street Chapel, on Sunday. Before proceeding with his sermon in the evening the rev, gentleman said that, although he had net yet begun his miniitry in Warrington, in a sense he appeared before the congregation as their accepted minister, and he was profoundly encouraged by the presence in the congregation of so many young people. Taking for his text the words " For behold I create new heavens and a new earth, and the former shall not be remembered nor come into into mind " Isaiah lxv. 17— the preacher said it might not be an easy thing to watch the old being destroyed to make way for the new, and it might not be easy to change i our methods according to the lives of others, but it had to be accomplished if we intended to live at all. To many these cea-eless changes sesmed full of dread, and it was said that "what was good enough for our fathers should be good enough for the sons., ' No one could stay the progress that was going on. Many thought of the sanctity and sacredness of this or that institution, but there was nothing absolutely sacred under the sun. There was nothing which could not be different to-morrow. We did not, perhaps, notice tho changes which were actually going on about us at the moment; it was because we lived in them, and more or less unconsciously adapted ourselves to them, but the fact of our not noticing did not make the change less certain. New heavens and a new earth were being created every day, and the former things were not remembered or brought to mind. To live meant to change, to become something different. The old year which was just passing away left none of us the same as it found us. We were not the same in body, mind, or character; there was, indeed, a sort of mysterious identity all throng); which we called "ourselves,"each recognizing himself as having existed at the beginning of the yearEas well as at the end. New things had come into our lives, old ones had died away. The former things were not remembered nor brought to mind, and when we contemplated the fact of change in nature, society and in our own lives we could not fail to be impressed by the thought of the irretrievable and irreparable character of the past which was changeless. We mew-, said the preacher in conclusion, realize the responsibility laid upon us. On the ruins of the past could rise the future. We were the children of the frame and towards the future we looked and for it we worked. The old died and the new succeeded. It was the law of life. vve must then welcome the new year bravely, realize its responsibili- ties, faithfully discharge its duties, loyally accept its changes and willingly make the sacrifice of the past for the sake of the future.</p>
        <p>`TENASITIN'E,' ti New Liquid Cement for Mending and Making. In tubes Id., 2d., 3d, 6d.—Advt.</p>
