      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>Warrington A.C. had the moderate muster of 18 to follow a trail laid by T 0 Thomas, J Hayes and .1 Battersby for the inter-club race viith West Cheshire At the last minute the latter wired that owing to various circumstances they were unable to fulfil the fixture, so the locals raced for points, and a medal for first novice borne as per fixture lit. Over a five miles course J Parsonage, A Siddall, F Shadwell and T F Turton were always well placed and closely followed by Jas Hands, E Scott and W Thorniley, finished in the order given. Marked improvement was shown by Stanley Holbrook, who fully earned the novice medal with a very creditable eighth position. Notable absentees were C Vose, whose indisposition will keep him off the active list for a few weeks H Holbrook, W Bolton, H K Jepson, I Ditchfield and the brothers W and F Antrobus. Three o'clock is:the time fixed for the start of to-days run from headquarters and if Broughton Harriers can keep the fixture it is probable that W Scott, E Owen and a former loci( favourite, 0 Watmough, may be included in the opposition. Evening runs at 7.45 p.m. on Tuesday and Thursday next.</p>
        <p>R3lands W.R.C. Harriers. Only a small company turned out and a better muster is anticipated this afternoon.</p>
        <p>With ,the championships in a month's time every opportunity should be taken if fitness is to be assured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>"THE STATE INSURANCE ACT."</head>
        <p>"THE STATE INSURANCE ACT."</p>
        <p>This Act, with all its faults, may, nevertheless, be the foundation upon which a great edifice may be con- structed within whose walls hundreds of thousands of sick and diseased people may find a secure asylum. It is clear that this measure cannot stand alone, and that as soon as experience has shown what new steps must be taken, it may he necessary to supplement it by a fresh effort which will deal more directly with the weakest class of the community. It does not render any less pressing the need for direct reform of the Poor Law, but it does prepare the way for such reform.— Percy Alden, M.P., in the January "Contemporary Review."</p>
        <p>THE EXTENSIVE IRRIGATION WORK'S which the Southern Alberta Land Co. have in hand for the development of 400,000 acres of land are rapidly approaching completion. The lands in question are practically in the same district in which the Canadian Pacific Railway has for some years been occupied in adapting upwards of a million acres to close settlement by means of irrigation work. The rcheme has involved the construction of over 200 miles of actual canal, dams, the formation of reservoirs, bridges, syphons, S.c., itimated at a cost of two million dollars. The intake works are on the Bow River, and the main clnal, 50 miles of which is already comdleted, will eventually have a length of 75 miles. The supply of water at all times is secured by creating two large storage reservoirs covering an area of 1,000 and 13,000 acres respectively. The work is Bo far advanced that ths contractors will be able to deliver water over one-half of the Company's land by next midsummer.</p>
      </div>
