      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>POPULAR SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>POPULAR SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>INtaxious CLEANixe Thelma. An implement for cleaning ceilings, re- cently invented, has a funnel-shape body to put up against the ceiling, and in which are provided rotating brushes to which a fluid is delivered to cleanse the ceiling.</p>
        <p>IMPROVEMENT POR CRICKET CLUBS.</p>
        <p>Cricket clubs should welcome an improved form of scorer which has been invented. By means of this, all clubs will be able te adopt the county. method of scoring. Another advantage is that the plates being fixed in position, cannot get lost. They can- not "blow off" in windy weather or get trodden on and made almost undecipherable through being constantly laid on the ground. Hanging as they do, the plates screen one another, and consequently keep</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>IMPROVED SCORER</head>
        <p>IMPROVED SCORER</p>
        <p>clean. Being always in position the board is ready at all times for immediate use with- out the nece,sity of collecting up and sort- ing out numbers as in the old system. The scorer 'has been constructed on very simple lines. It was felt by the inventor that the less the mechanism the cheaper it would be cud the easier to manipulatt. These two points bring it within the reach of every cricket c:ub desirous of affording its teams and its spectators a full knowledge of the state of the game.</p>
        <p>IMPROVED MOTOR-CYCLE.</p>
        <p>In an electric motor-cycle, invented by an American, six cells of storage battery are used to overate a specially designed 12-volt motor, which drives on the rear wheel. The vehicle is said to have a radius of one hun- dred miles per charge on paved streets, and seventy-five miles over country roads. It is provided with a three-speed controller, by which speeds of four, fifteen, and thirty-five miles an hour May be attained. The weight of the complete outfit is 2001 b. The battery is mounted low in the frame. The frame is so made that any form of standard storage battery may be used. The seat and the foot- board are conveniently arranged, and com- fort in motoring is said to be assured to the rider. This motor-cycle has a 51in. wheel base, and the motor is provided with ball bearings and is noiseless. The brake is of the external band type, and the spring fork is cushioned.</p>
        <p>;•la 4 • ..010 _ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>FURNACE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>FURNACE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>A new type of steel furnace in which electric power is applied in combination with the Bessemer process, has been pro- duced by a Sheffield firm. The apparatus consists of a double-ended converter, which is mounted on bearings, and can be tilted through an angle of rather more than ISO degrees. One end is provided with tuyeres for blowing, and at the other end are the electrodes, with cooling jackets of cold air or water. In use the Bessemer part of the furnace, pre-heated in the ordinary way, is charged with molten metal direct from the cupola or blast furnace mixer, and is then brought to a vertical position, when the blast comes into operation automatically, and the process of conversion goes on as in the ordinary Bessemer converter. When this is completed, the furnace is tilted and the metal transferred to that portion which contains the eketrodes, and which has already been heated by the gases, etc., re- sulting from the Bessemer part of the opera- tion. Current is then switched on, and re- fining carried to any desired extent, both arc and resistance heating being obtained in the furnace.</p>
        <p>NEW DELIGHT POD CHAIIPPEIIRS.</p>
        <p>America certainly seems the home of inven- tive genius. A clever device has been thought of by a citizen of this progressive nation. lie has arranged an indestructible phono- graph record in combination with a loud- speaking telephone instrument on his -motor- car. A touch of the chauffeur's button causes this noise-making Mechanism to hurl the name of the car or any other desired phrase at obstructive persons who dare to venture in his path.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>METHOD 08 FELLING TREES.</head>
        <p>METHOD 08 FELLING TREES.</p>
        <p>The sketch shows a much-used method of felling trem which is much le os laborious than hewing them down. This is done by fire. A hole is bored horizontally into the trunk of the tree for about one foot, and another bole bored at an angle to meet the</p>
        <p>FIRING A TREE.</p>
        <p>first one. The next step is to place hot coals at the intersection of .the holes so as to ignite the pitch running from the pores. The draft will cause a rapid combustion. and in a short time the tree will be burned through, causing it to fall towards the burn- ing side.</p>
        <p>' /10 "ht.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>INNOVATION IN SURGERY.</head>
        <p>INNOVATION IN SURGERY.</p>
        <p>A valuable new discovery has been intro- duced into the surgical world. A. French surgeon claims to have found a superior substitute for skin grafting. This opera- tion is a very necessary one, when as a re- sult of some accident the patient has lost a considerable amount of skin. Consequently, the system of hair grafting should be wel- comed, as it is apparently mere effective. The method is to sew finely-chopped hair in the desired place. The hair cells are readily transformed to skin cells, and the fine bits of hair, kept in place by a suitable band- age, soon take root, and the white' spots that appear within a week gradually grow into a complete covering of skin,</p>
        <p>PARCHMENT INDUSTRY. In these scientific days a use can be found for everything. Old par. chments can ap- parently be put to a variety of uses, and. it is interesting to follow the different stages. Dealers have invented a process for remov- ing the ink, and eventually the cleaned parchments in many cases come back as "fine French kid gloves." The clippings re- maining when the gloves are made are not wasted. Mixed, with vellum and bits of leather they are boiled down for "size.", There are now the coarse shavings to dis- pose of, and these, with odds and ends of other skins, are utilised as fillings for cheap tennis balls.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>PICTURES OF</head>
        <p>PICTURES OF</p>
        <p>THE DURBAR. Our picture shows one of the ruling chiefs, dressed in quaint robes, who was prctented SO their Majesties at the Durbar.</p>
        <p>SEEING THE NEW YEAR IN. rat St. Paul's Churchyard, on New Year's Ev:-, is always the scene of a great throng of s4Z:Y People who want to hear the great clock boom out the hour of twelve. This year I, a greater gathering than ever, the crowd stretching down Ludgate Hill and partly ItoPi!leet Street. As tile clock finished striking the hour, everybody cheered, and tk43 froni sirens, whistles, and catcalls could be heard for a consithrable distance.</p>
