      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SIRIUS, THE FAMOUS DOG STAR.</head>
        <p>SIRIUS, THE FAMOUS DOG STAR.</p>
        <p>From now until next April there will be fine opportunities of seeing the most lovely star in the heavens, the famous Sirius or Dog Star. I wonder how many of my readers know how to find Sirius, and how many have watched his dazzling, sparkling, glowing light. If you have never seen Sirius in all his glory you have missed one of the most beautiful sights of the Universe. To find Sirius look South. Just now in the Southern heavens the constellation of Orion rises and when you have found Orion it is easy to locate Sirius. What you don't know Orion - -</p>
        <p>Then 'lOok for five bright stars, three fixed close in the centre as though the central ornament of a mighty "brooch, This is the belt of Orion.</p>
        <p>Next draw a line through the three stars downwards towards the earth and it will pass just above the star Sirius. You will not need to ask anyone whether you have found the right star. It is the brightest and most beautiful in the heavens, and when the air is clear you will see it sparkle and glow with green, red and white flashes of light. He looks a very proud star does Sirius, and seems to blink at you and show a fresh colour as though to say "Yes, I am Sirius, have you ever seen another star that could do as I do. There are many fine fellows in the sky, but not one like me. lam the King of Suns."</p>
        <p>And so he is, and once you get to know him you will look for him like lookiug for an old friend. He happens to be fifty billion miles away, and it takes his light about eight and three-quarter years to reach us. A distant friend to be sure, but if you get to know him he won't seem distant. Again I say, get to know Sirius "You will find him such a sweet dazzling fellow.'</p>
        <p>THE LAND OF JOY. I dreamed a ridiculous dream one night : 'Twas that I had journeyed to Mars, and there Found people working with all their might, And everything up there, everywhere, Was just as it is on our native sphere • Scme men gave orders and some obeyed ; Yet one thing struck me as being queer: There wasn't a toiler who lot keel dismayed.</p>
        <p>I marched for the cause of the hope men had, And the dearth of tears and the lack of woe; I wondered how all could appear so glad While they toiled as people do here below. And I found, ere long, how it came to pass That each man worked with a willing heart, That no one muttered nor Eigbed, "Alas !" Or thought of his part as a hateful part. On every corner high stores were rained, And the names of the living were chiseled there, And those who merited praise were praised, Hence gladness abcunded everywhet e ; Whenever a fair thing might be said, They chiseled it there on a gleaming stone; They didn't wait till a man was dead To praise the courage that he had shown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>THIS WEEK'S STORY.</head>
        <p>THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Your Uncle Caleb once had tea with the Archbishop of Canterbury. The Archbishop is very fond of children and very fond of a joke, and one of his stories about a little girl is so good that I am going to tell it you here. He was once walking in the country near to London when he saw a little girl looking ruefully at a gate. "0 please sir" she asked " Will you open this gate for me." Smiling at the maiden he lifted the latch and pushed it open. It swung quite easily. "You're such a big little girl that you could have opened so nice a gate yourself," said the Archbishop. "Oh yes I c3uld sir," she said "but then I should have got my fingers all over paint." AndY then the Prelate saw that that was what had happened to him.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE'S NEW COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>• The opening of the Cheshire County Council's Train ing College for Teachers, which is being built near Crone on land acquired from the Marquis of Crewe, has been postponed until March on account of delay in completing the buildings. The woik of training teachers, which was begun by the county authority two ,_years ago, will be continued at the Crewe Mechanis'a Institute, %here a suite of rooms have been placed to the service of the County Cc uncil by the railway com- pany. The new college will cost about £30,000 and will provide a two years' course of b aining for 120 students —5O men and 70 women. It is understood that the county schools will be able to find employment for nearly all the teachers trained at the College. This is the first unsectarian teachers' college in Cheshire.</p>
        <p>With the festive season over and the last tradesman's messenger pacified it may be safe to discuss the subject of Christmas boxes and New Year gifts. And for the solace of tormented householers let it be written first of all that we are not the earliest sufferers. Begging, under the guise of asking New Year gifts, was a nuisance in Roman days as may be gathered from Tacitus, and Claudius earned the gratitude of the Empire by issuing a decree against it. But even the Emperor had to have respect for the past, and dare not attempt to extinguish at a stroke a custom so hoary and so universally honoured. But he did go to the length of ordaining that presents should be demanded upon one day cnly, and that day the first of the year. This much may be read in the annals of Rome and it is to be assumed that his sub- jects were grateful for the relief. Either from this decree of Cladius, or from a sense of the propriety of things, New Year giving passed to all the nations of Europe and for centuries the custom has lingered. In England Christmas has become the season of gifts, but it was not always so, and in France New Year's Day is still the day of days for those %; ho are able to give or hope to receive. Even in the jolly days when Christmas was the feast of feasts in England presents were generally kept until New Year. There is interesting illustration of this in Ben Jonson's "Masque of Christmas,"for the man who gives presents is not Santa Claus but "New Year gift in a blue coat with an orange and a sprig of rosemary on his head, his hat full of brooches, with a collar of gingerbread, a bottle of wine under either arm."</p>
        <p>The subject of New Year's 'giving is an alluring one to the Antiquarian but several discouraging discoveries await him. The first is that the giving was chiefly the giving of the poor to the rich. In the cold light of history many yarns of the "good King Wencelas " type will have to be gently but firmly rejected. The tenants of the great estates flocked to the landlord's door to give and not to receive a gift of table fowl or tasty food, and the indoor servants waited on their lord to make the best presents they could afford.</p>
        <p>Such a custom must have been oppressive and lacking in spontaneity. It is easy also to see that much subtle bribery might come in the guise of New Year giving.ln the Tudor and Stuart period it was the subject of terrible abuse. Judges openly received enormous sums in presents from those who had received favours or who had hopes of favours to come, and those who held high places at Court reaped a great harvest under the thin dis- guise of New Year good will.</p>
        <p>"GLOVE MONEY."</p>
        <p>It was a customary and allowable thing to receive presents of gloves for cold January days, and these went to the favoured ones stuffed with pieces of gold. When the bribe was too big to be pushed into the gloves big sums under the name "glove money" were openly given. A good story is told of Sir Thomas More, who in evil days set a fine example of integrity. As Lord Chancellor be had decided a case in favour of a lady named Crooker and on the New Years day which rollowed she sent him a pair of gloves with forty gold ' angels ' hidden in them. Sir Thomas returned the gold coins with a note, Mistress, since it was against good manners to refuse your New Year's efts. lam content to take your gi yes, but as for the lining I utterly refuse it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PIN MONEY.</head>
        <p>PIN MONEY.</p>
        <p>Pins were first made in England just about four hundred years ago and became a most popular present to make to ladies. Then came the habit of giving money instead, and 'pin money' on the first of January often bought my lady's favours. 'Pin money 'as a term for a woman's private expenditure still lingers with us, though happily without evil flavour. It may be interesting to give the reminder that the cost of pins was once quite an item in a lady's expenditure. Fancy prices were charged for these indispensible little cgmmodities, and under Henry VIII. it was found necessary Ito pass "An act for the true making of Pynnes " which enacted that the charge should not exceed. 6s. Bd. a thousand. Remembering the value of money in those days 6s. Bd. is a tall price. Poor people had to go on using wooden skewers for a long time after that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>GIFTS FOR THE MONARCHS.</head>
        <p>GIFTS FOR THE MONARCHS.</p>
        <p>The Public Record Office contains some rather curious manuscripts which record the official present giving to the Kings and Queens of England. While the poor were groaning under the obligation to give to the rich, the rich were endeavouring to look cheerful under the obligation of giving to the King..</p>
        <p>The gifts thus brought were recorded in durable documents and placed in the National archives. These go back as far as 1428, and the first belongs to Henry VI. The roll of Philip and Mary shows the Lord Cardinal Pole bringing in a saulte ' with a cover of silver,having a stone therein much enamelled of the story of Job' ' • and there is a long list of oxen, poultry and fruit for the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ELIZABETH THE MAGNIFICENT.</head>
        <p>ELIZABETH THE MAGNIFICENT.</p>
        <p>In Queen Elizabeth's days the custom ran to extravagant lengths. It pleased the Queen's vanity to be smothered. in tokens of affection, and as she delighted in gorgeous dress and jewellery her lords and ladies had little difficulty in knowing how to please her. After a perusal of the official list of presents no one would suppose that Elizabeth would ever need to buy any articles of dress at all; indeed there would be a lingering sense of wonder whether the gifts could possibly be worn in the 364 days of the year that were left. Gowns, mantles, smocks, petticoats are recorded in profusion many of them pearl- covered and embroidered with Venetian silver.</p>
        <p>In 1561 the Queen received the gift of a pair of silk stockings from her silk-woman, Mrs. Montagu, and thenceforth, says Howell, ' she never wore cloth hose any more.' Silk stockings henceforward fikure plentifully in the lists.</p>
        <p>Money gifts included £2O each from tho temporal lords, and the Bishops and Arch- bishops figure at £lO to £4O each, the total in money in the year 1561 being £1,260. For sheer magnificence and costliness Earl Leicester's presents easily hold the record. Here is , a description of his present to Elizabeth for 1572. " One armlet or shakell of gold, all over fairly garnished with rubyes and dyamondes, having in the closing thereof a clocke, and in the fore part of the same a fayre lozengic dyamonde hanging thereat a round juell fully garnished with dyamondes, and perle pendant, weying lloz. qu dim., and arthin g golde weight; in a case of purple</p>
        <p>vellate all over embroiderid with Venice golde, and lyned with green vellate. Thus did the noble lords tickle the vanity of Queen Bess, and thus were the gifts des- cribed upon the rolls already mentioned. One of these great parchments is ten feet long and closely written over. In justice to the Queen it ought to be said that she made New Year's Day an occasion of giving, and on the 'back of the rolls are inscribed long lists of the things she gave away. Now Year's Day was a great occasion and Elizabeth sat in State as her admirers laid at her feet the dazzling gifts and happy sur- prises that were prepared for her. No one will expect to be told that a canny Scot like James I. would discourage present giving, but the rolls of his reign show a remarkable change in the nature of the gifts. Jewellery and wearing apparel disappear almost entirely and lists of hard cash take their place. They evidently knew their man, and the change gives us no surprise. But it is too much to suppose that anyone savelJames enjoyed it, and the picture ,of a canny Scot scraping in the bawbees would contrast terribly with the vision of 'Elizabeth in all her glory receiving dazzling jewellery at the hands of brilliant courtiers. The romance had fled, and the old custom died. _ _</p>
        <p>Possibly it lingered during the reign of Charles I. but no records remain. Among common folks New Year giving was the rule for a long time and was the period when tips and perquisites were looked for. One story of the court of Charles I. will be sufficiently ancient and good to close our gossip. Archy Armstrong was Court jester to the King at Whitehall Palace, and on New Years morning was very attentive in bidding good morning to the great folks who gathered there A nobleman whom he greeted dropped as usual a few gold pieces into his hand. But Archy was not contented, thinking it ought to have been more, and muttered that they were too light. Prithee, then Archy' said the nobleman 'let me see them again; and by the way there is one that I would be loth to part with.'</p>
        <p>A.rchy, expecting to get a larger gift, returned the coins. But all he got was a dis- appointing bit of humonr Archy thou must be a fool indeed, for any man is one who sees money in his hand and has not wit to keep it.' A.M</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>:„.....„..,‘.......,..‘"„‘,..,..."...,‘,,,,..".v FROM . ' LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>:„.....„..,‘.......,..‘"„‘,..,..."...,‘,,,,..".v FROM . ' LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P.</p>
        <p>WILL THERE BE A COAL STRIKE? The ballot of the miners of Great Britain on the question of a general strike is to be taken next week, and so far as present appearances go they all point to an over- whelming majority in support of a stoppage of work. The last ballot of the miners was taken in 1909 on the question of supporting the Scottish miners, and on that occasion the vote was 518,361 for a general strike and 62,980 against. The intensity of feeling among the miners of England and Wales at that time was not nearly so strong as it is to-day, and the only thing which can prevent a vote more overwhelmingly in favour of a strike than the vote on the last occasion may be a feeling that if the necessary two-thirds majority for a strike is obtained, a stoppage of work will be certain whereas the voting on the last ballot was more a demonstration to frighten the Scotch coalowners into submission to the men's demands.</p>
        <p>The question which is responsible for all this unrest in the coal mining industry is that of a minimum wage. This question of a minimum wage has been forced into pro- minence through what is known as the "abnormal places" problem. As everybody can imagine the earning power of the coal getter is determined to a great extent by the nature of the seam at which he may be working. This varies not only as between colliery and colliery, but as between place and place in the same mine. Not only that, but the character of the seam at the same place may vary from day to day. It can be easily understood how difficult it is to fix a piece rate of wages which will be in pro- portion to the labour that he honestly 'puts into his work. The solution of the abnormal places question has proved impossible. Thel owners have recognised that special con- sideration should be given to abnormal places; but in practice it has not been found possible to devise any system of payment which would satisfy both men and masters. THE NEW DEMAND.</p>
        <p>Recognising the difficulty of devising any satisfactory way of paying for abnormal places, the miners have now abandoned the demand for special treatment for abnormal places by way of a minimum wage for those who work in such places. At the Miners' Conference last October it was decided to demand the establishment in each district of an individual district wage for all men and boys working in the mines, without refer- ences to the places being abnormal. This was anew policy for the Miners' Federation, and one of tremendous importance. It has been described in some quarters as an entirely new demand in trade unionism, but that can hardly be correct. It may be, and probably is the first time that a guaranteed minimum wage has been demanded for men who are employed at piece rates; but the minimum rate of wages is the established practice in most of the trades where workmen are paid by time and not by the piece. But so far as the miners' demand can be understood it is for the guarantee of a minimum wage to every man and boy under a system of piece rates; and most of the difficulties in dealing with this demand arise from the novelty of the proposal. The owners have asked the men to draft a prac- ticable scheme for the establishment of a system of a guaranteed minimum, but the men's leaders quite naturally reply that they have not the material at their disposal to do this. They have not access to the books of the colliery owners from which alone can the facts and particulars be derived which would et able them to construct the details of a new wages scheme. In this the men appear to be quite right. It is for the men to demand what they think is just and reasonable; and it is for the masters to show that this demand is impossible or impracticable.</p>
        <p>The Conference in October suspended national action until efforts had been made to deal with the question in the respective districts by conferences with the employers. The reports from the districts have been con- sidered at a recent Conference in London, and the South Wales, Scottish, and Lancashire delegates reported that the coal owners have refused to consider the demand. The English coal owners have refused to concede the minimum wage, but have not definitely closed the negotiations. After considering the reports from the districts the Conference decided to take a ballot on the whole coal- field of Great Britain on the question of putting into force what is known as Rule 21, which provides the machinery for calling a</p>
        <p>general strike. A hopeful feature in the situation is that meanwhile the districts in England are encouraged to continue their negotiations with the employers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>GOODALL)S STOCKTAKING SALE NOW- PROCEEDING AT • KING STREET MANCHESTEV.</p>
