      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>HORRORS OF A STRIKE.</head>
        <p>THE HORRORS OF A STRIKE.</p>
        <p>There does not seem to be much reason to hope that the coal owners will concede the demand for a guaranteed minimum wage without a struggle. The men appear to be equally determined to force the demand; and therefore the strong probability is that there will bc a stoppage of work over the whole British coalfield at the end of February. There has been nothing of the sort in this country before, and it is impossible to conceive the seriousness of such a disaster. The appalling nature of the consequences of a general strike in the coal industry are such that the very general desire to prevent them - from happening is,perhaps,the most important of the things in the present situation which make for a peaceful settlement. A few weeks of a general strike in the coal industry would stop every factory in the country. The rail- ways would be stopped,and gas and electricity works would have to shut down. Such a strike could not continue. It would inflict horrors far greater than could come from the invasion of the country by a foreign power.</p>
        <p>In view then of the fait that ihe' miners and the coal owners could not be allowed to carry on the strike until the one or the other had exhausted their resources, we are driven to find some way of settling the dispute without all this suffering and horror. The question is one which is suitable for reference to some judicial tribunal. The men appear to have a strong case. The owners them- selves admit the justice of the men's demand but deny that it is practicable. Everybody can see that there are practical difficulties,but it is the business of conciliation to discuss these difficulties and to solve them or prove that they are insoluble. SOME DIFFICULTIES ADMITTED.</p>
        <p>The men's demand is that every man who goes down the pit shall be guaranteed a day's wage, whether his output of coal at piece rates will pay that wage or not. At first sight that might seem to be an unreason- able demand, but consideration will suggest a good many strong reasons in its support. The idea which occurs to the mind when it first hears of that proposal is that it would be an encouragement of laziness and shirking. and also that it is not fair to pay a man more than he has earned. Let it be admitted at once that if it were impossible to devise ay safeguards against shirking then the guaranteed minimum wage would be impracticable. But there are plenty of possible safeguards. The man's output would be checked, and it would soon be found out who were the habitual shirkers, and the other men would be the severest in their condemnation of such, because they mould understand that such conduct, if persisted in, would lead to a general reduc- tion of wages through reduced profits. Every miner would realise that the wages could only come from profits, and that no minimum wage could be paid for long unless the earnings of the whole body of workmen enabled it to be paid - -</p>
        <p>It is always ur-ged when questions of this sort are raised that it is not fair to pay all workmen alike—the good as much as the bad. But that point is not involved in the present controversy. It is not a maximum, but a minimum wage which is being demanded. The minimum wage will not be what the best workman working under average conditions will earn. It will be much less than that. Moreover, the miners have agreed that special treatment shall be given to the old men and to others who from physical causes may not be up to the ordinary standard of efficiency. The demand there- fore is narrowed down to a guaranteed minimum wage for the man who is able to earn that wage under normal conditions. If he does not earn that minimum wage it will be for one of two reasons. It will be because he shirks" his work, or because the place in which he works is abnormal. If the first be the reason, then his sins wf,l soon find him out, and neither the trade union nor the master ought to show any consideration to such a man. If the second be the reason then the case for a minimum wage is unanswerable. The man has done the work. He has laboured as hard as others who may have earned four or five times as much through no more exertion, but owing to better luck. A miner's right to take home a living wage should not depend upon luck.</p>
        <p>If the miners succeed in getting the principle of the minimum wage accepted it will have a tendency to equalise wages in the mines. Of course, if the trade unions are strong enough they may be able to prevent that, but it is doubtful if it is a desirable thing to do. It is not fair that there should be such wide differences in the earnings of men working in the same mine, when the difference is due not to individual merit or exertion but merely to accident. The a;in of the miners should be to raise the total of the wages bill, and to ensure a fair average distribution. The miners are acting on a sound economic principle in endeavouring to get a minimum wage.lt is a principle which is defensible both on moral and economic grounds.</p>
      </div>
