      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[kit RUMS RESERVED.] (Copyrighted in the United States of America by Arthur W. Marchmont.)</p>
        <p>SIR GREGORY'S SILENCE</p>
        <p>ARTHUR W. MARCHMONT, Author of "The Man Who Was Dead," "By Right of Sword," tcc.</p>
        <p>shall not listen to anything you wish to tell me with great sympathy." "You think I ought to have explained more about what was behind Mr. Garwood's recognition of me?" "If I have wished you to do so, it has been on your own account, not on mine. I should have welcomed a chance of helping you. That is all." "I know that. I know what a true friend you are. If it had been only my own matter I should have told you everything, but there is a secret which concerns someone else and hoped that I should have been left in peace. I'm afraid now, however, that what occurred in London is to be tried again here. It seems selfish only to turn to you when I may need help." "I think you had better tell me. You may think it selfish, if you wish, but I shall not, and whatever you say shall never pass my lips." was Mr. Garwood's typist for a short time, and while there I was accused of hav- ing stolen some jewel. I have never under- stood it. The jewels were entrusted to him by a client and he kept them in a safe. One of them, a ring, was found in the drawer of my desk at the office, and Mr. Garwood and a detective, named Simpson, came that same evening to my little flat and made a search among my things. To my horror they found in one of my trunks two more rings and a duplicate key of the safe. I assure you on—" "Don't bother to do that. I know you," he interposed. "Go on with the story." "I don't know who actually put the rob- bery upon me, nor how it was done, but I had been cruelly persecuted before going to Mr. Garwood, and all who had employed me were set against me;" and she described the interview with Mrs. Chancellor and the lat- ter's reference to the visit of a woman of ill repute. "I can only conclude that in all the other cases similar means were adopted," she added. "But whatever the means were they succeeded, and I was in great distress." "How did this Garwood hear of you?" "From one of the firms who had employed me as typist. He wrote and offered me a situation. " _</p>
        <p>"And that did not strike you as curious?" "Not at the time. I was getting very anxious to find work and jumped at the offer. My money was nearly gone." "You were meant to jump at it, of course. This Garwood—" _</p>
        <p>"He was no party to anything, Mr. Steven- son," she interrupted, quickly. "I am sure of that. After the rings had been found in my rooms, I had a talk with him and he de- clared his belief in my innocence, and said that nothing more should come of it. I was to see him the next day and tell him every- thing about myself in order that together we might try and find out the truth." "And when you saw him, what passed?" "I did not stay to see lim. I left London the next day. I had already made all my preparations to do so—and something hap- pened which made it necessary." "Also part of the same scheme, so that you might appear to have run away from tne charge. It is an infamous story. And who is behind it? Do you know?" "I —I am afraid so," replied Helen reluct- antly. "Well, whoever it is, he has found out that you are here in Manchester, and has begun the same game. I have been considering whether I ought to tell you," and he described about what had occurred.</p>
        <p>"I met the detective, Mr. Simpson, in the street this afternoon; and it was that which worried me."</p>
        <p>"I wish I could see him," exclaimed Mr. Stevenson angrily. "The thing's as plain to see R 6 blight on a cotton plant. Whoever's be- hind this is going to do what was done in London. I'll run up to town and interview that Garwood."</p>
        <p>"I think I had better tell you everything," said Helen. "It is really the story of my life; and you will see then what I fear is the answer to the puzzle, and also why I am bound to silence. My name is really Helen Powell. Paxton was my mother's name. I was born in a mining camp out in Montana, close to Helena—that's how I got my name— and my father, Gregory Powell—" "Good God I" exclaimed Mr. Stevenson, suddenly sitting bolt upright in his chair and seizing the arms in excited surprise. Then recovering himself quickly, he cried, "What was that?" and jumping up he went into the other office.</p>
        <p>Helen looked after him in astonishment. "It's all right. I thought I heard some- thing out there," he said when he returned minute later, looking pale and disturbed. "Excuse my bolting out like that. It might have been another of those people. Well?" and ha went back to his chair and sat down, not looking at Helen, but staring down at the table with his hands clasped tightly and a set, stern look on hie face.</p>
        <p>"My father deserted my mother coon after I was born, and she left Montana and went away to her own people in Massachusetts and took their name." She then told of her mother's death, and her own bringing up by her aunt, and how on the latter's death she had come to England. He listened without another interruption, and when she finished sat silent for a while.</p>
        <p>"And your father?" he asked at length, in a tone so strangely unlike his usual voice that Helen glanced at him in surprise. "And your father?" he repeated. "I never saw him or heard of him again until—" she paused. "Until?" he repeated in the same set, strained tone.</p>
        <p>"This is the painful part of it all, Mr. Steveqson," and she told of her engagement to Bulrner, her visit to the Chase, and her in- terview with Sir Gregory in London and con- sequent flight to hide herself from them all. "What did you learn of your father's life in Montana? Did you ever hear of—of a partner of his—a Jake Stevens?"</p>
        <p>. - . "Did you ever hear of my father then?" she cried in intense astonishment.</p>
        <p>"Answer me! Answer me I" he said im- patiently. "Yes. He betrayed him, I was told, and had him put in gaol on a false charge, so that he might keep for himself the gold which they had found together." With a supreme effort he restrained the ex- clamation which rose to his lips, his hands locked even more tightly than ever. "And what proofs have you that you are that man's child?" he asked, after a pause. "I had my mother's marriage certificate, but Sir Gregory destroyed it. He said it was by accident." "And Gregory Powell is now Sir Gregory Trevellock? You are sure." "Oh, yes. He admitted it to me." He sat a long while buried in thought, and drew a deep sigh before breaking the silence. "Your story has affected me more than I can say, and for reasons I will not now ex- plain. But I can help you. You have indeed done right to tell me everything. You will be persecuted no more. Trust me for that. But I must think things out. It is infamous —infamous!"</p>
        <p>And more than that he would not say then, but Helen could see how profoundly he was moved.</p>
        <p>-76 IA continued.)</p>
        <p>MORE CHURCHES THAN PUBLIC-HOUSES. The Bishop of Liverpool on Monday drove in the first pile of the new Church of All Souls at Blowick, Southport, and was described as setting a good example, for he neither smoked nor drank. The Mayor of South- port said the town wm in the somewhat unique position of having more churches than public-houses.</p>
        <p>WAN TED.—Happiness 1 To which nothing conduces so much as good health. Especially is this the case with children. Steedman's Powders preserve a healthy state of the constitution, and contain no poison. Always observe the EE in Steedmans</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>CHAPTER XXI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXI.</p>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER CAMPAIGN FAILS.</p>
        <p>Bostock heard of Lotta's death from Mrs. Caulfield while he was etill in Manchester, where he had found many more difficulties in his plans against Helen than he had ex- perienced in London. He had easily been able to discover the life she led and where she lived; but having . . ..</p>
        <p>no ally in her home, had been unable to d'O' anything against her there. He had resorted to his former device of sending disreputable people to ask for her both at her house and at Mr. Stevenson's office. But the result had been worse than failure. Bostock had no knowledge of Mr. Steven- son's feelings for Helen, of course; but he heard what occurred to his emissaries. The first had been simply ordered out of the place; but when a second and a third _ .</p>
        <p>came., one a flashily-dressed woman of ob- vious occupation, and the other a loafer with drink written all over his blotched, puffy face, Mr. Stevenson began to question them, and both had mumbled some excuse and hurried away. The coincidence that they always came when Helen was out struck him; and when a fourth put his ragged head inside the door just as Mr. Stevenson chanced to be in the outer office alone, he collared the fellow and 'walked him into his own room, determined to bottom the thing. "What do you know about Miss Powell?" he asked, with his back against the deor. "That's her business and mine. I want to see her, not you," replied the fellow, a surly bulldog out of the slums. "Well, I'm going to make it mine, too. Now, out with it." "No, I shan't. Let me go. You can't keep me here." "You'll stay here till you tell me, my man. New, what do you know about her?" "She's one of us, that's all," was the reply with an oath and a laugh. "You lying dog. What do you moan by that?" "She told me to come here. If she ain't in, you can tell her that I came for her all right. She'll know who it is. We don't use no visiting cards. " "Who sent you here?" "Don't I keep telling you? She did." "You've made a blunder, my man; and you've got hold of the wrong man to tell lies to. How much have you been paid to do this?"</p>
        <p>_ "No one ain't paid me anything." "You infernal scoundrel," cried Mr. Stevenson, furiously. "You dirty, lying dog, you don't understand what you have been bribed to do. I know your sort, and l'll have the police here. I'm the wrong sort for you to try and fool. I'll--" "Let me go," broke in the man with an oath, showing fight. Mr. Stevenson snatched up a heavy ruler. "Stand off, or I'll split your head open with this. Now, I'll give you one minute to tell me who put you on to this, and if you don't tell me, I'll send for the police." "All right, guv'nor ; there ain't no need for anything of that. I don't mean no harm," said the man, cowed and alarmed. "Time's nearly up, and Pm a man of my word."</p>
        <p>. "1 dunno who it was. I'll tell you all I do know. A chap met me in the street and gave me a drink and a bob, and promised me a couple more if I'd come and ask for her. That's straight, on my word, guv'nor." "Where is he?" " Waiting for me, watching outside pro- bably to see I don't sell him. I'm to meet him at the corner."</p>
        <p>"I'll go and meet him with you," de- clared Mr. Stevenson readily; "and I should like a word or two with him myself." They went out together, but nostock was on the 'watch, and, seeing that thinga had gone wrong, he hurried off. The man looked about for him and not seeing him remem-</p>
        <p>bered the threat of the police, and drawing his companion's attention to one direction, ran off in another, and was soon lost to eight Mr. Stevenson returned to his office pro- foundly puzzled by the affair. He had not said anything to llelen about the former visits, but now he considered it necessary to put her on her guard. The relations between them since the day of his propoeal had been almost the same as before it. His tact had helped to overcome the first reluctance Helen had felt to remain with him. He had scrupulously avoided re- ferring to any subject except the work on which they were engaged, and his unfailing kindness and thought for her had won upon her until her peace of mind had been re- skred, and she felt able to accept the fresh footing. . _ _</p>
        <p>When she returned that afternoon he was autprised to observe that she was both agi- tated and alarmed. There was cause for this. When Bostock had rushed away from the prospect of trouble through having sent the man to the office, he had been in too great a hurry to notice the people he passed in the street. Amongst them was Helen. She had seen him, however, and was quick to connect his presence in Manchester with the recent recognition by Mr. Garwood, and to augur trouble. She connected it with Sir Gregory, and jumped to the conclusion that he had found her out and intended to resume his persecu- tion of her. Bitterly angry, as well as alarmed at the prospect, she had been con- sidering the advisability of telling Mr. Stevenson everything, so that he should be on his guard in view of any attempt to pre- judice him against her, similar to those adopted in London. She had promised Sir Gregory to keep silent, but had made it a condition that all</p>
        <p>persecution should cease. If he broke that condition she was at liberty to speak. She had the nest absolute confidence in Mr. Stevenson, and was convinced that he would both protect her and keep her secret; but it would be much better for her to be first with him, and to speak before anything could be done to prejudice her. "I am very sorry to be so late," she said, as she sat down to her desk in his room, "but I have been worried."</p>
        <p>"Would you like to have a holiday this afternoon, and run down to Blackpool for a breath of fresh sea air?" "Oh, no, thank you. You seem to think that a breath of sea air is a necessity for anyone living in Manchester. You suggest it so often; but you do not use your own prescription, I notice." "Oh, I'm different. Case-hardened, I suppose. Let's get to work, then." They worked for about an hour, but Helen found it difficult to concentrate her atten- tion on the task. "I am very sorry, Mr. Stevenson, but I can't keep my thoughts from wandering. May we stop?" Ire looked across with his usual kindly smile. "I have been waiting for this," he said. "You are not quite yourself this afternoon," and he put his papers together and closed the portfolio. She shut up the typewriter and arranged her own papers, and sat a moment in thought. "I think I should like to tell you something," she said !lowly. "I am afraid there is some trouble in store for me." "I am afraid you are right. I hayon't spoken to you about your affairs for some time, you know, but that doesn't mean that I have not thought about them, or that I</p>
      </div>
