      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>rE</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>REV. A. M. MITCHELL AND CHRISTMAS</p>
        <p>VISITORS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Mr. G. Mather presiding. There were also present:— Mrs Simpson, Revs T It Matthews and A M Mitchell, Messrs E L Bent, W Gill, A Brooks, W C Brooks, W Furness, It Lee, .1 A Hughe G A Allen, J A Rushworth, M W Seton, W Flanagan, A Bottomley (clerk), and It W Peacock (dsputy clerk). STATE TELEPHONES. In reply to Mr. Bent, the CLERK said nothing had yet been done by the Government to reimburse local authorities for the loss to local rates which would accrue from the transfer of the N ktional Teleinone Co.'s undertaking to the State. Unless some steps were taken the loss would be a heavy one. The CHAIRMAN : If we don't get any rates it will be another hen rcost robbed.</p>
        <p>DINING HALL EXTIINSIONS. The CHAIRMAN moved a resolution authorising application to be mid° fir power to borrow £1,816 for the purposes of kitchen and dining hall extensions at the Workhouse, in accordanoo with plans already submitted to the Local Government Bari.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. BROOKS seconded. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL asked if the members of the Board were not going to chAnge their mind' and abandon that" wild cat" scheme? A case had not been made out for the extension. TII3 number of inmates of the IVerkhouse W. 13 not increasing but the patients in the iufirmary were on the increase. ['here was also a prospect of new diseases cropping up—they were hearing of them nearly every day, as the result, he supposed, of innoculation, anti-toxin and s ) forth—and they would be wanting more hospital accommodation. It would be much better to apply £l,OOO to the hospital than to the enlargement of the dining hall, which was unnecessary. The resolution was agreed to.</p>
        <p>NEW OUT-RELIEF REGULATIONS. The CLERK informed the Board that a new Order of the Local Government 11 ard with reference to the administration of out-relief would come in force in March. There were rimy points to consider, and he would like the Bard to appoint a committee to go thoroughly into the matter, with a view to a good work • ing of the Order. It was decided that the Clerk should obtain a copy of the Order for eich member of the Board.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL : Is the trend of the Order to reduce out-relief or to increase it ? The CHAIRMAN : Would it not bo as well if you raised the question after you had gone throu;ll it yourself ? Mr. A. BitooKs moved that a committee consisting of the chairman and vice-chairman of the committee, and the various relief committees, be appointed to consider the Order. Mr. W. C. BaooKs seconded and the proposition was carried. CHRISTMAS VISITORS TO THE WORKHOUSI. REV. A. M. MITCHELL TAKEN TO TASK. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL called for the production of the porter's time book in which the arrival and departure of visitors to the workhouse are entered. He stated that on looking through the book on Friday he did not see any record of the vislis of the Mayor, Lady Greenall and AL H srold Smith, MP. to the workhouse on Christmas Day. He thought the names of such distinguished vLitors ought to be entered, for it there was an omission in their ease it was possible that others might also have been left out. The book was produced and the Clerk read entries of he names of the Mayor and Mr. Harold Svith, the times of their arrival and departure being day recorded. The GitaxamaN told the Rev. A. Mitchell that he ought to be more careful. He had wasted a good deal of the Board's time with frivolous talk. The Rev. A. M. MITCHEL : I am very sorry I have made any such mistake. The CHAIRMAN : it IS DOI the only mistake. The Itev. A. M. MITCHELL : The book was not on the table. If it had been, there would have been no difficulty about it. The CHAIRMAN: You have made a bad start of the new year. Mr. FURNESS said the rev, gentleman put the Board in an unfortunate position, for he made statements which the members knew full well were not correct, no discussion took place upon them, Mr. Mitchell's own version got into the Press, and the Board had no oppor- tunity of justifying themselves or of bringing him to book. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. BROOKS said he was pleased the matter had been brought forward and that the entries were duly recorded, because the rev, gentleman's statement amounted to a charge against the officer who MS responsible for keeping the book. That officer's con— duct had been justified by the entries that had been proved. The Rey. A. W MITCHELL: I did not make any charge ag %lost any officer. I simply wanted to point out that the names of all the distinguished people ought to be entered. The CHAIRMAN : There were things reported in the papers about the last meeting that I did not hear, with reference to me personally. Had I heard them, very likely you (the Rev. A. M. Mitchell) would have wished you had never been born. The itev. A. M. AIETCRELL : Will you tell U 3 what you refer to ? The CH-Ur:max : That is all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LATE DR. HENRY WATSON,</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. HENRY WATSON,</p>
        <p>Anticipating the anniversary of the death of Dr Henry Watson, Which took place on January 3rnl, 1911, a memorial provided by personal friends was on tuesday placed upon his grave in the Manchester Southern Cemetery. Mr. Thomas Kay, the president of the Gentlemen's Glee Club, has taken special interest in all the details. The memorial, executed by Mr A Fieldsend, of West Didsbury, is in the form of a low Gothic tomb, in what is known as blue pearl granite. The granite is polished throughout, and its recumbent Latin cross is cut in relief in the solid. The memorial bears the following inscription :—" Henry Watson, Doctor of Music, Cambridge. Born 30th April, 1846 ; died 3rd January, 1911. Founder and donor of "rho Henry Watson Musical Library,' by the gift to the city of Manchester In 1899 of his extensive collection of musical works and instruments. This memorial is placed upon his grave by grateful, loving and admiring f riends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SOCIETY OF MUSICIANS.</head>
        <p>SOCIETY OF MUSICIANS.</p>
        <p>At the conference of the Incorporated Society of Musicians at Llandudno on Tuesday, it was stated that one of the most implrtant questions was that of regis- tration, and unless a.. active campaign was organized for bringing the claims of musicians before the proper authorities the registration of musicians might be placed in the hands of a body entirely foreign to their interests. It was not believed probable that legislation would interfere with the freedom of anyone to teach music, but a great deal could be done to persuade the public to require the full value in teaching of the fee paid. It was also stated that some fees were so low as to disgrace a civilized country. It was decided to hold the conference next year in Birmingham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>" 4</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST,</p>
        <p>AMATEUR FOLLIES. One who had evidently been reading the account of the workers' performance at St. Peter's school, and who would have been quite inexcusable if he had not been very young, mentioned to me a certain crooked lane in order to turn the thing into what Artemus Ward would have called "a goak." Said he—" Why is that lane like the drama which was given at John-street for the poor children's treat ?" And when I gave it up, the delighted youngster exclaimed, "Because it's 0-li-ver Twist !" Now why the Dickens didn't I think of that P It's nearly as old as the work itself. The leading comedian in Offenbach's " Princess of Trebizonde " indulged in that identical witticism when I was in my teens ; and I doubt not many of the old folk who on Thursday assembled zlt the Parr Hall for their annual treat would have recognised it as of the days of Auld Lang Syne. IHE LIGHT FANTASTIC.</p>
        <p>- The appearance of the ',letter of the wiredrawers' secretary reminded me of an amusing incident of which a namesake of his told me. My Mr. Lightfoot had two daughters who attended an evening elms in a room which was barely large enough to accommodate the students comfoi tably. One night, when all on the register were present and the lack of rcom was pain- fully apparent, the teacher suggested a rearrangement, and, indicating a corner which was not so crowded, said" Perhaps the two Miss Light f eel would not mind sitting over there!" UNDER CHLOROFORM.</p>
        <p>In last week's "Notes and Notions" there was a story arising cut of a surgical operation at Manchester. In a hospital there I have seen several patients under chloroform, and it is curious to notice how differently the anresthetic affects various tempera- ments. Occasionally cue will behave so violently that three or four mvn will be required to hold him down by main force ; another will conduct himself like a pot valiant ft How "three sheets in the wind "; while others simply lie and moan "My head, my head," and most are very sick. All this, of course, ii when they are recovering from the effects of the chloroform ; until the successful completion of the operation (for which at any particular institution their wrilen consent is necessary) they ate quiet enough—the doctors see to that. ANOTHER TRIFLE.</p>
        <p>I am just now dealing in trifles, and while I am about it I may as well refer to one brought to mind by the title of the Warrington Dramatic Society's latest project "When we were twenty-one." A party of young fellows whom I knew well, numbering precisely that, entered a restaurant with the under- standing that the first to speak should "stand pies round.' As they marched through the shop and filed upstaii s, the proprietress beamed benevolently through her spectacles, and at once told off a couple of smart waitresses to attend to them specially. lhe latter repeatedly asked what they might have the pleasure f supplying, but the mysterious strangers appeared to be dumb dogs all. Eventually the gels left the room for a while; then returning,' they patiently proceeded to "try, try, try again." As the tongues of the entire one-and-twenty seemed to have been paralysed, the servants ultimately called their mistress, and she, having likewise failed to make any impression upon the silent crew, begged them to leave quietly and give no further trouble. Thereupon the jokers passed out in Indian file as they entered.' That was in a New Year's week, "when we were twenty-one." THE EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>I presume that by this time many readers of the "Examiner " will have visited the Trades and Food Exhibition at the Empire Rink. Those who have not should by all means do so forthwith. In the course of the last twenty years I have been stationed in..two other towns where similar exhibitions have been held periodically, and I always found them vastly entertain- ing. Once I varied my descrip lin of an event of the character by writing it In the local dialect—as if bon' the pen of a dull-witted old native ; and the manager was good enough to s 4y some very smooth and pleasant things about the " unilue press notice." As many of our young people must have discovered era this, samples of jellies, custards, chocolate and other toothsome delicacies are readily obtainable at the exhibition, either free or at merely nom;nal rates. The idea of the promoters is, not to compete unfairly with local retailers, but to advertise specialities which may be procured from shopkeepers in the town. WARRINGTON AND WAGNER.</p>
        <p>"Richard Wegner and his worls" formed the E 'abject of a lecture at the Socialist Hall on Sunday afternoon last. As cne inteieeted in a Leeds Musical Festival which I attended many years ago wrote (inter alia) of the toaster who had died not long before, "eseentially a man of ideas and ideals,Wagner was an enthusiastic reformer, in both politics and music. Early impressed by ite grandeur cf Beethoven he was fired by the idea of carrying into every detail of opet a the spirit of improvement that Beethoven had already so masterfully shown in his music. Discarding the old feeble type of libretti he delved in the Scandinavian mythology for something more virile and commanding. A master of orchestral colour he enlarged the oi chest's and divided it into sections to suit his polyphonic compositions. Ni detail of stage management was too small t) receive his intelligent attention in order to secure the perfect rendering of his masterpieces in all their unity and balance of parts. " ONE FOR THE LAWYER.</p>
        <p>I read in my " Examiner "of Robinson of Cleve- land, whose addresses to a jury used (according to an American legal paper) to attract a crowd in the old days. It served in my case to recall how the United States Secretary of War, himself a distinguished lawyer, once illustrated what grievous mistakes lawyers sometimes make by trying to browbeat witnesses. An attorney of considerable reputation went to a small town to try a case, in the course of which be saw that, in order to win for his client, he would have to discredit the testimony of a young man. After several offensive questions, the lawyer asked the youth sharply, " Are you not a worthless fellow ?" " I don't know, sir," replied the young man. " Now, as a matter of fact," repeated the lawyer, tapping the table in front of him to emphasise his remarks, " don't you know you are so regarded in this community ? " " I don't know, sir," repeated the boy. Well," continued the lawyer, "isn't your father a worthless fellow?" "I don't know, sir," was the monotonous reply. " Come, come ? " roarded the attorney, "Answer me. Don't you know that your father is a worthless fellow, who lies around the house all day and won't work?" " I don't know, sir," said the youth, " Why don't you ask him ? There he sits on the jury." ONE FOR THE SCRIBE.</p>
        <p>I once had a somewhat similar experience. I was working as a young reporter in a West Riding town, and I heard that a constable at an out-station had suddenly gone out of his mind and run amok, with results calculated to make a sensational story for my paper. I hastened to the police office and, regardless of a meek-looking stranger in civilian dress, I said aloud to the c!erk in charge, "They say that P.C. -- has gone off his nut and has been cutting up rough all over the place. Is that true ?" And the answer be gave me with an inclination of the head toward the plain clothes man, was "He's there ; ask him !" It would be easy to multiply such instances; I have a good collection to draw upon. However, I was speaking more especially of a certain legal question THOU sHALr NOT. So they are complaining in America that the style of forensic eloquence is changing? However that may be there is still plenty of scope for its exercis3. Owners of pigeons and of gardens who have been driven well-nigh to desperation by marauding domestic animals may be interested in the information that placing poisoned meat in a pigeon house, situated on the ground in an enclosed garden attached to a house, for the purrose of killing trespassing cats, has been held an offence under the Act of 1864 In one case the Court intimated that to lay poisoned meat in a garden, with the intention of poisoning a dog that habitually trespassed there, would be an offence under the Act. But it should be noticed that neither Act in terms prevents the placing of, say, a bowl of poisoned water, or, presumably, of poisoned bread on laud or in a building, unless indeed the use of the " meat "in the Act of 1861 is to be understood in the old senso of anything usually eaten as nourishment by man or beast. But this would hardly include water, and the exact meaning of " meat " has yet to he determined by the courts. I came across the item while turning over some back numbers of the " Justice of the peace." It will convince those who need evidence upoa the pint —if any such there be—that there is still no lack of hairs for legal advocates to split at so much a-piece. A SPOKE IN HIS WHEEL.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday this paper. reported a case in which an impostor who was convicted of " obtaining food</p>
      </div>
