      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>12.</head>
        <p>12.</p>
        <p>and lodgings by false pretences " was by way of punishment awarded 14 days' further food and lodgings at the public expense. I well remember how, some 40 years ago, an old sinner of this heart- less class used regularly to come round doing the like, his victims being mostly poor widows. He was a singularly plausible villain of venerable appearance, and was known as " Grandfather Whitehead." As imprisonment quite failed to deter, be was finally taken in hand by a self-constituted vigilance com- mittee. They prepared a s'rong magenta compound, and with this dyed the flowing white locks of the incorrigible offender. Deprived at once of his really beautiful hair and of the benevolent aspect it gave him, the o'd rascal became as helpless as did Samson when Delilah played her barbarous trick upon him. HIS EPITAPII.</p>
        <p>Last week's antiquarian chapter contained a section on churchyard epitaphs which brought to my mind's eye a host that I have made acquaintance with in my travels; but Ido not find among them one to beat "Harper's," according to which in Zanesville, Ohio, they tell of a young widow who, in consulting a tombstone-maker with reference to a monument for the deceased, ended the discussion with " Now, Mr. Jones, all I want to say is, 'To my husband' in an appropriate place." "Very well, ma'am," said the stone-cutter. When the tombst me was put up the widow discovered to her amazement, that upon it were inscribed these words: "To my husband. In an appropriate place."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Women' Stature.—The clergy are not alone in giving way to pessimism just now. They mourn over the grossing mass of people who fear neither God nor man, and although less seders than is this alleged moral decadence, we have an A.R.A. lamenting the dis- eppesrance of the feminine figure of normal proportions owing, be says, to womans' indulgence in outdoor exercise and gymnastic. Similar (pinion! have been expressed by several A.R.A.'s, but one can only put these down to some spleenish outburst, because there is sbundant evidence to show that there has been a pro- gressive improvement in svomans' stature in the last generation or two. Whale one figure comparable to the Venus of Milo could have been discovered in mid- Victorian times, ten coul I be found to-day, and if artists have any diffisulty in getting perfect models, one is ir clined to think it is due rather to the want of physical taming because artists models as a rule, do not know much about athletics. Many of the most 'beautiful and graceful fi ;tires would be found among our stage dancers, Rho:e whole life is more or less one long training in physical exercise. Bigger Babies.—Whatever the pessimists may say about their pet subject—national (If cadence—if babies are becoming more scarce, they are, says Dr. Ezekiel Boyd, bigger, heavier, and better babies than were those of former generati ns. It is not unusual, he says, to find them almost double the old-time average weight. Improvement in stature i 3 more marked amongst women than in men, but ample couromation of Dr. Boyd's opinion may be seen in the average modern girl, who 13 better grown, better developed,and Inches taller than her maternal ancestors. That is an andoubted fact but the pessimists will not agree with Dr. Boyd as to One contributory cause of this improve- ment. They usually tell us that the sturdy race of old was built up upon good old-fashioned wholemeal bread, as a staple diet, Dr. Boyd thinks that only tended to bring about prometure ossification and is of opinion f that the improved growth of the modern child is I irgely duo t 3 the better quality of modern flour, although it has been c midemned by so many people as being deficient in some of the most valuable con- stituents of the wheat berry. Suitable Dress for Out-door Gunes.—The doctors hare in many places set their faces against hockey as a suitable game for young growing girls, and not a few schools have given it up. ['his seems to be a pity, and , a step back to the efileninacy of early Victorian times, and it is unnecessary. It may to true that is it every constitution is adapted to the gams, but in such cases, the same objection applies to every recreation that involves a strain upon heart and lungs. Strong healthy girls may play hockey and cricket, with perfect Impunity. The physical exertion would be less severe if they were allowed to play in rational dress, and that confession might go some way towards meeting the doctors' discouragement of such games, in the case of the weaker girls. Women when they go mountaineering in the Alps, wear a suitable cestnme for the purpose, with a skirt which can be easily dgached, and resumed again when entering the towns. If school girls were similarly provided, they would look much more grac •ful in the cricket and hockey field, it would be more healthy for them, and both games would lose much of that appearance of tomboyishnees which they present when the players era hampered with skirts so obviously unsuited to such recreations. No Servants —The housewife who is in search of a domestic servant and cannet obtain one, will probably find small comfort in the knowledge that the problem is quite as acute in other countries as it is in thig. Of the dearth of maid servants in the United States we are constantly reminded, and so:ne figures which have now been published which confirms the common kno sledge that the position on the Continent is very much the SAMB as it is here. In France the present number of domestic servants is said to be only one-half what it was in 1888, and Switzerland has had a similar experience. In the last-named country en attempt has been made to adjust the matter by institut- ing schools of domestic economy, but alas the results have been in many cases disappointing, for we are informed that in one such institution it was found that from 70 per cent. to 80 per cent, of the pupils returned to their homes, and never entered domestic service." It is evident therefore that these schools will not solve the problem, and in some quarters the effect is being tried of a series of rewards to maid servants already in situations. That p'an is, already in operation in this country, and no doubt it has had some effect in producing good servants, although not in increasing the number of those w'ao are willing to enter domestic service. Tailor Suits.—The tailor-made suit becomes every day more and more picturesque, and many new models are built of two materials, one plain, the other of check or stripe, but to avoid monotony great discretion is required in the combining of the two kinds or patterns of the materials. On some of the new models straps of cloth take the place of collars. The straps are finished with buttons and the same trimming is used for the skirt. For dressy functions nothing com- pares with the velvet tailor-made, provided the cut and velvet are both of the best. Crystal buttons are a favourite, and often the sole trimming of these velvet coats and skirts. A new idea which is finding favour in some of the best millinery houses is the wearing of cape hat and muff to match. Many of these sets are of coloured velvet, or velveteen edged with for. The muff is a dainty affair, and is furnished with quite a big and most convenient bag between the fur and the lining, the clasp hidden by a flap of fur. Like all the big muffs, it is limp and of granny design. The Russian coat is a favourite style for skating. It has a waist belt, is short and not full, Spots, large and small, rings and all kinds of single designs on plush grounds are having quite a vogue just now. Millinery and Veilings.—Quite a new hat is of seal- skin, which is tightly drawn over a large shape, of not or wire. Of course real sealskin is handsomer than musquash or coney, but its price is prohibitive to soy but the well-endowed. Very little trimming is required and consists usually of a jewelled ornament. Another , new model is a closely fitting cap made entirely of tiny blue and green iridescent feathers, with wings at an unusual angle. One resembles a bird in flight, and another rests on the side of the hat. A new matinee hat is also shown. It is of velvet, satin or plush, is quite flat, untrimmed and put on sideways, a feather however ostrich or osprey is placed under the brim, end rests on the hair when the bat is removed. Women who are inclined to stoutness should avoid fur millinery, although strips of fur may be used to advantage. Leopard skins for millinery purposes are a decided novelty although the possibilities of this fur for coats has been realised for some time. Mesquaah and leopard skin make a very effective combination. Worsted quill are very suitably for sporting or wet-wassailer hats and caps, and these may be easily made by a clever needle- woman. The heavy-patterned veil—so injurious to the sight is being superseded by a heavy openwork mesh— made of chenille threads woven in big diamond effects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>ENTITLED TO OUR CONFIDENCE.</head>
        <p>ENTITLED TO OUR CONFIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Anything that is again and again heartily endorsed by local people is assuredly entitled to our confidence. Week after week we read cases like the following, where local men and women speak out frankly and unreservedly in the hope that their experiences may be the means of bringing relief to others. Mr. J. Clucas, of 3, Cartwright-street, Runcorn, says :—" I was laid up with sciatica for eleven months and during that time I was like a cripple, and unable to do a stroke of work. Terrible pains used to take me across the bottom of the back; and I also had these pains in my hips, legs and feet. " The kidney excretions were unnatural and con- tained sediment. I got so worn out with pain that I seemed to have no strength. I tried any number of different remedies, but until I used a box of Doan's Backache Kidney rills, I got very little relief. Before I had finished the contents of the first box, I began to feel so much better that I kept on until I had used four boxes of the pills. By that time I was quite free from the pains in every way, and able to return to my work again. "I have never lost a day's work through illness since I was cured by Doan's pills and I have often told others what great benefit I received, three years ago, from this medicine. (Signed) JOHN CLUCAS." 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9; of all Dealers. Ask for Dthilll".s Backache Kidney Pint, like Mr. Qum had,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>THE WIDENING OF SANKEY BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>I The usual moathly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held on Wednesday in the offices, Bowsey-street, Mr W Backhouse presiding. Others present were :—Messrs 0 Mather, A Brooks, W Furness, W Houghton, the Rev A M Mitchell and Dr Murray, J C Sutton (clerk), 13 S Newcombe (surveyor), 111111 Burnage (assistant surveyor), Dr Sephton (medical °Meer), and F Kerfoot (inspector). A petition of the ratepayers of Rixton and Glaze- brook, requesting the Council to take over a further portion of Holly Bush-lane, was read by the Clerk. Mr A Brooks hoped the Council would look sympa- thetically on the matter. He moved a resolution that the Surveyor be instructed to go fully into the matter, and report at the next moeeing. Mr. G. MATHER seconded and the resolution wss- nand ed.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Messrs. Pueblo' Ltd., chemical manufacturers, Warrington, asking the Council what the rates and expenses would be in connection with buildings at Penketh. Mr, Button said the matter had been referred to the Parish Council. _ _</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED WIDENING 0-i-Si-N-K-ET BRIDGE. The CLERK read a letter from Mr. Harcourt H. Clare (clerk to the Lancashire Csunty Council), with regal to the Sankey Bridges R sad Improvement, which stated with regard to the agreement of the Rural Council and Lsrd Lilford eal Messrs. Clare and Ridgway, that it was never intended ter throw the whole of the land acquired into the roadway as psrtions of the land would be utilized for the purposes of a pevver house, and similar works in con- neotion with the electrically-worked canal bridge. A draft agreement would be nrwarded to the WWII Council between the County and District Council with regard to the grant of £l,OOO to cover the cost of the whole of the lands necessary for the widen- ing under the Highways and Bridges Act. Mr. SUTTON said a further letter had been receive 4 from Mr. Avoline on behalf of the L.N.-W. Railwal Company in which it was stated that Lord Lilford held the right of pre-emption in reference to the land and ft was necessary to obtain from him a letter stating that he did not wish to exercise such right. The COUNTV BRIDGEMASTER (Mr. W. Compton Hell) had also written a letter advising the Council not to, complete the agreement until the points were clearea up. The Railway Company claimed as their property; certain plots, the purchase of which the Rural Councq had negotiated with Lord Lilford and Messrs. Clare an° Ridgway. Mr. SUTTON pointed out that since be received thost letters be had had an interview with Mr. Compton Bell with regard to the nor plea. The plan covered some thing more than he hdd agreed at first to purchase but not to a material extent. It would only be a small sure —a matter of a few pounds. The only point for the Council was to wait until the difference between the parties was settled.</p>
        <p>The SURVEYOR (Mr B S Newcombe) said there were other small pieces of lan] which the Council would have to purchase. Mr. A. Bawl:a said be thought they ought to 11° ahead in the matter. The danger was getting worse every day. TIIE HEALTH REPORT. Dr. SErntrox in submitting his usual monthly report said there had been no deaths in the symotio class. The death rate equilled 4,1 of the estimated population which was a very low rate indeed. There was one criee of enteric fever which was removed on December 11ta to the Warrington Fever Hospital. He hid been informed that afternoon that the patient had since diet PENKETIt COMPLAINT. Dr. MURRAY called attention to the condition of the grids on the main roid from Penketh. Several people had cimplained of a number of Vie grids being stapp )4:1 up. He would like the Council to remind the County' Council of the fact.</p>
        <p>TOWN PLANNING. Mr. A. BROOKS inquired what the Council ware doing in regard to the Town Planning Act. lie thought it would be advisable to hurry forward the scheme. Thsl ought to send out the notices to the owners, free holders, &amp;0., that it was the intention of the Council to apply the scheme as soon as possible. It was no ufle playing with the thing. The CLERK said it was impossible with the present clerical staff to do the work. It was decided to hold a special meeting of the I Council a fortnight hence to discuss the details of the scheme. - I</p>
        <p>In reply to a recommendation of the R3v A g Mitchell in respect of new offices for the Council, the CLERK (Mr Sutton) said the Council had no power to spend money on the erection of new premises. On the suggestion of Mr W FURt•IESS it Was decide&amp; . to leave the matter over until they knew what CO Warrington Board of Guardinna were ching in ail matter.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRM&amp;N said he wished to draw the attention of the C)uncil to the dearth of cottages in the district Under the new bye-laws builders had power to erect suitable cottage property.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Winwick.</head>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>ASYLUM BALL.—What will in all probability be the last County Asylum Ball, was held in the institution on Wednesday evening. There was a good attendance, the beautiful ballroom being crowded with dancers. The asylum orchestra, under the very able leadership of Mr. Haines, provided the music for dancing, which was kept up until about 2 a.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Daresbury.</head>
        <p>Daresbury.</p>
        <p>PARISH ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL CLUB DANCE.— A successful dance in connection with this club was held on Friday last in the Council School, Dareabury. The attendance numbered about sixty. Dancing was indulged in from 8 p.m. to 2 a.m., to music supplied Mr. George W Lomas. The room had been tastefully decorated for the occasion with flags &amp;c. by Miss C Foulkes, E Hankey and It P Booth. The duties of were carried out by Messrs It P Booth and A Ryder. The catering was in the hands of Mr and Mrs W Garnett, of the Ring o' Bells Hotel, and gave every satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Warburton.</head>
        <p>Warburton.</p>
        <p>,0113 RESIDENT'S DEATII.—The eoath occurred On "eoember 28th, at Church Green, Warburton, of Sarah Wright, who had resided in the village for over 70 years, Sod had attained the ripe age of 85 years. She was vvarmly devoted to the service of the Beckett family, late of Overtown Farm, Warburton, every one of whom have pre-deceased her. She was most highly respected and will long be remembered for her kindness of heart. The funeral took place at Warburton old church, on M°ndav, when every mark of respect was shown to her Memory..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Moore.</head>
        <p>Moore.</p>
        <p>NEW YEAR'S TEA.—On Monday, the scholars and teachers of the Moore Wesleyan Sunday school had ,their annual New Year's Tea. At 5 o'clbck shout one !Mildred scholars sat down to an excellent tea prepared la the schoolroom by the teachers and lady friends. At half-past six an entertainment was given by a party e_f sixteen children from the Frodsham branch of the -National Children's Home and Orphanage, which r4flected very great credit upon the youthful artistes. elle veil' commendable item was a recitation by Freddy rose, who is 5 years old. After the entertainment, A.,znes were heartily indulged in until ten o'clock, when the Party dispersed after a very enjoyable evening. WESLEY GUILD.—On Wednesday evening the first 'uevotional meeting of the Tear w.is held in connection with the Wesley Guild. Uniortunately the inclement nature of the weather prevented there being a good attendance. Mr. Lawton presided, and the Rev. W. A. Beckett, superintendent of the Warrington Circuit, gave a very helpful New Year address on "Going one better.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>ANNI:AL TF,A. AND ENTERTAINMENT.—The annual Parochial tea and concert was held in the schoolroom (nt Thursday. Owing to the wretched weather the attendance was not as large as usual. An excellent pro- Framme had been arranged, and was well carried out °Y the Orford Concert Party. Miss D Lawton acquitted herself well in her two songs " Swallows " and " Far frora the land," being recalled each time and respond- ing to the first encore with " Roses." Miss J Edwards ,Was in good voice, and gave a pleasing rendering of ome back to me." The warmest encore of e evening 'was acoorded to MiSEO9 Lawton and c'dwards for the duet " Life's dream is o'er." J Gibson was recalled for his two songs "True till death," and "The King's own" and sang for an encore, Jack's the boy." Mr W White was well received in hie two songs, "Mary of Argyll" and " My sweetheart when a boy." Mr F Richmond maintained his repute- with some excellent violin solos aud the trio with Mr Wheelhou P. se, cello, and Mr Wilson, piano, was a very e9Pular item. Mr Wheelhouse also played a cello solo. phtter, air R Barlow caused roars of laughter with his quaint recitations and his songs, " Logic end " Down 13Y the farmyard gate." Mr Wilson officiated at the Piano. The duties of chairman were carried cut by Mr P Densharn.</p>
        <p>Cu!client • 7.lvlss GREEN INDEPENDENT METHODISTS.—Th- qiirts-sixth anniversary of the Band of Hope in cornec lion with this chapel was held on New Year's Day end Was a huge success. Tea was provided in the school- rooso after which a concert was held prcsided over by Ir. George Fenton. The Secretary read the usual report and gave a brief account of the work done during the past year shewing the success which had attended the efforts put forth by the members. The concert items were long and varied but they provided good entertainment for atl present as there was Something suitable for the various tastes of the audience. Part songs were very ably rendered by the Choir and a pianoforte solo was very well played by Mr If Griffiths, who also acted as accompanist. Recitations 'were given by Edna Cooper, Sarah Watson. May Lambert, Kate Goodwin Alice Barrow, B Fenton, Bessie Barrow W H Howarth, John Duffy j'an W Sutto'n, J Sutton and K Barton. Vumorous duets were given by Percy Cleworth and red Dodd, W Kenyon, and J R Fletcher. Songs were ale° well sung by Miss Puncorn and Miss Barton. Two very interesting sketches were given by Gladys Cooper and others and Ruth Snelson and others and dialogues Were dramatically rendered by Miss Bickerstaffe and of others and lkfr E Barton and others. Trios by members the choir created much amusement and a musical a2Onologue by Miss Griffiths was much apprsciated. olbere was a very good audience composed principally the younger element and the concert throughout •qts very enjoyable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>LAII3L-11, PARTY.—The usual fortnightly meeting ot lila "eve was held in the Wesleyan schoolroom on Wednesday evening. Mr H Shaw was the speaker and,took for his subject, "An Englishman's Home," ZIA demonstrated the absolute necessity of better for the working classes. He was listened to great interest. A good and educAtiva dig- -118510t] followed. Mr C Vilozencroft presided. ~.WEsLEvaN Cii uncrr.—The usual watch night service "as held in the above on Sunday night at 11 0 when a companyll dent minister, was present. The real Rev ' by kliddlewood occupied the pulpit. Appropriate IN roes were sung, the service being over just as the ew Year dawned. WRsLEYAN C111:12( Ir.—The annual social usually held New Year's Eve took place on Saturday last, in the ft,.'4‘e,YEln Sunday school. About 90 attended. The Cs'angements were in the. hands of the Guild Social nalnittee and the Men's Bible Class the former being ..3P onsible (as in other years) for the refreshments and i't,e latter for the concert and games. The opening St44cl Or the concert was a pianoforte solo by Mrs. 8 Bvanson which was given in her usual capable style. 4Zas were rendered by Miss Dodds and Mr. J. Ireland ea Nvero heartily applauded. An interesting musical teab2yetition was arranged. Mr. H. Shaw gave the p i;lng and Mrs. Stevenson was the accompanist. The .es r were won by thelklisses D. M. Alderley, S ;•:r eenhalgh, and B. Scott. Refreshments were then ,w'aandeet round, ani followed by games. Mr. 0. Carman at! ebsirman. The gathering broke up about 10.30, ter a very enjoyable evening. TANNERY BAND'S NOTABLE SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Tannery Band achieved a notable 4 .11C0e, 11. sat the nineteenth annual Eisteddfod and Brass 71,11,„e_Contest at Colw3n Bay, on Monday. " the "" greater part of the afternoon was taken up with of lleYeral competitions on brass instruments, in each I Pros nch there was a numerous entry. First on the I Nvht;aranle was the brass band march competition, in sti' nt the following eight bands took part :—Port rth!ilis_h.t Silver Prize Band (conductor. Mr J A Fidler), 13.2, 6ilver Band (Mr \V Rimmer), Penketh Tannery \Varrington (Mr .1 A Greenwood), Silverdale ' Prize Bend (Mr E Booth), Northop Brats Band J. Griffith.), Llandudno Town Band Lucio Traversi), Connell, s Quay Silver Litz° Bard (Ir. .1. A. Greenwood), and ' j 0 Silver Prize Band (Mr. Angus Holden.) 'ee march was played on the platform, and not as i,t,retofore actually on the march in the street. The test Illece was "'flls Saracen," by J. Robertson. endelssohn'a " St. Paul" was set as the test piece in the . • instrumental quartet cont m est in which tin, quartets participated. Later the" selec' Si: contest for brass bands was held, the bands nat'n.t, "Theve Playing Edward Newton's charming fantasia, be_land of the Shamrock." The competition lasted three hours, and the audience for the evening thju,ng was congreeating round the pay gates before last band began its performance. All the bands remarkably well, and the adjudicator, Mr. J. en'R:igton, awarded the march prize, the quartet prize, Te'briTe first prize in the selection contest to Penketh the , Warrington. Connah's Quay were second in atter event. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
