      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Preston Brook.</head>
        <p>Preston Brook.</p>
        <p>I TRACTION ENGINE SMASH.—On Tuesday a traction engine, with a furniture van attached, belonging to Messrs. Williams and Sons, Manchester, was travelling down the ste9n declivity at Preston Brook, on the Warrington to Frodsham road,wben,in the fog, it missed an awkward turn and crashed into a side bridge crossing the Bridgewater Canal. Thirty feet of stonework was smashed away, and the engine bung suspended with two wheels revolving over the canal. The driver had a lucky escape. The bridge is one of the most dangerous of its kind in the county and it is said that within a few years a dozen mishaps may have occurred at this spot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>PALISH COUNCIL MEETING. The usual monthly meeting was held on Wednesday evening in the ouncil schools. There were present : Messrs F R Roberts (chairman), A Lister•Esye, F Fox, J Bradford, F Ellison, W Lomas, J Carter, N Cooke, jun., Dr Hutt and Charles H Hoult (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>_SEWERAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>THE _SEWERAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from Mr. George F. Ashton, clerk to the Rural District Council of Runcorn as follows : Grappenhall, Latchford Without, and Thel- wall Sewerage. I beg to inform you that at a meeting of this Council held yesterday, it was resolved : (1) That a scheme of sewerage and sewage disposal for the parishes of Grappenhall, Latchford Without and Thelwall, as prepared by the Council's engineers, Messrs James Diggle and Son, of Heywood, be accepted and that application be made to the Local Government Board for sanction to a loan of .C 18,500 to be repayable by half-yearly instalments of principal extending over a period of 30 years. (2) That each of the Parish Councils of Grappenhall, Latchford Without and Thelwall be notified of the District Council's intention to carry cut the works and they be informed that if they can arrange for a joint meeting, the Council's engineer will be asked to attend and fully explain his scheme. I shall be glad to bear in due course whether your Parish Council can arrange for a joint meeting as proposed and if so where such meeting can be most conveniently held. As regards time. B:c., about 7.30 p.m. on a Friday will suit the Council's engineer best to attend.—Dated. December 20th, 1911._</p>
        <p>Mr. Kaye said that he had seen the Chairman of the Parish Council of The!wall, and he bad suggested two dates whereon they might hold a joint meeting, namely Janury 11th and 18th, 1912. He Mr Kaye) then pro- posed and Mr Lomas seconded :—" That a joint meet- ing of the three parishes named be held on either January 11th or 18th." This was carried. Mr Kayo again moved and Mr Roberts seconded : "That this Council would be under a great obligation if Mr G F Ashton (R.D.C.) could see his way to attend the joint meeting along with the surveyor, as a number of legal questions were likely to be asked." This was agreed to. Mr Kaye suggested that a public subscription might be formed, with a view to getting expert opinion on the scheme as to whether it was the best scheme they could get for the outlay involved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>RATEPAYERS' MEETING.</head>
        <p>RATEPAYERS' MEETING.</p>
        <p>After a long discussion, the Chairman (Mr Roberts) undertook to call a meeting of the ratepayers as soon as the date of the joint meeting had been fixed.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>ANGLIN() CLUE HOT POT SEPPER.--:The members of the angling club in connection with St. Thomas' Men's Class B concluded the season with a hot-pot supper on Saturday night and a most enjoyable time was spent. Mr. Joseph Starkey supplied excellent fare. The season has been a moderate one and matches have taken place every other Saturday, many of the members carrying off prizes. OLD FOLK'S TREAT.—The old people over sixty years were entertained at their annual New Year's treat in the Church Room on Monday. About eighty- three persons sat down to an excellent tea, their wants being attended to by a large body of parish workers. After tea an entertainment was held, presided over by the Vicar. A capital programme was provided by the Bros. Evans and the Misses Jones, of Liverpool, whose prformance was highly appreciated, The choir boys also beautifully rendered a number of carols. At the close, on the the proposition of the Vicar, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to all who had contributed to the success of the evening. Before leaving each man was presented with tobacco and a mince pie and the women with packets of tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Com- mittee was held on Thursday evening in the Hall Cafe. Mr George Pilling presided, and there were also present :—Messrs J H Clarke, II J Westbrook, G Brown, W Maclntyre, J Whitley, J Hallows, C W Hallows, J McDermid, J Wood, G F Ashton (clerk) and J swinton (surveyor). NEW YEAR'S GOOD WISHES. The Chairman, at the outset, said that as that was their first meeting this year he had pleasure in wishing his fellow-members a happy and prosperous New Year; might they long continue to meet together. The members cordially reciprocated the good wishes. BLOCK ED SEWERS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Manchester Ship Canal Company in regard to the Twenty-Steps syphon Complaint had been made that it was working irregu- larly. The Ship Canal Company replied "In regard to this matter we hope to be in a position to communicate with you." Mr. Clarke said that on Wednesday, 20th December they had a heavy fall of rain and when he want over the bridge he naturally expected to find the syphon pouring out the overflow from the sewer. To his surprise he found it perfectly dry. He thought at the time there must be something wrong with their drains and heard afterwards there were cellars flooded in Stockton Heath, and be thought someone had been remiss in not having the syphon open. They ought to have some understanding as to whether or not the Surveyor should see that the gyphon was open wh&lt; n there was a heavy fail of rain. However, he had since learned that it was not the fault of their drainage that there was a flood. Ile was told that a cellar on the Warrington side of the Causeway had 14 inches of water in it, backed up from the sewer. There was a high tide and a" fresh ' ill the river, so that the block- ing up of the Warrington sewer probably resulted in the blocking up of the Stockton Heath sewer. It was not a question of the capacity of their drains, but of the sewage being " backed up" from Warrington. Then there was the question of the valve being backed, Mr. Brown asked if it did not work automatically. Mr. J. Hallows said they had to get a key to open it. It was carelessness or the part of someone in not seeing that it was opened. The Chairman thought the Surveyor should see that it was open when there was heavy rain. The Su.veyor stated that the valve had been covered with soil and had not been in use for some time. Mr. Clarke proposed that the Ship Canal Company be written to asking them to have the valve put into and kept in working order, Mr J Hallows seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE RATES. A latter was read from the deputy-clerk to the County Council acknowledging the receipt of a resolu- tion from the Committee in regard to the increase in the rates and stating that it would be "submitted to the Finance Committee in due course.' Mr. 11. J. Westbrook mentioned that the Parish Council hod passed a siruilor resolution to the Parochial Committee, but probably more stringent in its terms, calling attention to the increase of the County rates, particularly in regard to the erection of public build- ings and the salailes of officiale. Mr. Whitley added that the overseers of the various parishes had joined in a protest against the County Council's extravagance, especially in the education department, and there would very likely be a general committee of overseers formed to take further action</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>UNPAVED STREETS.</head>
        <p>UNPAVED STREETS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Local Government Board sanctioning the paving of certain streets. Mr. Brown asked if Alexandra-road was scheduled.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: No, Viotoria-road is. Mr. Brown said there were many complaints about the state of Alexandra-road, The Chairman said the owners were written to a week or two ago stating that unless something was done the road would have to be put under the Private Street Works Art. Mr. Brown said he was told that some of the owners had seid they would do nothing. He asked if more pressure could not be brought to bear on them. After further discussion it was decided to notify the owners that unless the road was put into a satisfactory state of repair within 28 days, it be scheduled under the Private Street Works Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LIG lITING.</head>
        <p>LIG lITING.</p>
        <p>LAMPS OUT AT MIDNIGHT. Mr. Whitley submitted a report of the Lighting Committee in regard to street lamps. The committee recommended that the lights of 28 lamps in various parts of the township should be extinguished at twelve midnight. The lamp in the Square, it was recom- mended, should be reduced from three lights to two, and that one of the lights should be put out at twelve o'clock. It was stated that Mr. Haddock, of the Warrington Corporation Gas works, bad suggeseed that lamps could be regulated by clocks. Mr. Westbrook inquired where the saving would come</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarke said that they had yet to ascertain from the Corporation what the saving would be. If it was only trifling they would not go further with the proposal. Mr. Brown said it had been thought they might save a penny in the £ on the rates, but if the saving would not affect the rates they would drop the proposal. Mr. J. Hallows : Nothing will be done till we hear from Mr. Haddock ? The Chairman : No. Mr. Brown mentioned that Mr. Pemberton wanted a lamp in Alexandra-road. The question was referred to the Lighting Committee. WHICH ? WHEATSTIE AVE% MUSHROOMS, OR CHAMPAGNE CORES? Mr. H. J. Westbrook humorously remarked that the Surveyor had passed him a sheet of official notepaper and he should like to know what the device on it repre- sented wheatsheaves or mushrooms ? (Laughter). Mr. Clarke thought they were more like champagne corks. (More laughter). An inspection of the device seemed to satisfy the members that Mr. Clarke's description was not wide of the mark.</p>
        <p>Lymm. LYMM AND DISTRICT WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCII- TION.—A pleasant evening was spent in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom on Thursday. An instructive address was given by Mr. Arthur H. Weller, of Man- chester, the subject being" How to abolish taxes on industry." Mr. G. L. Welford occupied the chair during the evening. Refreshments were provided during the interval and these found a ready reception from all. _ .</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH Cnotu.—The church choir boys bad their annual treat on Thursday evening in 51rs Walker's entertaining room, Booth's Hill. The Rev G B Thurston was present and helped to make the evening most pleasant by joining in the variolts games with the boys. The patty broke up about 10 o'clock, after having given cheers to Mr W Cooke, the choirmaster. . _</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—A social evening was held on Wednesday in the schoolroom, when there was an excellent attendance. Games and competitions were most heartily indulged in. Refreshments were pro- vided during the interval and these soon disappeared from view in the usual way. The evening came to a close soon after ten o'clock. CONGREGATIONAL CHRISTIAN SET:VICE BAND.—The meeting was very well attended on Monday evening last, when a good address was given by Mr George Wiliam A solo was rendered by Miss Annie Miller. Mr James Hewitt occupied the chair. The meeting concluded with several prayers from the members. WATCH-NIGHT SERVICE.—There was an impressive wind-up to the year 1911 in the Congregational Church, on Sunday evening when the United Watch-night Service took place. The largest number of people attended that has been known in the history of Lyrr.m. A most spirited address was given by Mr G L Welford, on the " Great unrest of the country during the closing year." It was most interesting and was quite apart from the ordinary addresses of to-day. The ser- vice was bright and inspiring. Mr JD B Smith pre- sided, and the Rev 11 W B Whiteway took part in the service.</p>
        <p>LYNIM DEVELOPMENT COMPANY SMOKING CONCERT, —The Lymm Estate Development Company, Lt 1, entertained a large company of tenants and friends of the Beechwood and Oughtrington Estates at a dinner at the Plough Hotel on Wednesday evening, and the party afterwards adjourned to the Drill Hall where a smoking concert was held. The programme was exceptionally interesting, and was provided by the K.F. and R. Club (consisting of members of the staff of Messrs. Knight, Frank and Rutley, who acted as Sir W. H. Lever's agents in the recent sale of some of the properties on the estates). Songs were rendered by Mr. Scott Joynt, Mr. James Foye and Madame Annie Radford, while Mr. Lindon Wyatt admirably fulfilled the role of humorous enter- tainer. The second part of the programme consisted of a pot-pourri of comic items by " The Nuts," (Elsa Butler, Beatrice Keys, Eric Butler, Bruce Ker, and Vincent Clarke). The whole entertainment was of a high order,and met with enthusiastic appreciation from a large audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS. H. S. PAGES—We regret to have to report the death of Mrs. H. S. Pager who passed sway late on Tuesday night. The circumstances were particularly sad, one little son having died only a week or tv o ago, and his sister having been so seriously ill at the same time that her life was despaired of. The deceased lady was suffering in hospital from typhoid fever and for a time progressed favourably. A relapse, however, occurred at the week end and she sank and died as stated. Much sincere sympathy is felt for Mr. Page and family in this sad and double bereavement. The funeral tooklplace at Warrington Cemetery, yester- day, and was conducted by the Revs. J. Orange Bennett and E. Morgan. There were many beautiful wreaths.</p>
        <p>CHRIST CHURCH—PARISH TEA AND CONCERT.—On Wednesday evening the Church of England schoolroom was completely filled by those who atrended the annual Parish tea. A substantial repast, provided as usual under the careful superintendence of Mrs. Grounds, was greatly enjoyed. The Vicar (Rev. G. W. Dickinson) presided at the meeting, and iii an interesting speech expreesed his pleasure in having so large an audience present. He gave a resume of the events of the past year, and said how delighted they were to have the presence of Mr. and Mrs. Henry Antrobus' after the rather serious illness each had sustained subsequent to the celebration of their golden wedding a few months ago. Referring to the Garden Fete, the rev, gentleman said it had this year been a greater success than ever. The calls on the proceeds obtained had been exception- ally heavy, owing principally to the alterations to the schoolroom, necessary to bring the premises up to Board of Education requirements. The musical part of the programme was in the capable hands of tho Orpheus Quartette Party, Messrs L. Aukland,Walker, Critchley and Cook. Their unaccompanied rendering of several part songs greatly delighted the audience and these, together with several solos rendered by them made the musical part of the meeting an unqualified success. A dialogue entitled " An unexpected entertainment " was given with success by a number of elder girl scholarls. The entertainment concluded with a dramatic sketch "The £5OO Prize " by the following members of the Padgate Amateur Dramatic Society :—The Misses Elsie Lunt and Vera Collin and Messrs. S. Denham, F. Hardman and G. Houghten.arhe parts were well sustained and the performance was much appreciated. Dancing was indulged in until about midnight. Mr. Blackwell was the accompanist throughout. On Thursday the scholars' tea and entertainment was held. Mr C Turner, the superintendent, gave his report of Sunday school work remarking particularly on the increase of punctuality and regularity as evidenced by the number of prizes to he presented. Mr Alfred Bates was responsible for two violin solo played in a very creditable manner. The Misses Heath and Turner received a well merited encore for their duet. The 1 Vicar was loudly applauded for his amusing songs Doddle-oddle " and Tootle-turn-tee." Come back to Erin" was sweetly sung by Miss May Turner. The dialogue given on the previous evening "An unexpected entertainment " WS3 repae I. A large a 'miser of wall chosen books were presented by the Vicar, as prizes. Several of the scholars received two—one for unbroken attendance and use for learning end repeating collects each Sunday. Dancing cono'ncleel evening-s enter- tainment. Mr. Critchley was M.C. and Mr Wainwright accompanist SUNDAY SCHOOL Tka. MEETiscs.—On Thursday evening the annual tea party and entertainment in con- nection with the Wesleyan school was held. About 100 persons sat down to a substantial tea, and that number was greatly augmented for the entertainment which followed, over which the Rev E Morgan presided. The report of the Sunday School Council presented by the Secretary (Mr A Ward) showed that the number of scholars on the books at the end of the year was 235- 150 males and 136 females. The number of officers and teachers is now 39-17 males and 22 females. During the year 60 depositors have contributed the sum of £lO6 to the Savings Club and that amount with interest has been repaid to them. The officers elected for the year are : Superintendents, Messrs F Wright and H E Roscollor ; secretaries, Messrs Arthur Ward and Leonard Bennett ; librarian and club secretary, Mr H Brough ; ;treasurer, Mr James Dandy ; registration secretaries, Messrs Stanley Ezard and George Brough ; auditor, Mr P Houghton Bennett. The entertainment was of a miscellaneous character, a duet by the Misses C 'reinstall and E Fryer being well received. Miss Moston, of Lymm, delighted the audi- ence with her two recitations An inventor's wife" and Barney." Mr W Bate saug "A jolly old cavalier " and " England, mighty England " in first class style. A recitation " A leak in the dyke," by our promising local reciter, Miss Amy Tomlin, wee as might be expected, very well rendered. Mr Handier made his debut at Padgate by contributing in a pleasing manner, The Flight of Ages" and " Come back to Erin." Three young scholars. Elsie Seed, John Cook, and Charles Dakin recited respectively " Barbara Frietche," " The jvind in a frolic," and " The Enchanted Shirt."</p>
        <p>Miss Rigby's beautiful soprano voice was much appre- ciated in the rather difficult song "The Fairies Lullaby." Time did not allow her to sing the second song down in her name, but we hope to have the pleasure of hearing her again at no very distant date. Mis Sutton was warmly applauded for her song "Angus Macdonald," and as an encore sang "Home sweet Home." She is the possessor of a powerful contralto voice, and, on the higher notes particularly, purity and richness of tone place her far above the average soloist. Two dialogues by senior scholars entitled "Paddy's mistake" and " Henpecked " provided much interest and amusement. Mr. S. H. Morgan gave a sLort but very interesting and instructive address on" Boys." The speech was packed full of suggestions and information and was listenad to with careful attention. The accompaniments were ' played by Air. Levi Richardson and Mrs W. Bate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LABOURERS AT LOGGERHEADS. At Runcorn, on Monday, John Oars was summoned by James Burke for assault. The parties are Irish farm labourers in the employ of Mr. Wade, of Aston Grange. Burke alleged that, he entrusted half-a-sovereign to I Gars on Saturday night, out of which Gars was to pay 6s. 7d. for some groceries for the prosecutor and take 3s. that was due to him. When the parties met in the " shanty " late in the evening, Gars had not brought the groceries back, and when asked for the money abused and kicked Burke. Burke was asked if Gara worked for Mr. Wade, and replied "Not now—he's here now." (Laughter.) He added : " He's no countryman of mine. I'm ashamed of him. He's not an Englishman, nor an Irishman either."—Gara, who alleged that Burke attacked him with a poker, was fined ss.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN R URAL COUNCIL. At Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. T. Wright presiding. A I` RODSH AM_ ROAD. _</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board -t;;Ii-e-d- for the Council's observations upon a letter from Mr. Radford Shawcross, of Cromer's Lake, Overton, in which he complained that the Rural Council and the Frodsham Lordship Council, kept deferring the matter of the condition of the road at Cromer's Lake, leading to his and other houses. "We pay our equal share in rates and taxes and get no returns, but simply have to plod through mud and water, which is very disheartening and not just. Just immune our childreu going through this every day to school, getting their feet wet and not being able to change till they get home at night." The Clerk said the road in question was complained of last year and the year before, and was referred to the Highways executive committee. They reported that the road was not repairable by the Council and they could not recommend its being taken over by the Council. The Parish meeting of Newton had also called attention to the same road. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Horton said the local owners had offered to do something to the road. The Chairman : It would be very expensive to take this road over. _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward said the Highways Committee had carefully considered the matter. No definite offer had been received from the owners, but such an offer would no doubt be considered if adequate. He moved that they reply that the road was not repairable by the public. In reply to Mr. Horton he said, speaking from memory, he believed the estimatad cost of making the road was about £l,OOO. This was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CASTNER KELLNICR AND ELICTRICITY.</head>
        <p>CASTNER KELLNICR AND ELICTRICITY.</p>
        <p>_ A letter was read from the Castner Kellner Alkali Co., Ltd., giving notice of their intention to lay dis- tributing mains for the supply of electrical energy in certain streets in Weston. The supply to be given was • 230 volt direct current one. It was their intention to commence the opening of the streets after the expire- ticn of one month from the date of the notice.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the company had power to lay the cable as was intended up Banke-road. SURVEYOR'S ILLNESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Dig-gie--WWt-e--staiing t-13-;:i his brother had been operated upon and was recovering from his illness. He would be confined to his room for three weeks. . . .</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Woodward suggested that during Mr. Diggle's absence the road roller should be utilised on Mr. Swinton's area and Mr. Swinton supervise Mr. Diggle's area as far as possible. This was agreed to. The Chairman expressed their regret at Mr. Diggle's illness and a hope for his speedy recovery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>OPPOSITION TO THE -BRINE BILL.</head>
        <p>OPPOSITION TO THE -BRINE BILL.</p>
        <p>The special committee appointed to go into the pro- posed Mid-Cheshire Brine Bill reported having met on December 27th under the chairmanship of Mr. Linaker. After considering the provision of the Bill it was resolved" that it be a recommendation to the District Council that the Bill be strenuously opposed by the Council and that the Clerk be authorised to take all such steps as he may con3ider neces-ary for the preparation of a petition against the Bill. " This was agreed to.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. At Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. J. Spruce presiding. CHRISTMAS GIFTS.</p>
        <p>The thanks of the Board were tendered to the follow- ing donors of Christmas cherr :—Mrs Boston, Halton Grange, half-pound tea to each of the poor of Halton and Balton Brook ; Mrs Posnett, The Mount, Runcorn, half-pound tea to each of the poor of Weston and Weston Point ; Mr S Taylor, Rydal Mount, Runcorn ; lcwt of coal to each of the poor of Runcorn ; Mr T W Wright, Frodsham, lcwt of coal to each of the poor of Halton and Halton Brook.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>DISTRIBUTION' OF RELIEF,</head>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION' OF RELIEF,</p>
        <p>The Clerk produced a new order issued by the Local Government Board in regard to relief. The chief alterations appeared to be that the compulsory case paper system should be adopted in every Union. Alterations had been made with reference to periods of relief. In future in now cases coming before the Board for the first time relief could only be granted for six weeks after which the case would have to be recon- sidered and an increased period of indoor or outdoor relief cou'd then be allowed. A point he would have to consid-r with the relieving officers would be as to whether the relief order book system should be con- tinued or another system adopted.</p>
        <p>DELEGATE TO POOR LAW CONFERENCE, ETC. The Secretary to the North Western Poor Law Con- ference wrote asking the Board to appoint two repre- sentatives as members of a committee to consider what better provision could be made by Boards of Guardians in the North Western District for the care and treatment of the feeble-minded and to prepare a report of their recommendation to the conference and a meeting to be specially convened. On the motion of Mr. Wright, Messrs. Pilling and Woodward were appointed RS representatives of the Board. Messrs. Pilling and Mclntyre were appointed delegates to the Central Poor Law Conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MOVER COMM ITTER.</head>
        <p>MOVER COMM ITTER.</p>
        <p>The House Committee -r-e-c-Onl-r-u-eniled that votes of thanks be accorded to all the donors of toys to the children. It was recommended that Mrs E Wormald, of Liverpool, be appointed portress at £2O a year and the usual allowances. The application of the Rev A. L. Temple for an increase of his salary as chaplain to the Workhouse was comidered and it was resolved that the application be deferred until after the removal of the children from the House. The committee recommended that candidates for the position of Superintendent Nurse be interviewed at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>RELIEF.</head>
        <p>RELIEF.</p>
        <p>............ It was reported that the number of inmates was 196 as compared with 191 last year and the numter of vagrants returned 93 as against 72.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>CONGRATULATIONS TO SHI. JOSEPH BEECHAM.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of St. Helens. Alderman Sir Joseph Beecham, spent a busy morning on Monday, visiting the eleven centres where poor children of the borough were being entertained to the annual New Year's breakfast, and on all hands the greatest pleasure and satisfaction was expressed at the bestowal of • the honour. Sir Joseph is certainly ore of the most open-handed and generons of men, but he is also of a retiring disposi- tion. He is now serving his third year as Mayor of the borough, and no more popular Mayor ever presided at the Town Hall. The Mayor was accompanied by Mr J. B. Leach, J.P., Mr. Rigby Swift, M.P., and Mrs Swift, and he was most warmly cheered and congratu- lated by those in charge of the free breakfast and by the children themselves. Sir Joseph also received a large number of telegrams and messages of congratula- tion from London and other places. SUICIDE OF A DEPRESSED WIDOWER. LITTLE GIRL'S TRAGIC DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>At the inquest on James Kennedy (49), 52, Morley- street, St. Helens, n gas producer man at the glass works, on Tuesday, it was stated that, since the death of his wife twelve months ago, Kennedy had been depressed. Margaret Kennedy, a ten-year-old daughter, said that during Christmas week her father had been off work and drinking. On Saturday night, when under the influence of drink, be told her he would either cut his throat or drown himself. Soon afterwards he went to bed with his little eon, aged five years. Next morning, about half-past 'even o'clock, when she got up, she went into the kitchen. She found her father lying on the sofa with a rubber tube in his mouth, the other end being fastened to the gas bracket, and the gas tn. nad on full. William Travis, 51, Morley-street, said he was called in, and found the man quite dead and cold. The gas was turned on, but the gas was supplied through a slot meter, awl the supply was exhausted. A verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>UNDERFED CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>UNDERFED CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>FREE HOLIDAY MEALS AT ST. HELENS. At the St. llelens Education Committee. on Wednes- day, Alderman Bishop referred to a minute of the Elementary Education Committee deciding to notify the general public that meals were being provided for underfed children at the schools during the holidays and that the cost could not be put on the rates. He</p>
        <p>asked how many children had been fed, and what was the probable cost. The Deputy Chairman (Mr. J. lleaton), who presided, said that about 130 meals had been provided per day, and the cost to cover the holidays was about .CG, MI defrayed by voluntary contribntions. Alderman Bishop thought if it was any considerable sum, the members ought to know so as to open the pockets of people disposed to give.—The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>CARTERS AND BAD DEBT.</head>
        <p>CARTERS AND BAD DEBT.</p>
        <p>County Court Action at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Stanger' K.C., a claim for 48 12s 4d was brought against Robert Nelson, a carter, of 174, Lovely-lane, by the Wigan Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., for coal supplied, and £1 5s for damages for breach of con- tract in leaving without notice. Defendant counter claimed for £l, money paid in error, and 5s promised him for the building of a wall. Mr. Allen (of Peace and Ellis, Wigan,) appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. T. S. Steel defended. The case for the plaintiffs was that Nelson was engaged as a carter to take out coal on one of their lurrios, at a weakly minimum wage of £1 5s and a commission upon all coal sold over a certain tonnage. When the coal was taken out from the yard its value was debited against him and he had to kring back all money for the coal sold. A certain amount of latitude was shown to carters because they sometimes co - tracted debts, but a list was never supplied to the com- pany of the names of the customers. The noticEs ad not been sent out to the customers exc,pt on one or two occasions when the defendant asked one of the clerks to issue notice. The clerk had no authority to do so. Mr. Brandreth, local manager, for the Wigan Coal and Ir,n Co„ Ltd., said that the coal sold by carters was not entered in the customers' accounts. They did not know to whom the coal had been sold. Mr. J. E. Horabin, clerk, said the defendant was responsible for the coal he sold. In reply to Mr. Steel, witness said carters had contracted bad debts during the last few years. It was brought to the notice of the carters that the company were in no way respon- sible for bad debts._</p>
        <p>_ By his Honour : Have you known a case where pro- ceedings have been taken to discover such money? Witness : Yes' some years ago. Mr. Allen said he had conducted several cases for the company when carters had been sued for the recovery of certain monies. He remembered a case heard at Warrington where the circumstances were identical and a verdict was given for the plaintiffs. The defendant, examined by Mr. Steel, said he com- menced working for the company at Warrington in 1809. He was never told by Mr. Brandreth that he would be responsible for bad debts. The names of his customers were supplied to the office for the half-yearly audit.</p>
        <p>His Honour, in giving judgment, said he thought the case for the plaintiffs failed on the point of the terms of the agreement. It was admitted by Mr. Horabin that from time to time certain allowances were made and that where they were satitfiad it was a bad debt they did not look to the carter to pay. Another fact which bore out that statement was that certain customers were circularised. He was not satisfied nor would he be justified in inferring that there was an applied contract. That being so, the case for the plaintiffs failed, except with regard to the £1 ss. He gave judgment for the plaintiffs for £1 ss. and judg- ment for the defendant for ss. No costs were allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WARRINGTON SOCIALIST SOCIETY.</head>
