        <p>Mr. J. Laurence Toole gave a lecture on " lbsPnism and Socialism," Mr. Moses Baritz occupying the chair. The lecturer stated that the chief aim of drima was—or ought to be—to portray on the stage what transpired in actual life. Ibsen could not tolerate cant or hypocrisy, and he endeavoured to show in his dramas the hollow- ness of the numerous institutions, traditions and characters. Ibsen's characters, like those of Dickens, could be found in every•day life, and like the latter Ibsen had cleverly exposed the fraudulent. Mr. Toole illustrated his remarks chiefly by outlining the drama " Pillars of Socirty." Ibsen wrote with a purpose, his fight being for justice and truth and freedom for all.</p>
