      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Visit of Moses Baritz.</head>
        <p>Visit of Moses Baritz.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon last at tbe above Society's rooms Wilderspool, Mr. Moses Baritz of Manchester delivered a lecture the " Life and Works of Richard Wagner. Mr. 3. L. Toole of Manchester occupied the chair. There was a large appreciative audience, every seat being occupied. The lecture was unique for the fact that it was interspersed with musical numbers taken f rem" Lohengrin"" Tanhauser " "Meistersingers' and the "Flying Dutchman." The pianist was Mr. Albert Sherry and the tenor soloist Mr. Fred Crompton both of Manchester.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer explained that the early life of Richard Wagner was one of misery, pain and tribulation. He was born in the year 1813 during the Napoleonic Wars, when distress was so rampart in Leipsig. He was the youngest of a family of nine and was sent to school at Dresden. He learnt the piano and also indicated a knowledge of poetry. He was a great lover of English literature, particularly Shakespeare's works. One of his earliest attempts at writing was a mixture of " Hamlet " and" King Lear " in which 42 people died in the first act and Wagner had to let their ghosts appear to complete the play. He was influenced by Beethoven when he was twenty ond got a position as chorus master. He composed the opera 'Die Few," which was to have been produced on a certain date, but the manager refused to allow it to proceed. He fell in love with an actress, Willielmina Planer, and married 1836. Still be bad bad luck and the young musician had to go to London and travelled by sailing boat, lie became very sea sick, and hie wife—who was in delicate health at the time—tended him with devoted attention. They encountered a storm and Wagner was so impressed, that the "Flying Dutchman " overture was the outcome. This overture being a reflex of the happenings on the North Sea, the thunder and howling winds being depicted. His next opera " Tanhauser " is one of the popular operas to-day Air. Baritz fully explained the opera and Mr. Sherry played most of the best known tunes therein. `Lotierigrin' was next dealt with, the lecturer explaining the chief incidents connected with the opera. Mr. Crompton rendered the " Prize song" from the " Die Aleistersingers," the "Swan song" and also Lohengrin's "Narration." The audience evidently appreciated these renderings, and at the close of the latter particulaily, gave the artiste quite a hearty ovation. Mr. Sherry's contributions were listened to with great pleasure, his cleverness receiving repeated deserving applause. The lecturer proved himself thoroughly intimate with his subject. His clever interpretations, ready wit, and pleasant mannerisms added not a little to the tone of things, and his arranging of the various items and manipulation of the whole lecture was a feature of the afternoon. Mr. Moses Barit2 can certainly be complimented on his presenting such a blending of education and enjoyment. In the evening</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
