      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>Guardians and Christmas Fare. Sir,— ilong with your correspondent who styles himself " Oulcoker," I have frequently beeu amazed, when reading the fortnightly lopes of the doings of the Warrington Board of Guardians, at the impotent attempts to stifle debate and empty sneers of several members. It is not necessaty to agree with every- thing the rev, gentleman from Burtonwocd says and does, but most people admire anyone whose aims are honourable and straightforward, and I hold from the reports as printed in your valuable paper that this is the sole reason of Mr. Mitchell's attempt to get the work of the Board done in on open and straightforward manner. The public impression of the conduct of numbers of the members is that they are desirous of stifling open discussion. It is also surprising to see the absence from the Board meetings of one or two of its members. One can only draw conclu- sions : is it because it saves them them the necessity of publicly declaring themselves either one way or the other? If E 0 would it not be more honourable to retire? It is no use going up and down the country preaching temperance and soberiety to the Churches, Chapels, and P.S A.'s if men are not prepared to stand by these principles el e vhere. lam in entire agree- ment with you your corresp tneent; the inconsistencies are very glaring. Fancy 1,2G0 gill bottles of beer, besides 210 bottles of mineral waters. I would not begrudge the inmates their pleasure, but sorely a little of this genercsity might well have been extended to the poor eutside. What is 2.. or s granted to people outside, towards the amount of Ts. which was required for each of the inmates? I am pleased to add my testimony to the heroic efforts of the Vicar and one or two others to improve matteis.— I am, yours nly,</p>
        <p>H. A. J.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the columns of your bright and interesting issue of the :?9th ult. appears a letter signed I,y Onlooker" complaining of the provision of a few bottles of beer and mineral waters as an addition to the Christmas Fare for he inmates of the Workhouse. There always will be (such is human nature) those who are unable to share or appreciate the taste of others. There are those who severely condemn Him, -tvhose birth we commemorate at Christmas time, for countenancing the provision of wine at the marriage feast of Cana. Therefore, one can understand the type of mind that objects to the addition of a g'ass of beer to the paupers' Christmas dinner. But " Onlooker " even complains of the provision of mineral waters. Oh Shade of Scrooge ! Oh Shade of I Bumble ! Mineral waters for Paupers I In Merry England at Christmastide? Horror ! ! Wishing you, Sir, n happy New Year, and to " Onlooker" a larger heart and a broader view.—l am, yours truly, 2nd January, 1912.</p>
        <p>G. E. HOLME,</p>
        <p>.... -70,111W60010.00910011.4.119107 • _ ' ^ - /, 145 ' (4 til bi1...1:41fi Lc rte to the , • t t'EDITOR* ~„1.,2 The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>In Old Journalist and Women's Suffrage.</head>
        <p>In Old Journalist and Women's Suffrage.</p>
        <p>Sir,—" Old Journalist" confesses that he does tot know enottgh about Women's Suffrage to cross swords with anyone who does. Isn't it a pity in that case to write about it ? " Old Journalist " raises some points, I might say truisms, which he considers damaging to Women's Suffrage. One is that women are women and men are men, and the other is that the home coti- atitutes the urit of society. I lave yet to come across the person, Suffragist or Anti- Suffragist, who denies these facts. The more you argue that men are different from women, the more apparent does it become that it is impossible for men to legislate fa'rly for women. The vote is the matter by which expert opinion on every subject ought to reach the House of Commons. To deny any class a vote is to neglect the expert cpinioa that that class alone can offer. To deny the vote to aft men is to neglect the expert opinion of a sex. This neglect is all the graver seeing that this' sex is the expert authority not only on matters that concern women, but on matters that concern children, and that concern the home, which "Old Journalist," I repeat, declares to be the unit of society. Lady Frances Balfour tells 113 the story of an M.P. who at an election time gave orders to Ms secretary to put all letters coming from men into one waste paper basket and attend to them, all coming i from women into another and burn them. That s how women's interests are treated. Seeing that representative government is representative govern- ment, and pretends to be nothing more, and that a Parliamentary cardidate has all he can do to attend to his orders at election time, how can he be expected to attend to non-voters' affairs? • Are toenevolent despotic ideas to be combined with democratic ones ? When a nation tries to progress on the expert knowledge of one half of the race only, it i., so to speak, progressing on cue leg. A certain skill may be acquired in hopping, hut this will never be the same as a ran. And what is perhaps more important one leg can nexer he so steady as two.—Yours, etc., L. F. WARING.</p>
        <p>I atchford Hcme, ITal ringtcn, Jatuary Ist, 1012.</p>
        <p>Does Efficiency Benefit the Workers? Sir,—" Labor Omnia Vincit " in his last letter, instead of answering my arguments, takes refuge in dogmatic assertions. He tells us in reference to the point at issue " Mr. Parker has failed to prove his point, and failed miserably." He speaks cf my argument about productive capacity of the workers as only piffle." I have eften noticed when a disputant feels the foundation of his argument rocking he takes refuge in dogmatic statements, and calls his opponent's arguments " piffle" or the like. Surely L.O.V. does not imagine that this sort of writing will carry any weight with thoughtful readers. I would be justified in declining to continue corresponding on such lines. But I recognize that, however prone L.O.V. may be to think he proves his contention by its assertion and reassertion, he is thoroughly in earnest, and this trust = be his excuse both for his dogmatism and discourtesy. I have also to rememl er that if L.O.V. cannot, or will not, reason the matter cut, there are a number of readers who may be interested in considering the ' effect of efficiency upin the well b. jug of the workers. 3 Let the reader go back say thirty or forty years and compare the ccst of the articles in daily use in which efficiency in produttion has had full scope, and let him -decide for himself whether cr not efficiency has bene- .!fted the masses. It wilt then matter little whither -Mr. Parker has, or has not, " failed miserably " in proving his point, and whether his arguments are • piffle" or sound tense. The cheapening (I mean in the sense of real value) of the products of manufac- turers goes on continuously, but is en gradual that we scarcely realize it. It is also obscured by changes .r in the form and utility of the article made, hence it is • difficult to measure the reduction as we can in such simple articles as wheat and sugar. Still the reduc- tion must be manifest to those who can recall the values of forty years ago. Think of the things in daily ,use in the home. Begin at the child, think of the cradle he is rocked in, the perambulator he rides in, the clothes he wears, the rattle he shakes, and the feeding bottle his chubby bands clasps. Look round the home and consider the money values now and forty years ago. The pictures on the wall, the crockery on the dresser. the glass and cutlery in the cupboard, the cookirg vessels by the hearth, what covers the floor and papers the well, the price of the bed clothes and the wearing clothes, hats, rubbers and umbrellas. Ccmpare the sugar that sweetens puddings .and teas, and the jams that dirty little mouths and s. please little palates. Recall costs of old means of transit, and compare with the cycle ar d the tramcar. The cnly articles which have not come down in price are those which from their very nature (such for example as the bides mentioned) efficiency can very little effect. In Mr. Sauerbcck's figures showing the ; average value of his fifty-four principal articles of use , the price between 1870 and 1910 goes down from 100 to 78, a 22 per cent reduction. That is, what forty years ago we had to give 100 s. to procure we now can get for 7Ss., this leaves 225. to be set on new wants calling for additional labour to satisfy them. But these fifty-four articles include a considerable number of articles of raw material little effected by efficiency. It is in the transformation of raw material . to finished articles that efaciency has most room. The reduction in these in the last forty years cannot well be less than 40 per cent ; in many manufactured - articles it is over 100 per cent. And, yet L.O.V. • contends at the masses have not reaped the benefit of efficie ! Whatever can he mean ? He tells us that he is still prepared to prove</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>MR. JOHN DRURY. (Aged 87.) The oldest guest at the dinner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <p>respected president of the committee—Alderman James Smethurst—was, of course, in command and he had splendid assistance from other members of the committee, which is constituted as follows :—Vice- presidents, Alderman W J Forshaw, Councillor W Rogers and Mr Richard Shackleton ; hon. treasurer, Major AF G &amp;tier ; hon. secretary, Mr Thomas A Sutton ; Messrs Charles E Bunch, Harry Hope, Thomas Atherton, Thomas Locker, William Webster, Harry Maddock, William Murray, Councillor Steel, Frederick Taylor, C.,uncillor Archer and Harold Houghton. Amongst others who attended were :—Sir Gilbert Greensll, C V. 0., Bart., and Mr Harold Smith, M.P. Whilst the meal was in progress, the Hippodrome orchestra, under the leadership of Mr E A Widdop, played chbice selections which were appreciatively received. After dinner, when the guests had settled down to a whiff of the fragrant weed, there was a little speech-making Alderman SMETHURST, who presided, wished all present a happy" and prosperous new year. They must all feel thankful that they had been spared to attend another treat. Twelve months ago he did not expect that he would ever be able t 3 come amongst them again, owing to his serious illness, but '''he thanked God that he was able to join their happy gathering once more. (Applause) The doctor had advised him to retire from that and other committees, but he felt that be ought not to fail to put in an appearance at the old people's treat. Referring to the history of the treat, he said they could net find the first minute beok but in 1868 —which was the farthest they could go back dinner was provided for old men and women to the number of 227. That day they were entertaining no fewer than 418 old men. (Applause.) That was his thirty-third year as a member of the committee, and during the whole of that time he had only missed one treat and that was last year's. There was only one gentleman living who was a member of the canmittee when he (Alderman Smethurt) was appointed, and that was Mr. William Dooley who now lived at Blackpcol— away from the smoke. (Laughter). A show of hands was taken and disclosed the fact that five Crimean veterans were present at the dinner and only five guests were under the age of sixty. The MATort, in proposing a vote of thanks to the cammittee, said the organisers of the treat had done a very good work which it was a real pleasure to witness. He believed, however, that it might be better to provide a less elaborate treat and to have it more frequently. (Applause) One day out of 365 was a very small percentage—(laughter)—and he could not he' p think- ing that, although he hoped none of them would be any the worse for their temperate indulgence at the festive board, it would be pleasanter if they had a simpler meal four or half-a-aozen times a year. That, of course, would entail a great deal of extra work. Tne fact that so many old people were eligible for the treat was a testimonial to the healthiness of Warring- ton. It showed that the sanitary improvements of the past had done a great deal. Taking the increase of popula'ion int) account there were probably,judging by the numbers attending that treat, twice as many people in this town who lived to the age of sixty, seventy, eighty or even approaching ninety as there were forty years ago. (Applause.) That was only a rough test but there was something in it and even if it were only approximate, it spoke eDquently for the sanitary progress of the town In spite of the smoke which some of their friends railed' against—and he had been very much slated for defending the town and its smoke—the presence of so many old maple and their fine appearance was an el-quent testimony to the healthiness of Warring- ton. Last year they were all feeling regret and anxiety at the absence of their president, but they were able to compensate for that sorrow that day by showing their pleasure and satisfaction at seeing him again amongst them, looking so well. (Applause). 'Alderman Smethurst's heart was in that work. He (the speaker) did not know of another man who went in for anything so enthusiastically. Whatever the Alderman undertook he put his heart and soul into, and he was not satisfied unless it proved a perfect success. The Mayor also voiced the general appre- ciation of the work of the indefatigable secretary, the treasurer and the other members of the committee. (Applause). Mr. Jolts Dnurtv, the oldest guest (who was intro- duced by the Mayor as a townsman for whom he had the greatest respect) seconded. He said it gave him great pleasure to have the privilege, tit his advanced age, to say a word in praise of the Mayor and Alder- man Smethurst whom he had long admired for their worth, energy and stability in working for the welfare of Warrington. The treat that day was the best spread be had ever partaken of in Warrington. Mr. Drury also paid a tribute to the musicians who had so beautifully entertained the guests, and to the ladies who had waited upon them. Mr. HAROLD SNIITII, M.P., in supporting, offered a word of congratulation to Mr. Drury, and wished him and all the guests long life and prosperity. It was, he continued, a wonderful thing—and something of which Warrington might well be proud—that a committee was able, with such dispatch, to make arrangements for the entertainment of so many old people. Moreover, it was particularly gratifying that so many lead- ing citizens had been prepared for years past to make sacrifices in order to bring one bright ray of light into lives which were often dull and squalid. Speaking as one who had the frood honour to represent the borough in the House of Commons, he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <p>.•</p>
        <p>efficiency in production does cause unemployment and misery. If he simply means that efficiency sometimes ' causes temporary unemployment, and at times hard- ship through displacement, I have already admitted this, bui the major point which concerns us is, does; efficiency benefit the people as a whole? I have no quarrel viith the quotation L.O.V. intro- duces from " Riches and Poverty " about the boot trade. It is true efficiency in any trade may cause production to overtake demand, but the process is generally gradual, and the displacement of labour, say through a more efficient machine, is often more than balanced by the creation of increased &amp;mend conse- quent cn the lowering of the price through competi- tion. This is what has taken place in the boot industry to which the quotation refers. The increased efficiency has created an increased demand, and instead of less labour being employed a considerably greater number of workers are now engaged in the boot industry than for several decades. As a result of efficiency in this trade those engaged in it have secured a firmer hold on the hone market, ac,l our exports of boots have doubled during the last ten years. The value in 1900 as £1,479,115, in 1910 £3.030,312. And as a result of general efficiency the people are able to buy more boots than formerly. Thus the trade L.O.V. instances to prove his point that working people have 'good reason to dread efficiency affords a striking instance fits henefits to the workers. The "Manchester Guardian" Trades' Report for the year says of Leicester, " The boot and shr e trade is by far the largest and ' most important ir.dustry. It may be safely stated that it was r ever ino• e highly organized and pros- perous for alike employer and employed than in 1911. Of Northampton it reports " No other period has e me up to the last year in the matter of employment. The nut-of-work amount paid by the Trades Unions has been the lowest on record."</p>
        <p>In the development of this trade, especially in late years, increased effiCiercy has (coupled an important place, the Blake Sewer may be instanced as one which displaced lahour and at the same time led t) its increase. As a result of this efficiency we have these conditions, first, an increased direct wage to the workers engaged ; Second, a greater number employed and steadier employment ; third, a reduction in the price of boots to every worker and user. This last is the most important because through the reducticn the wage earner has an extra amount svailable to spend on come ether labour product requiring vvcrkets to produce. I have emphasized this all important point in every letter because through it the main benefits of efficiency come to the workers. L.O.V. pertiAently ignores this. Could he not be induced to leave out the irrelevant part which appears in every letter in varied form, but with the same refrain, I mean the part about the public ownership and control of capital' and industry being the sure and only cure all &amp;c. If he would use this letter space to deal with the points emphaslzed above we might hope to make some progress towards agreement. I am prepared to discuss the claims of Socialism in its .proper place —have dote so before in your journ d—hut am not called upon to debate it as a side issue on the practical question of whether efficiency Lecefits the workers in the conditions under which they have to live here and now. This point and this only will I here discuss ; but L.O.V. must please conduct the correspondence in a manner worthy cf himself and the "Examiner." CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketb, January 2nd, 1012.</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, January 6, 1912</p>
        <p>Ymime ?be urnishing Problem -Solved.-</p>
        <p>could only say that none of the events of the year gave him greater pleasure than his,visit to the old folk's treat and of all the charitable objects which were promoted in Warrington, none was better than that institution which sought to give one real good day to those who- had fallen by the wayside or who, unfortuuately, were not able to provide .for themselves the good things of life. It would be a bad day for Warrington if ever it lost the services of the gentlemen who had' worked on behalf of that cause. It was essential that the Parliamentary representative of a great manufacturing district should be reminded as often as possible that there were in his constituency, many old men and women who were unable to defend them- selves or to resist attacks upon them and who were unable to make those demands for the alleviation of their lot in life which assuredly they all desired. There was in every town a great deserving class of poor to whom not only the local authorities, but the legislators at St. Stephen's had a distinct duty. (Applause). The vote of thanks was put to the meeting and passed with great enthusiasm, three rousing cheers being given. Alderman FORSHAW respond ed. He congratulated the old men on their excellent appearance and expressed the hope that they would live for many years to come to that festive table. They looked almost as handsome as the assembly in the House of Corn moos. (Laughter and applause.) The committee could not provide that. treat without funds and they were greatly indebted to the subscribers, to whom he moved a vote of thanks. Councillor ROGERS seconded and the proposition was agreed to. The National Anthem having been sung, the com- pany dispersed. There was another happy gathering in the evening, when over 700 old ladies were entertained at tea. The senior guest was Mrs. Holt, of Kautsford.road, who is in her ninety-fourth ye tr and still looks remarkably well. A substantial tea, consisting of sandwiches, cakes, &amp;c , was provided. Afterwards Alderman SMETHURST delivered a short speech in which he referred to the progress of the treat from year to year and said it was astonishin4 how the committee were able to find funds to maintain it. The kindners and-generosity of the people of Warrington. were, however, never wanting when occasion arose. Until recent years it had been the practice of the committee to keep sufficient money in hand to enable them to provide a treat without asking anyone for a, single Fenny. Lately, however, their funds had got a little bit " below par," but they were trying to raise them to the old standard. They had got .ElO in hand, but he would never rest content until they g3t above £7O. (Applause). The MAYOR proposed and Sir GILBERT GREENTALL /mended a vote of thanks to the committee and offialals.</p>
        <p>Mr. HAROLD SMITIf, M.P., supported and, adopting, the idea that a woman is supposed to be as old as she. looks, said he never saw such a "jolly lot of girls " ira his life, as had assembled for that treat. (Applause). The vote was passed with cheers and musical honours. 1 A most interesting and well varied musical pro- gramme was then gone through, the committee having had the hearty co-operation of the managements of the Palace and Hippodrome and the Theatre Royal in arranging for the appearance of numerous artistes. 1 Margaret Ismay and girls dalighted the aged people with a dancing and singing not aad had a capital reception. Lawrence Bros, Scotch comedians and dancers, were great favourites and ware not allowed to retire until they had responded to an encore. Charles Mildare, whistler, was a host in himself, his unique performance affording much pleasure. The above- mentioned artistes were from the Hippodrome and gave I their services by kind permission of Mr. E. Turpin, on behalf of Mr. Frank Macnaghten. From the 'theatre I Royal there came George Young, a blind musician,who gave choice selection on the concertina ; Harry Val Housden, whose humorous Irish stories made the old ladies roar with laughter; Fred Wright, comedian, whose song about " Sally " was a decided hit; and Richard Walsh, comedian, who gave capital songs. The committee are indebted to Mr. C. E. Bradley (on behalf of Mr. Wilberforce Turner) for permission for the Theatre Royal " turns to be performed. In addition several well-known local artistes. acquitted themselves with great credit. Mr. J. H. Draper, tenor, sang "Thora " in finished style. Doris Welsby, child comedienne, did remarkably well for one so young. Carl Vernon, ventriloquist and illusionist, contributed a very clever and acceptable feature and Mr. W. W. Whittle, comedian, kept the audience in a state of hilarity with his song" The country curate" and2a capital sketch "A day trip to Douglas," introduc- ing the songs" Boss of the house " "My wife's gone to the country." The duties of accompanist were capably undertaken by Mr. Harold Bevan, who opened the second half of the programme with a bright selection. The concert had been arranged by Councillor W. Rogera and Mr. H. Maddock, who had the willing assistance of Mr. W. W. Whittle as stage manager. Befole leaving, each old lady received an orange and a packet of tea.</p>
