      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>MR. HAROLD SMITH AND REV. W. COOPER.</head>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH AND REV. W. COOPER.</p>
        <p>AN ARGUMENT ON TORY TYRANNY.</p>
        <p>[TIIE EDITOR, WARRINGTON " EXA3IINER Hill ClifTe, Nr. Warrington. Jan. sth, 1912. Dear Sir,— I shall be obliged if you can find space for the enclosed cotrespondence in your is,_ue of to-morrow.</p>
        <p>Yours faithfully, HAROLD SMITH. COPY. 4, Elm Court Temple, Dec. 14th. Sir,—lt has recently been brought to my notice that a speech of yours is reported in the " Exatriner " of November 11th, as follows: __</p>
        <p>"Even here he had fourd people who dare not attend the Church of their choice because of Tory tyranny." This charge is so serious and reflects such grave discredit upon the Unionist Party in Warrington, the good name of which is very dear to me, that I invite you to enable me to make full inquiries. I cannot, of course, believe that you wculd have made such a statement without full knowledge and I shall be grateful if you will forward to me particulars of a few specific instances into which I propose to iLquire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Yours faithfully, HAROLD SMITH.</head>
        <p>Yours faithfully, HAROLD SMITH.</p>
        <p>Rev. W. Coop, r.</p>
        <p>18, Alpley-street, Warrington, December 16th, 1911. Dear Sir,—l am in receipt of your letter of the 14th ult. and beg to state that I was not aware the " Examiner" bad reported my remarks in nalnner given. My woids were : "Within five miles of where I stand, there are people who dare not attend the Church of their choice, because of Tory tyranny." As the events to whicn I referred did rot occur within your constituency I do not see that it can be a matter affecting your personal concern. Yours faithfully, (Signed) WILLIAM COOPER. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P.</p>
        <p>Walton Hall, Warriugt,m, December 22nd. Rev. W. Ccoper. Dear Sir,—l am obliged by your letter and note with pleasure that you have been inaccurately reported by the "Examiner." lam gratified that I have been able to thus afford you the opportunity of explaining that so far as Wallington is concerned your grave charges do not apply May I add that, although , these cases of "Tory tyranny" are not happily within the Borough, my services are still at your disposal and I will take the necessary steps to inquire into them if you care to roma: d the particulars. Ycurs faithfully, (Signed) lIA.ROLD SMITH. January sth. Warrington. Rev. W. Coorer. Dear Sir,--s I have not received the courtesy of an acknowledgement of the letter which I addressed to you two or three weeks ago I must assume that you do not find it convenient to give me the particulars I asked for.</p>
        <p>he 1</p>
        <p>I propose sending a copy of this correspondence to the local press in order that the public of Warrington may place their own comtruction on sour regrettable reticence. Yours faithfully, H AR 0 LD [Mr. Smith's virtuous indignation at a charge of " Tory tyranny ' is amusing, and we are sorry to have to disappoint both himself and his critic by vouching for the accuracy of our report on the occasion in question. The Rev. NV. Cooper said " I find it here," but we have no doubt that, he will also find Tory tyranny elsewhere within a radius of five miles. There is no scarcity.—ED. " Ex."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>NEW YEAR TREATS AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>NEW YEAR TREATS AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. P Leach, 1.P., the founder of the free break- fasts, bad this year extended the slops of his operations and on New Year's Dly over 2,400 children were fed at eleven centres—viz , Ragged Szhool,St Anne's, Sutton; Gerard's Bridge Mission, Sacred Heart Schools, Catholic Temperance Hall, Lord-street ; Park-road Wesley, St Austin's, Thatto Heath ; Salvation Army, Milk-street ; Salvation Army, Tbatto Heath ; Parr Stocks School, and Holy Trinity, Peasley Cross. The arrangements at each centre were in the bands of old and trusted workers in the movement, and the children thoroubly enjoyed the good things.—The Nfayor (Sir Joseph Beecham) again gave a New Year's Day dinner to the aged people of the borough. Dinner was served at one o'clock to 350 at the Engineer Hall, the menu being of a most substintial and appetising description. A capital entertainment followed, and tea, tobacco, were distributed at the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND ' (Aniseed, Senesa, Squill, Tolu, &amp;c.) For Coughs and Colds, Influenza, &amp;o.—. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>FAMOUS MISSIONARY'S RETURN.</head>
        <p>FAMOUS MISSIONARY'S RETURN.</p>
        <p>Dr Griffith John, of the London Missionary Society who has spent fifty-six years in China, has this week returned to England. Nearly the whole of his great work has been done in Hankow, and he is now in his 81st year. His health suffered considerably by his recent terrible experiences in Ifankow, and is so pre- carious that be will be unable on his return to see any- one excepting a few personal friends. Dr John is a native of Swansea, and the University of Wales is to confer on him the degree of Doctor of Divinity.</p>
        <p>BIRTH. EARP.—At Norton Cottage on the 3rd itst., the wife of Mr. Rowland A. Earp, 0f eon.</p>
        <p>• DEATH. PAGE—On Jannary 2nd, 1912, at the Isolation Hospital, Warrington, in her 33rd year, Jessie, the dear wife of H. Stuart Page, The Croft, Padgate. She gave her life for her children. IN MEMORIAM. In ever loving memory of my dear father, WILLIAM: OWEti, who fell asleep, December 31st, 1899, aged 71 years. "Gone but not forgotten."</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.—Printed and Published by JOHN WALKER &amp; CO. (Warrington), Ltd., at The Examiner General Printing Works, liewsey-street, and published by them at the same place, and also at 2, The rExamine Offices, Victoria-road, WIDNES. 3, Geoßge-street. ST. HELENS. 3, Market-street, EARLEsTowN., Church-street, Iturtco7l3. JANUARY 6 1912.</p>
        <p>MNIMMI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-01-06_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>NEW CHESHIRE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>NEW CHESHIRE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Photo Bipt!ca. MR T. J. RIDG WAY.</p>
        <p>Photo Ash, Blackpool. MR, SAMUEL DAVIES.</p>
        <p>The names of the following- gentlemen, among others, have, on the advice of the County Advisory Committee, been added to the Commission of the Peace for the County of Chester.</p>
        <p>Mr, THOMAS JOSEPH RIDGWAY, Wilders- moor, Lymm, solicitor, retired from practice, except as Registrar of the Warrington County Court Mr. Ridgway was at one time a member of the Warrington Town Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN COXON, Statham Lodge, Lymm, managing director of Messrs. S. Reynolds and Co., tanners, Latchford.</p>
        <p>Photo Ardent.</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN COXON.</p>
        <p>MR. H. HOLDEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. HARRY HOLDEN, The Cottage, Thelwall, director of Messrs. Holden and Brooke, Ltd., engineers and injector manufac- turers,_Manchester.</p>
        <p>Mr. 'SAMUEL DAVIES, Platt Farm, Heatley, Lymm, farmer, and hay and corn merchant, and a member of .the Lymm Urban District Council.</p>
        <p>Of the above, Jr. Davies is the only Liberal. Of the thirty-two new Cheshire magistrates appointed, 12 are Liberals, 19 Conservatives and 1 Labour—a political proportion which has been the subject of adverse comment in the county.</p>
