        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Visit of the Moody-Manners Opera Company.</p>
        <p>was in some respects a new reading in the sense that she threw into it an abandon so reckless and so corn- In the woild of grand opera the Moody-Manners plete. As Don Josa Mr. Wilson Pembroke played up t o combination has long stood for all that is best, and Miss Westvvood's '• Carmen " in quite a thorough-going the management of the Royal Court Theatre deserves way and at the conclusion of Acts 3 and 4it was the gratitude of local music lovers for arranging seen, when the curtain was raised for a recall, that another visit of the famous company. The repertoire be bad strung himself to a high pltch of feeling in for the week has consisted entirely of works which the portrayal of the part of the infuriated lover. Mr. may be classed as old favourites, and their presenta- Charles Moorhouse's " Escape:llo " was not quite so tion has been adequate in every respect. A large convincirg a presentment—one felt that woile he audience gave an enthusiastic welcome to the company tried apparently to throw himself into the part there on Monday afternoon, and listened with great interest was some feeling of restraint from which he was and discrimination to a really notable performance of unable to flee himself. The minor characters in Verdi's best known opera " n Trovatore : or the "Carmen "—all of them of importance—were exceed- Gipsy's Vengeance." It was a most promising open- ingly well done. The orchestra has throughout the week proved itself highly efficient, while in point of log of the week's engagement, for artistically the principals concerned achieved a distinct triumph, the number and variety of instruments represented, it and the app: eciation so loudly ruanifes'ed at has been quite adequate and complete. the close of each scene was very gratifying. On Thursday and Fliday evenings "The Daughter The Leonora of Miss Raymond° Amy deserves nothing of the Regiment" and " Tannhauser " were given, but the highest commendation. She made the part while to-night (Saturday) is reservel for a concluding live an held the audience spellb-uud by the dramatic performance of" The Bohemian Girl." intensity of her portrayal and the wonderful skill with KNIGHTHOOD FOR THE PROPRIETOR. which ehe used a vice of exceptionally good quality . and range. Mr. Aubrey Standing as Manrico fell tsheathmeonuenecreef knight- with ht. Great satisfaction was expressed in Warrington and Helens Monday h 't d that his St.th the King short of the ideal lover in that he is s rue chat stiff as an actor, a shortcoming that will doubtless be minimised M wconefileriredwa hoodajsuy ton Alderman Joseph Beecham, Mayor of St. with maturer experience. But he has a voice quiteP . . R I C 1 Helens and proprietor of the ya ()intTheatre, phenomenal in its extreme sweetness, pu•ity an, , . • flexibility, and the wonderful amplitude of its range. late Mr. _, arrington. Sir Joseph Beechnra is the son of the late Mr. Thomas Beecham, the founder of the now Moreover he uses it always with artistic effect and due restraint. That Mr. Standing is able to finish with world-f a mous firm of pill manufacturers, and when his ease father retired a few pears before his death, he ease on a full-toned C in the uproar part of the treble , . b •ti re which of the business, staff and to moderate its volume when necessary to a 'Decal!" . 'cue ,Prolm:, , c whtch P</p>
        <p>most lovely sotto roc', is to acknowledge him the possessor of no small asset irk the equipment of an operatic tenor. He aroused a tumult of applause by his stirring interpretation of " Strike &amp;own that dread pyre," a repetition of which was insistently demanded. The Miserere Scene was, of courv, one of the gems of tire opera, in which Leoao• a and Mande° won further distincion. The Azncena of Miss Helen Culver. was a remarkable charac teri z ation au awesome combination of the wierd and pathetic. It would have been difficult to conceive anything more realistic. No one could fail to be impressed by the intensity of feeling and expression which she infused into her singing of "Fierce flames are raging." Mr. Graham Marr must be credited with a brilliant success as C,unt de Luna. Voctily and histrionically his presentment, of this trying part left nothing to be desired, and his robust perfect'y controlled baritnne voice found full scope in "The tempest of the heart " which was excellently given. The other principals, who did well in: their respective roles, were Miss Rigby, Mr. Clegg, and Mr. R. Griffin. The chorus— a nicely balanced, well-drilled combination - sang with freshness and confidence; while the orchestra did its work in masterly style. Herr ltichlrd , Eckhold, the conductor, directed his forces w:th a tact and skill which contributed in no measured degree to the all-round success of the production. In the evening Benedict's Lily of Killarney " was enacted before an audience equally as large and equally as enthusiastic as that which assemb'el for the matinee performance, the principal characters of Myles-na-Coppaleen, Hardress Cregin, Danny Mann, Mrs. Cregan, Ann Chute and Eily O'Connor being impersonated by 11r Albert Bowyer, Mr Wilson Pembroke Mr. Charles Moorhouse, Miss Lily Moody, Miss W. Burns and Miss Florence Morden respectively. With so strong a cast the opera ran its length smoothly aud with a vivacity and brightness which is so peculiarly characteristic of the Irish people who move in the play, a striking feature of the perform- ance being the magnificent costumes and the ensemble and scenic effects. On Tuesday evening G,unod's masterpiece "Faust" was portrayed and it is curious to note that so popular a work attracted so small an audience. As "Faust " Mr. Albert Bo vyer was not quite so happily placed as he was as "Myles' in the Lily of Killarney for though he proved himself in both works to be the possessor of many of the requirements of a first rate act-o• the peculiarly hard quality of his voice was much more noticeable in its lack of sympathetic expression in Gounod's opera. Miss Morden was on the other hand more charming than ever as "Marguerite," a character which in its unsophisticated simplicity is not at all unlike that of" Eily O'COnnor " in Benedict's work. Curiously Miss Morden appeared also as " Micaela " in "Carmen " on Wednesday evening, and further confirmed our good opinion of her capabilities as both actress and vocalist— capabilities that have for their chief characteristics a natural grace, ar.d a charming itairete. The Mephistopheles of Mr. W. Anderson was a powerful impersonation, a fine presence, a splendid voice and most appropriately sardonic manner combining in Mr. Anderson's personality to produce here the happiest results. Nor was the "Valentine" of Mr. Graham Marr less conspicuous in dignity and manly bearing. Seldom have we seen this character more finely portrayed. The " Siebel" of Miss Bessie Weir and the "Martha " of Miss Cartwright were'rell</p>
        <p>ramifications of which extend all over the world. In the domain of art, the new kn'ght has taken a prominent part. Some twelve years ago when the St. Helens Theatre was burned down. Sir Joseph, along with the late Sir David Gamble, the lite Colonel 11 Pilkington and Alderman FII Dixon Nut'all bought the ruin, and the Theatre was rebuilt, the datermina- ti m being to supply the town witi first-rate drama, anfl to culttvate a taste for the best things in music. He became more actively a•socia•ed with theatrical management a few years ago, when he purchased the Royal Court Theatre, Warrington, which has since br en successfully carried on under the management of Mr. A. M. Loader. More recently he took over the Aldwych Theatre, in London, and there is now being produced there with conspicuous success, a fairy play for the children, entitied " The Golden Land of Faily Tales," while it is intended later to produce a series of operas wni-th will ba elm-Note:l by -Mr. Thomas Beecham. Sir Joseph hopes to make the Aldwych a great musical centre for London. Sir Joseph is himself a talented musici in, his favourite instrument being the organ. At his Huyton and Hampstead residences are rn,gnificent organs designed by Mr. S. 'N. Pilling. During the last thirty-five )ears, Sir Joseph has heard most of the greatest singers of the wor!d. As is now well-known, his s n, Mr Thomas Beecham, has stepped into the front rank of conductors, and he has had the active support of Sir Joseph in some of the most important operatic enter- wises of the present day. Sir Joseph Beecham has one of the finest and most valuable collections in the country of the works of the old masters, as well as of modern painters, at his Hampttead residence. He has what is probably the best collection in London of water colour drawings by Turner, and many nitable works by Morland, Con- stable, David Cox, and other eminent artists are to be found on the walls. Three of Sir Joseph's pictures have just been returned from Rome, he having lent them to the Inter national Fine Art Exhibition which was held in that city.</p>
        <p>Great Sankey. SANKEY GUILD PARTY.—The annual guild party which is generously given by the Rev. J. R. Jones to the members of the Sankey Women's Guild took place on Thursday evening at Sankey Schools, and like others which have preceded it, was a great success. A first class tea was served at six o'clock to about 70 members. Other ladies who were invited were the Misses Tinsley, Miss Bolton, Mrs. T. Owen and Miss Jeans. The ladies who were in cha-ge of the tables were the Misses Hatton, Mesdames Gorse and Gilbert. The schoolroom presentad a pretty appearance with decora- tions which had been carried out by Miss Gorse and Mrs. F. Davies. After tea the Vicar presided at the concert which was interspersed with games and competitions. In a short speech the Vicar wished the members of the guild a happy New Year and gave encouraging advice to the members. The programme was in the bands of Mrs. Gilbert (singing), and Mrs. F. Davies (games.) Mrs. C. H. Johnstone accompanied. _</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Band of Hope. In connection with the above, an operetta entitled " A merry party," was given by the scholars of the Wrrking Men's Mission, Bank Quay, on Thursday, before a !erg:: and appreciative audience. The numerous choruses and dances were per:ormed most creditably.</p>
