        <p>THE OLD FOLK. ANNUAL TREAT IN THE PARR HALL. "What a striking transformation as compared with Tuesday evening! ' was ore of the first impressions of a visitor to the Parr Hall at noon on Thursday. But a few hours before, as it seemed, the ball resounded with childish merriment and boys and girls were tripping lightly over the spacious floor, at Warrington's annual juvenile ball. Now it was the turn of the old folk. Around the festive board were gathered many toil-worn veterans of industry and a few of that rapidly diminishing band of heroes for whom the title," veteran" carries the proud recollec- tion of service to their country on the famous battle fields which have long since pasted into history. They had gathered for the annual treat with which, thanks to the thoughtful generosity and willing activity of their fellow townsmen, the New Year is ushered in. And an interesting assembly it was ! Some of the men who had passed life's allotted span appeared hale and hearty, and were still able to walk with firm step and carry themselves well. Others, with whom life's struggle had gone mire hardly, showed signs of feebleness and decrepitude. But, on this occasion at any rate, all looked happy in anticipation of a dinner "fit for the King" as it has repeatedly been described by grateful partakers of the bounteous spread. There were 418 guests, the oldest being Mr. John Drury, of Algernon-street, who will be eighty-eight years of age in May next. Readers of the " Examiner " will recall his interesting reminiscences published in our columns some little time ago. Another prominent figure was Mr. Isaac Johnson, the oldest Wesleyan local preacher in this dit-trict. All the guests having taken their places at the long tables, the steaming joints were brought in and very soon each man was attacking with avidity his portion of the good old English fare. There was, as usual, a liberal supply of roast beef and pork, followed by plum pudding, with an allow- ance of beer or mineral waters. An army of willing helpers waited upon the diners and saw that their wants were expeditiously satisfied. The genial and</p>
